const DATA=[{"t":"scene","act":0,"scene":"I","loc":"Before an alehouse on a heath.","id":0},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hostess and Sly.","id":1},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["I'll pheeze you, in faith."],"id":2,"m":"I'll fix you, I swear."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["A pair of stocks, you rogue!"],"id":3,"m":"You'll end up in the stocks, you rascal!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Y'are a baggage; the Slys are no rogues; look in the chronicles: we","came in with Richard Conqueror. Therefore, paucas pallabris; let the","world slide. Sessa!"],"id":4,"m":"You're a slut. The Slys are no rascals: look in the history books, we came over with Richard the Conqueror. So, few words; let the world go by. Shut up!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["You will not pay for the glasses you have burst?"],"id":5,"m":"So you won't pay for the glasses you've broken?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["No, not a denier. Go by, Saint Jeronimy, go to thy cold bed and warm","thee."],"id":6,"m":"No, not a penny. Get lost, Saint Jeronimy: go to your cold bed and warm yourself up."},{"t":"sp","who":"HOSTESS","lines":["I know my remedy; I must go fetch the third-borough."],"id":7,"m":"I know what to do about this. I'll go and fetch the constable."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":8},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Third, or fourth, or fifth borough, I'll answer him by law. I'll not","budge an inch, boy: let him come, and kindly."],"id":9,"m":"Constable, or fourth, or fifth constable, I'll answer him in court. I won't budge an inch, boy. Let him come, and welcome."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lies down on the ground, and falls asleep.","id":10},{"t":"sd","text":"Horns winded. Enter a Lord from hunting, with Huntsmen and Servants.","id":11},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Huntsman, I charge thee, tender well my hounds;","Brach Merriman, the poor cur is emboss'd,","And couple Clowder with the deep-mouth'd brach.","Saw'st thou not, boy, how Silver made it good","At the hedge-corner, in the coldest fault?","I would not lose the dog for twenty pound."],"id":12,"m":"Huntsman, I order you to look after my hounds well. Give Merriman a rest, the poor dog is foaming with exhaustion, and leash Clowder with the deep-voiced bitch. Did you see, boy, how Silver picked up the trail at the corner of the hedge, where the scent was coldest? I wouldn't lose that dog for twenty pounds."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST HUNTSMAN","lines":["Why, Bellman is as good as he, my lord;","He cried upon it at the merest loss,","And twice today pick'd out the dullest scent;","Trust me, I take him for the better dog."],"id":13,"m":"Well, Bellman is as good as him, my lord. He bayed when the scent was completely lost, and twice today he found the faintest trail. Believe me, I think he's the better dog."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Thou art a fool: if Echo were as fleet,","I would esteem him worth a dozen such.","But sup them well, and look unto them all;","Tomorrow I intend to hunt again."],"id":14,"m":"You're a fool. If Echo were as fast, I'd value him at a dozen like Bellman. But feed them well and look after them all. I mean to hunt again tomorrow."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST HUNTSMAN","lines":["I will, my lord."],"id":15,"m":"I will, my lord."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["[Sees Sly] What's here? One dead, or drunk?","See, doth he breathe?"],"id":16,"m":"What's this? Is he dead, or drunk? See if he's breathing."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND HUNTSMAN","lines":["He breathes, my lord. Were he not warm'd with ale,","This were a bed but cold to sleep so soundly."],"id":17,"m":"He's breathing, my lord. If he weren't warmed up with ale, this would be a cold bed to sleep so soundly in."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["O monstrous beast! how like a swine he lies!","Grim death, how foul and loathsome is thine image!","Sirs, I will practise on this drunken man.","What think you, if he were convey'd to bed,","Wrapp'd in sweet clothes, rings put upon his fingers,","A most delicious banquet by his bed,","And brave attendants near him when he wakes,","Would not the beggar then forget himself?"],"id":18,"m":"What a disgusting beast! He lies there like a pig! Death, how ugly and loathsome your likeness is! Gentlemen, I'm going to play a trick on this drunk. What do you think: if he were carried to bed, wrapped in perfumed clothes, with rings put on his fingers, a delicious banquet by his bed and finely dressed servants around him when he wakes, wouldn't the beggar forget who he is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST HUNTSMAN","lines":["Believe me, lord, I think he cannot choose."],"id":19,"m":"Believe me, my lord, I don't think he could help it."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND HUNTSMAN","lines":["It would seem strange unto him when he wak'd."],"id":20,"m":"It would seem strange to him when he woke up."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Even as a flattering dream or worthless fancy.","Then take him up, and manage well the jest.","Carry him gently to my fairest chamber,","And hang it round with all my wanton pictures;","Balm his foul head in warm distilled waters,","And burn sweet wood to make the lodging sweet.","Procure me music ready when he wakes,","To make a dulcet and a heavenly sound;","And if he chance to speak, be ready straight,","And with a low submissive reverence","Say 'What is it your honour will command?'","Let one attend him with a silver basin","Full of rose-water and bestrew'd with flowers;","Another bear the ewer, the third a diaper,","And say 'Will't please your lordship cool your hands?'","Someone be ready with a costly suit,","And ask him what apparel he will wear;","Another tell him of his hounds and horse,","And that his lady mourns at his disease.","Persuade him that he hath been lunatic;","And, when he says he is—say that he dreams,","For he is nothing but a mighty lord.","This do, and do it kindly, gentle sirs;","It will be pastime passing excellent,","If it be husbanded with modesty."],"id":21,"m":"Just like a flattering dream or an empty fantasy. Then pick him up and carry the joke off well. Carry him gently to my finest bedroom and hang it all round with my sexiest pictures. Bathe his filthy head in warm perfumed water and burn sweet-smelling wood to scent the room. Have music ready for when he wakes, to make a sweet and heavenly sound. And if he happens to speak, be ready at once to bow low and humbly and say, \"What does your honour command?\" Let one of you wait on him with a silver basin full of rose-water scattered with flowers, another carry the jug, a third a towel, and say, \"Would your lordship like to cool your hands?\" Have someone ready with an expensive suit of clothes to ask him what he wants to wear. Have another tell him about his hounds and horse, and that his lady is grieving over his illness. Convince him he's been mad, and when he says he is himself, say he's dreaming, because he is nothing other than a mighty lord. Do this, and do it naturally, good sirs. It'll be wonderful entertainment if it's handled with restraint."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST HUNTSMAN","lines":["My lord, I warrant you we will play our part,","As he shall think by our true diligence,","He is no less than what we say he is."],"id":22,"m":"My lord, I promise you we'll play our part so well that he'll think, from our careful service, that he's exactly what we say he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Take him up gently, and to bed with him,","And each one to his office when he wakes."],"id":23,"m":"Pick him up gently and put him to bed, and each of you take up your job when he wakes."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sly is borne out. A trumpet sounds.","id":24},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","cont":1,"lines":["Sirrah, go see what trumpet 'tis that sounds."],"id":25,"m":"You there, go and see what that trumpet is."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":26},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","cont":1,"lines":["Belike some noble gentleman that means,","Travelling some journey, to repose him here."],"id":27,"m":"It's probably some nobleman on a journey who wants to rest here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Servant.","id":28},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","cont":1,"lines":["How now! who is it?"],"id":29,"m":"Well? Who is it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["An it please your honour, players","That offer service to your lordship."],"id":30,"m":"If it please your honour, some actors, who offer their services to your lordship."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Bid them come near."],"id":31,"m":"Tell them to come in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Players.","id":32},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","cont":1,"lines":["Now, fellows, you are welcome."],"id":33,"m":"Now, friends, you're welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLAYERS","lines":["We thank your honour."],"id":34,"m":"We thank your honour."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Do you intend to stay with me tonight?"],"id":35,"m":"Do you mean to stay with me tonight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PLAYER","lines":["So please your lordship to accept our duty."],"id":36,"m":"If your lordship will accept our services."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["With all my heart. This fellow I remember","Since once he play'd a farmer's eldest son;","'Twas where you woo'd the gentlewoman so well.","I have forgot your name; but, sure, that part","Was aptly fitted and naturally perform'd."],"id":37,"m":"With all my heart. I remember this man playing a farmer's eldest son once. It was the play where you wooed the lady so well. I've forgotten your name, but that part was well suited to you and naturally played."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLAYER","lines":["I think 'twas Soto that your honour means."],"id":38,"m":"I think your honour means Soto."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["'Tis very true; thou didst it excellent.","Well, you are come to me in happy time,","The rather for I have some sport in hand","Wherein your cunning can assist me much.","There is a lord will hear you play tonight;","But I am doubtful of your modesties,","Lest, over-eying of his odd behaviour,—","For yet his honour never heard a play,—","You break into some merry passion","And so offend him; for I tell you, sirs,","If you should smile, he grows impatient."],"id":39,"m":"That's right; you were excellent in it. Well, you've come at just the right moment, especially since I have some fun planned that your skill can help with a great deal. There is a lord who will hear you perform tonight. But I'm worried about your self-control. If you catch sight of his odd behaviour (because his honour has never seen a play), you might burst out laughing and offend him. I warn you, sirs, if you smile he'll lose his temper."},{"t":"sp","who":"PLAYER","lines":["Fear not, my lord; we can contain ourselves,","Were he the veriest antick in the world."],"id":40,"m":"Don't worry, my lord. We can control ourselves, even if he were the biggest clown in the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Go, sirrah, take them to the buttery,","And give them friendly welcome everyone:","Let them want nothing that my house affords."],"id":41,"m":"Go on, you, take them to the pantry and give them all a friendly welcome. Let them have anything my house can give."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit one with the Players.","id":42},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","cont":1,"lines":["Sirrah, go you to Barthol'mew my page,","And see him dress'd in all suits like a lady;","That done, conduct him to the drunkard's chamber,","And call him 'madam,' do him obeisance.","Tell him from me—as he will win my love,—","He bear himself with honourable action,","Such as he hath observ'd in noble ladies","Unto their lords, by them accomplished;","Such duty to the drunkard let him do,","With soft low tongue and lowly courtesy,","And say 'What is't your honour will command,","Wherein your lady and your humble wife","May show her duty and make known her love?'","And then with kind embracements, tempting kisses,","And with declining head into his bosom,","Bid him shed tears, as being overjoy'd","To see her noble lord restor'd to health,","Who for this seven years hath esteemed him","No better than a poor and loathsome beggar.","And if the boy have not a woman's gift","To rain a shower of commanded tears,","An onion will do well for such a shift,","Which, in a napkin being close convey'd,","Shall in despite enforce a watery eye.","See this dispatch'd with all the haste thou canst;","Anon I'll give thee more instructions."],"id":43,"m":"You there, go to my page Bartholomew and have him dressed in every detail like a lady. When that's done, take him to the drunkard's room, call him \"madam\" and bow to him. Tell him from me that, if he wants my favour, he must behave with dignity, the way he has seen noble ladies behave towards their husbands. Let him show the drunkard that kind of respect, with a soft, low voice and humble curtsies, and say, \"What does your honour command, so that your lady and humble wife can show her duty and her love?\" And then, with warm hugs and tempting kisses, laying his head on his chest, he must shed tears, as if overjoyed to see his noble husband restored to health, after seven years of thinking himself nothing but a poor, disgusting beggar. And if the boy can't cry on command the way women can, an onion will do the trick: wrapped up secretly in a handkerchief, it will force his eyes to water whether he likes it or not. See this done as fast as you can. I'll give you more instructions soon."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":44},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","cont":1,"lines":["I know the boy will well usurp the grace,","Voice, gait, and action of a gentlewoman;","I long to hear him call the drunkard husband;","And how my men will stay themselves from laughter","When they do homage to this simple peasant.","I'll in to counsel them; haply my presence","May well abate the over-merry spleen,","Which otherwise would grow into extremes."],"id":45,"m":"I know the boy will copy the grace, voice, walk and manner of a lady well. I can't wait to hear him call the drunkard \"husband\", and to see how my men keep from laughing when they bow to this simple peasant. I'll go in and advise them. Perhaps my being there will hold back their high spirits, which might otherwise get out of hand."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":46},{"t":"scene","act":0,"scene":"II","loc":"A bedchamber in the Lord's house.","id":47},{"t":"sd","text":"Sly is discovered in a rich nightgown, with Attendants: some with apparel, basin, ewer, and other appurtenances; and Lord, dressed like a servant.","id":48},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["For God's sake! a pot of small ale."],"id":49,"m":"For God's sake, a pot of weak ale!"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Will't please your lordship drink a cup of sack?"],"id":50,"m":"Would your lordship like a cup of sherry?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Will't please your honour taste of these conserves?"],"id":51,"m":"Would your honour like to taste these candied fruits?"},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["What raiment will your honour wear today?"],"id":52,"m":"What clothes will your honour wear today?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["I am Christophero Sly; call not me honour nor lordship. I ne'er drank","sack in my life; and if you give me any conserves, give me conserves of","beef. Ne'er ask me what raiment I'll wear, for I have no more doublets","than backs, no more stockings than legs, nor no more shoes than feet:","nay, sometime more feet than shoes, or such shoes as my toes look","through the over-leather."],"id":53,"m":"I'm Christopher Sly. Don't call me \"honour\" or \"lordship\". I've never drunk sherry in my life, and if you give me any preserves, give me preserved beef. Don't ever ask me what clothes I'll wear, because I've no more jackets than backs, no more stockings than legs, and no more shoes than feet. In fact, sometimes I've more feet than shoes, or shoes so worn that my toes stick out through the leather."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Heaven cease this idle humour in your honour!","O, that a mighty man of such descent,","Of such possessions, and so high esteem,","Should be infused with so foul a spirit!"],"id":54,"m":"Heaven put an end to this silly fancy of your honour's! Oh, that a mighty man of such noble family, with such property and such high reputation, should be possessed by so foul a spirit!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["What! would you make me mad? Am not I Christopher Sly, old Sly's son of","Burton-heath; by birth a pedlar, by education a cardmaker, by","transmutation a bear-herd, and now by present profession a tinker? Ask","Marian Hacket, the fat ale-wife of Wincot, if she know me not: if she","say I am not fourteen pence on the score for sheer ale, score me up for","the lyingest knave in Christendom. What! I am not bestraught. Here's—"],"id":55,"m":"What, are you trying to drive me mad? Aren't I Christopher Sly, old Sly's son from Barton-on-the-Heath, a pedlar by birth, a maker of wool-combs by training, then a bear-keeper by a change of trade, and now a tinker by profession? Ask Marian Hacket, the fat alewife of Wincot, if she doesn't know me. If she says I don't owe her fourteen pence on my tab for pure ale, put me down as the biggest liar in Christendom. What! I'm not out of my mind. Here's..."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["O! this it is that makes your lady mourn."],"id":56,"m":"Oh, this is what makes your lady grieve!"},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["O! this is it that makes your servants droop."],"id":57,"m":"Oh, this is what makes your servants so downcast!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Hence comes it that your kindred shuns your house,","As beaten hence by your strange lunacy.","O noble lord, bethink thee of thy birth,","Call home thy ancient thoughts from banishment,","And banish hence these abject lowly dreams.","Look how thy servants do attend on thee,","Each in his office ready at thy beck:","Wilt thou have music? Hark! Apollo plays,"],"id":58,"m":"This is why your relatives keep away from your house, as if driven off by your strange madness. Oh noble lord, think of your noble birth. Call your old thoughts home from exile, and banish these low, wretched dreams. Look how your servants wait on you, each ready to do his job at your nod. Would you like music? Listen! Apollo is playing,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Music.","id":59},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","cont":1,"lines":["And twenty caged nightingales do sing:","Or wilt thou sleep? We'll have thee to a couch","Softer and sweeter than the lustful bed","On purpose trimm'd up for Semiramis.","Say thou wilt walk: we will bestrew the ground:","Or wilt thou ride? Thy horses shall be trapp'd,","Their harness studded all with gold and pearl.","Dost thou love hawking? Thou hast hawks will soar","Above the morning lark: or wilt thou hunt?","Thy hounds shall make the welkin answer them","And fetch shrill echoes from the hollow earth."],"id":60,"m":"and twenty caged nightingales are singing. Or would you like to sleep? We'll take you to a couch softer and sweeter than the bed specially made up for the lustful queen Semiramis. Say you'd like to walk, and we'll strew the ground with flowers. Or would you like to ride? Your horses will be decked out with harness studded with gold and pearl. Do you love hawking? You have hawks that will fly higher than the morning lark. Or would you like to hunt? Your hounds will make the sky ring and draw shrill echoes from the hollow earth."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Say thou wilt course; thy greyhounds are as swift","As breathed stags; ay, fleeter than the roe."],"id":61,"m":"Say you'd like to go coursing: your greyhounds are as swift as stags in full stride, yes, faster than a deer."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Dost thou love pictures? We will fetch thee straight","Adonis painted by a running brook,","And Cytherea all in sedges hid,","Which seem to move and wanton with her breath","Even as the waving sedges play with wind."],"id":62,"m":"Do you love paintings? We'll bring you at once Adonis painted beside a running stream, and Venus hidden in the reeds, which seem to move and flirt with her breath, just as the waving reeds play in the wind."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["We'll show thee Io as she was a maid","And how she was beguiled and surpris'd,","As lively painted as the deed was done."],"id":63,"m":"We'll show you Io as she was when still a girl, and how she was tricked and taken by surprise, painted as vividly as if it were happening."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Or Daphne roaming through a thorny wood,","Scratching her legs, that one shall swear she bleeds","And at that sight shall sad Apollo weep,","So workmanly the blood and tears are drawn."],"id":64,"m":"Or Daphne running through a thorny wood, scratching her legs so that you'd swear she was bleeding, and at the sight sad Apollo weeps, so skilfully are the blood and tears painted."},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Thou art a lord, and nothing but a lord:","Thou hast a lady far more beautiful","Than any woman in this waning age."],"id":65,"m":"You're a lord, and nothing but a lord. You have a lady far more beautiful than any woman in this declining age."},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["And, till the tears that she hath shed for thee","Like envious floods o'er-run her lovely face,","She was the fairest creature in the world;","And yet she is inferior to none."],"id":66,"m":"And until the tears she has shed for you flowed like spiteful floods over her lovely face, she was the most beautiful creature in the world. And even now she's second to none."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Am I a lord? and have I such a lady?","Or do I dream? Or have I dream'd till now?","I do not sleep: I see, I hear, I speak;","I smell sweet savours, and I feel soft things:","Upon my life, I am a lord indeed;","And not a tinker, nor Christophero Sly.","Well, bring our lady hither to our sight;","And once again, a pot o' the smallest ale."],"id":67,"m":"Am I a lord? And do I have a lady like that? Or am I dreaming? Or have I been dreaming until now? I'm not asleep: I can see, hear and speak. I can smell sweet smells and feel soft things. Upon my life, I really am a lord, and not a tinker, or Christopher Sly. Well, bring our lady here for us to see. And once again, a pot of the weakest ale."},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","lines":["Will't please your mightiness to wash your hands?"],"id":68,"m":"Would your mightiness like to wash your hands?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Servants present a ewer, basin and napkin.","id":69},{"t":"sp","who":"SECOND SERVANT","cont":1,"lines":["O, how we joy to see your wit restor'd!","O, that once more you knew but what you are!","These fifteen years you have been in a dream,","Or, when you wak'd, so wak'd as if you slept."],"id":70,"m":"Oh, how glad we are to see your mind restored! Oh, if only you knew again who you are! You've been in a dream for fifteen years, or when you were awake, it was as if you were asleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["These fifteen years! by my fay, a goodly nap.","But did I never speak of all that time?"],"id":71,"m":"Fifteen years! By my faith, that's a good long nap. But did I never speak in all that time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["O! yes, my lord, but very idle words;","For though you lay here in this goodly chamber,","Yet would you say ye were beaten out of door,","And rail upon the hostess of the house,","And say you would present her at the leet,","Because she brought stone jugs and no seal'd quarts.","Sometimes you would call out for Cicely Hacket."],"id":72,"m":"Oh yes, my lord, but nothing but nonsense. Though you were lying here in this fine room, you'd say you'd been thrown out of doors, and you'd rant at the landlady of the inn and say you'd take her to the local court because she served drinks in stone jugs, not in proper measured quarts. Sometimes you'd call out for Cicely Hacket."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Ay, the woman's maid of the house."],"id":73,"m":"Yes, the landlady's maid at the inn."},{"t":"sp","who":"THIRD SERVANT","lines":["Why, sir, you know no house nor no such maid,","Nor no such men as you have reckon'd up,","As Stephen Sly, and old John Naps of Greece,","And Peter Turph, and Henry Pimpernell;","And twenty more such names and men as these,","Which never were, nor no man ever saw."],"id":74,"m":"Why, sir, you don't know any such inn or any such maid, or any of the men you've listed, like Stephen Sly, old John Naps of Greece, Peter Turph and Henry Pimpernell, and twenty more names and men like them, who never existed and nobody ever saw."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Now, Lord be thanked for my good amends!"],"id":75,"m":"Well, thank the Lord for my recovery!"},{"t":"sp","who":"ALL","lines":["Amen."],"id":76,"m":"Amen."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter the Page, as a lady, with Attendants.","id":77},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["I thank thee; thou shalt not lose by it."],"id":78,"m":"Thank you. You won't lose by it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["How fares my noble lord?"],"id":79,"m":"How is my noble lord?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Marry, I fare well; for here is cheer enough.","Where is my wife?"],"id":80,"m":"Well, I'm doing fine, because there's plenty of good food and drink here. Where's my wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Here, noble lord: what is thy will with her?"],"id":81,"m":"Here, noble lord. What do you want with her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Are you my wife, and will not call me husband?","My men should call me lord: I am your goodman."],"id":82,"m":"Are you my wife, and you won't call me husband? My servants should call me \"lord\". To you I'm your husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["My husband and my lord, my lord and husband;","I am your wife in all obedience."],"id":83,"m":"My husband and my lord, my lord and husband: I am your wife, and wholly obedient."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["I know it well. What must I call her?"],"id":84,"m":"I know that. What should I call her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Madam."],"id":85,"m":"Madam."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Alice madam, or Joan madam?"],"id":86,"m":"Alice Madam, or Joan Madam?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LORD","lines":["Madam, and nothing else; so lords call ladies."],"id":87,"m":"Madam, and nothing else. That's what lords call ladies."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Madam wife, they say that I have dream'd","And slept above some fifteen year or more."],"id":88,"m":"Madam wife, they say I've been dreaming and asleep for fifteen years or more."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Ay, and the time seems thirty unto me,","Being all this time abandon'd from your bed."],"id":89,"m":"Yes, and it seems like thirty to me, shut out from your bed all this time."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["'Tis much. Servants, leave me and her alone.","Madam, undress you, and come now to bed."],"id":90,"m":"That's a long time. Servants, leave me alone with her. Madam, get undressed and come to bed now."},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["Thrice noble lord, let me entreat of you","To pardon me yet for a night or two;","Or, if not so, until the sun be set:","For your physicians have expressly charg'd,","In peril to incur your former malady,","That I should yet absent me from your bed:","I hope this reason stands for my excuse."],"id":91,"m":"Most noble lord, let me beg you to excuse me for another night or two, or if not that, until sunset. Your doctors have given strict orders that I should keep away from your bed for now, or you risk falling back into your old illness. I hope this reason will excuse me."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Ay, it stands so that I may hardly tarry so long; but I would be loath","to fall into my dreams again: I will therefore tarry in despite of the","flesh and the blood."],"id":92,"m":"Yes, well, it's standing up so that I can hardly wait that long. But I'd hate to fall back into my dreams. So I'll wait, in spite of my flesh and blood."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Messenger.","id":93},{"t":"sp","who":"MESSENGER","lines":["Your honour's players, hearing your amendment,","Are come to play a pleasant comedy;","For so your doctors hold it very meet,","Seeing too much sadness hath congeal'd your blood,","And melancholy is the nurse of frenzy:","Therefore they thought it good you hear a play,","And frame your mind to mirth and merriment,","Which bars a thousand harms and lengthens life."],"id":94,"m":"Your honour's actors, hearing that you've recovered, have come to perform a pleasant comedy. Your doctors think it a very good idea, since too much sadness has thickened your blood, and melancholy feeds madness. So they thought it best that you should watch a play and turn your mind to mirth and fun, which prevents a thousand harms and lengthens life."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Marry, I will; let them play it. Is not a commonty a Christmas gambold","or a tumbling-trick?"],"id":95,"m":"Well, I will. Let them perform it. Isn't a \"commonty\" a Christmas romp or an acrobatic trick?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["No, my good lord; it is more pleasing stuff."],"id":96,"m":"No, my good lord. It's more pleasing stuff."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["What! household stuff?"],"id":97,"m":"What! Household stuff?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["It is a kind of history."],"id":98,"m":"It's a kind of story."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Well, we'll see't. Come, madam wife, sit by my side and let the world","slip: we shall ne'er be younger."],"id":99,"m":"Well, we'll see it. Come, madam wife, sit by my side and let the world go by. We'll never be younger."},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"I","loc":"Padua. A public place.","id":100},{"t":"sd","text":"Flourish. Enter Lucentio and Tranio.","id":101},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Tranio, since for the great desire I had","To see fair Padua, nursery of arts,","I am arriv'd for fruitful Lombardy,","The pleasant garden of great Italy,","And by my father's love and leave am arm'd","With his good will and thy good company,","My trusty servant well approv'd in all,","Here let us breathe, and haply institute","A course of learning and ingenious studies.","Pisa, renowned for grave citizens,","Gave me my being and my father first,","A merchant of great traffic through the world,","Vincentio, come of the Bentivolii.","Vincentio's son, brought up in Florence,","It shall become to serve all hopes conceiv'd,","To deck his fortune with his virtuous deeds:","And therefore, Tranio, for the time I study,","Virtue and that part of philosophy","Will I apply that treats of happiness","By virtue specially to be achiev'd.","Tell me thy mind; for I have Pisa left","And am to Padua come as he that leaves","A shallow plash to plunge him in the deep,","And with satiety seeks to quench his thirst."],"id":102,"m":"Tranio, because I longed so much to see beautiful Padua, the nursery of the arts, I have arrived in fertile Lombardy, the pleasant garden of great Italy. My father's love and permission have equipped me with his goodwill and your good company, my trusty servant, proven in everything. So let's settle here and perhaps begin a course of learning and clever studies. Pisa, famous for its serious citizens, gave me my life, and gave life first to my father, Vincentio, a merchant who trades all over the world and comes of the Bentivolii family. It's right that Vincentio's son, brought up in Florence, should fulfil all the hopes people have of him and add good deeds to his good fortune. So, Tranio, while I'm studying, I'll apply myself to virtue and to the part of philosophy that deals with happiness achieved through virtue. Tell me what you think, because I have left Pisa and come to Padua like a man who leaves a shallow puddle to plunge into the deep sea, and seeks to drink his fill."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Mi perdonato, gentle master mine;","I am in all affected as yourself;","Glad that you thus continue your resolve","To suck the sweets of sweet philosophy.","Only, good master, while we do admire","This virtue and this moral discipline,","Let's be no stoics nor no stocks, I pray;","Or so devote to Aristotle's checks","As Ovid be an outcast quite abjur'd.","Balk logic with acquaintance that you have,","And practise rhetoric in your common talk;","Music and poesy use to quicken you;","The mathematics and the metaphysics,","Fall to them as you find your stomach serves you:","No profit grows where is no pleasure ta'en;","In brief, sir, study what you most affect."],"id":103,"m":"Pardon me, my good master. I feel exactly the same as you, and I'm glad you're keeping to your resolve to suck the sweets of sweet philosophy. Only, good master, while we admire this virtue and moral discipline, let's not be stoics, or wooden blocks, I beg you, or be so devoted to Aristotle's rules that we give up Ovid's poems of love entirely. Argue logic with the friends you have, and practise rhetoric in everyday conversation. Use music and poetry to liven you up. As for mathematics and metaphysics, tackle them when you have the appetite for them. You get nothing out of what you don't enjoy. In short, sir, study what you like best."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Gramercies, Tranio, well dost thou advise.","If, Biondello, thou wert come ashore,","We could at once put us in readiness,","And take a lodging fit to entertain","Such friends as time in Padua shall beget.","But stay awhile; what company is this?"],"id":104,"m":"Thank you, Tranio; that's good advice. If only Biondello had come ashore, we could get ready at once and take lodgings fit to entertain the friends we'll make in time here in Padua. But wait a moment: who are these people?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Master, some show to welcome us to town."],"id":105,"m":"Master, it's some show to welcome us to town."},{"t":"sd","text":"Lucentio and Tranio stand aside.","id":106},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Baptista, Katherina, Bianca, Gremio and Hortensio.","id":107},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Gentlemen, importune me no farther,","For how I firmly am resolv'd you know;","That is, not to bestow my youngest daughter","Before I have a husband for the elder.","If either of you both love Katherina,","Because I know you well and love you well,","Leave shall you have to court her at your pleasure."],"id":108,"m":"Gentlemen, stop pestering me. You know how firmly I've made up my mind: I won't let my youngest daughter marry before I have a husband for the elder one. If either of you loves Katherina, then, because I know you well and like you, you have my permission to court her as you please."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["To cart her rather: she's too rough for me.","There, there, Hortensio, will you any wife?"],"id":109,"m":"Cart her through the streets, more like: she's too rough for me. There you are, Hortensio: do you want a wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["[To Baptista] I pray you, sir, is it your will","To make a stale of me amongst these mates?"],"id":110,"m":"I ask you, sir, do you mean to make me a laughing stock among these low types?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Mates, maid! How mean you that? No mates for you,","Unless you were of gentler, milder mould."],"id":111,"m":"Low types, girl? What do you mean by that? There'll be no mates for you, unless you were made of gentler, milder stuff."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I' faith, sir, you shall never need to fear;","I wis it is not half way to her heart;","But if it were, doubt not her care should be","To comb your noddle with a three-legg'd stool,","And paint your face, and use you like a fool."],"id":112,"m":"Really, sir, you'll never need to worry. Marrying you isn't half way to her heart. But if it were, you can be sure she'd take care to comb your head with a three-legged stool, paint your face with blood and treat you like a fool."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["From all such devils, good Lord deliver us!"],"id":113,"m":"From all such devils, good Lord deliver us!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["And me, too, good Lord!"],"id":114,"m":"And me too, good Lord!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Husht, master! Here's some good pastime toward:","That wench is stark mad or wonderful froward."],"id":115,"m":"Hush, master! There's some good entertainment coming. That girl is either stark mad or amazingly stubborn."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["But in the other's silence do I see","Maid's mild behaviour and sobriety.","Peace, Tranio!"],"id":116,"m":"But in the other one's silence I see a young woman's mild behaviour and modesty. Quiet, Tranio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Well said, master; mum! and gaze your fill."],"id":117,"m":"Well said, master. Mum's the word, and gaze your fill."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Gentlemen, that I may soon make good","What I have said,—Bianca, get you in:","And let it not displease thee, good Bianca,","For I will love thee ne'er the less, my girl."],"id":118,"m":"Gentlemen, so that I can quickly carry out what I've said: Bianca, go inside. And don't be upset, good Bianca, because I won't love you any less, my girl."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["A pretty peat! it is best put finger in the eye, and she knew why."],"id":119,"m":"The pretty pet! She'd better poke her finger in her eye to make herself cry, if only she could think of a reason."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Sister, content you in my discontent.","Sir, to your pleasure humbly I subscribe:","My books and instruments shall be my company,","On them to look, and practise by myself."],"id":120,"m":"Sister, be happy that I'm unhappy. Sir, I humbly accept your wishes. My books and instruments will be my company; I'll study them and practise by myself."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Hark, Tranio! thou mayst hear Minerva speak."],"id":121,"m":"Listen, Tranio! You can hear the goddess of wisdom speak."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Signior Baptista, will you be so strange?","Sorry am I that our good will effects","Bianca's grief."],"id":122,"m":"Signior Baptista, will you be so unfriendly? I'm sorry that our good intentions cause Bianca grief."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Why will you mew her up,","Signior Baptista, for this fiend of hell,","And make her bear the penance of her tongue?"],"id":123,"m":"Why will you shut her up like a caged hawk, Signior Baptista, because of this fiend from hell, and make her pay the price for her sister's tongue?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Gentlemen, content ye; I am resolv'd.","Go in, Bianca."],"id":124,"m":"Gentlemen, be satisfied. My mind is made up. Go in, Bianca."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bianca.","id":125},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","cont":1,"lines":["And for I know she taketh most delight","In music, instruments, and poetry,","Schoolmasters will I keep within my house","Fit to instruct her youth. If you, Hortensio,","Or, Signior Gremio, you, know any such,","Prefer them hither; for to cunning men","I will be very kind, and liberal","To mine own children in good bringing up;","And so, farewell. Katherina, you may stay;","For I have more to commune with Bianca."],"id":126,"m":"And because I know she takes most delight in music, instruments and poetry, I'll keep schoolmasters in my house fit to teach her while she's young. If you, Hortensio, or you, Signior Gremio, know any such men, recommend them to me. I'll be very generous to skilled men, and generous in giving my own children a good upbringing. And so, goodbye. Katherina, you can stay, because I have more to talk about with Bianca."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":127},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Why, and I trust I may go too, may I not? What! shall I be appointed","hours, as though, belike, I knew not what to take and what to leave?","Ha!"],"id":128,"m":"Why, I suppose I can go too, can't I? What, am I to be given a timetable, as though I didn't know what to take and what to leave? Ha!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":129},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["You may go to the devil's dam: your gifts are so good here's none will","hold you. Their love is not so great, Hortensio, but we may blow our","nails together, and fast it fairly out; our cake's dough on both sides.","Farewell: yet, for the love I bear my sweet Bianca, if I can by any","means light on a fit man to teach her that wherein she delights, I will","wish him to her father."],"id":130,"m":"You can go to the devil's mother. Your qualities are so good that nobody here wants to keep you. Their love isn't so great, Hortensio, that we can't blow on our nails and patiently wait it out. Our cake is still dough on both sides: neither of us has succeeded. Goodbye. Still, for the love I have for my sweet Bianca, if I can by any means find a suitable man to teach her the things she enjoys, I'll recommend him to her father."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["So will I, Signior Gremio: but a word, I pray. Though the nature of our","quarrel yet never brooked parle, know now, upon advice, it toucheth us","both,—that we may yet again have access to our fair mistress, and be","happy rivals in Bianca's love,—to labour and effect one thing","specially."],"id":131,"m":"So will I, Signior Gremio. But a word, please. Although our rivalry has never allowed us to talk till now, now that I've thought about it, it concerns us both, if we're ever to get back to our lovely mistress and be happy rivals for Bianca's love, to work together to achieve one thing above all."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["What's that, I pray?"],"id":132,"m":"What's that, please?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Marry, sir, to get a husband for her sister."],"id":133,"m":"Why, sir, to get a husband for her sister."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["A husband! a devil."],"id":134,"m":"A husband! A devil, you mean."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["I say, a husband."],"id":135,"m":"I said a husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["I say, a devil. Thinkest thou, Hortensio, though her father be very","rich, any man is so very a fool to be married to hell?"],"id":136,"m":"And I say a devil. Do you really think, Hortensio, that even though her father is very rich, any man is such a fool as to marry hell?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Tush, Gremio! Though it pass your patience and mine to endure her loud","alarums, why, man, there be good fellows in the world, and a man could","light on them, would take her with all faults, and money enough."],"id":137,"m":"Nonsense, Gremio! Though it's beyond your patience and mine to put up with her noisy outbursts, why, man, there are good fellows in the world, if you could only find them, who'd take her with all her faults, if there's enough money."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["I cannot tell; but I had as lief take her dowry with this condition: to","be whipp'd at the high cross every morning."],"id":138,"m":"I don't know. I'd as soon take her dowry on this condition: to be whipped at the market cross every morning."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Faith, as you say, there's small choice in rotten apples. But come;","since this bar in law makes us friends, it shall be so far forth","friendly maintained, till by helping Baptista's eldest daughter to a","husband, we set his youngest free for a husband, and then have to't","afresh. Sweet Bianca! Happy man be his dole! He that runs fastest gets","the ring. How say you, Signior Gremio?"],"id":139,"m":"Well, as you say, there's not much to choose between rotten apples. But come: since this obstacle makes us friends, let's stay friendly until, by finding Baptista's eldest daughter a husband, we free his youngest to have a husband, and then we'll go at it again. Sweet Bianca! Good luck to the winner! The fastest runner gets the prize. What do you say, Signior Gremio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["I am agreed; and would I had given him the best horse in Padua to begin","his wooing, that would thoroughly woo her, wed her, and bed her, and","rid the house of her. Come on."],"id":140,"m":"I agree. And I'd give the best horse in Padua to any man who'd start wooing her, who'd woo her thoroughly, marry her, bed her and get her out of the house. Come on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Gremio and Hortensio.","id":141},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["I pray, sir, tell me, is it possible","That love should of a sudden take such hold?"],"id":142,"m":"Tell me, sir, please, is it possible for love to take such a hold so suddenly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["O Tranio! till I found it to be true,","I never thought it possible or likely;","But see, while idly I stood looking on,","I found the effect of love in idleness;","And now in plainness do confess to thee,","That art to me as secret and as dear","As Anna to the Queen of Carthage was,","Tranio, I burn, I pine, I perish, Tranio,","If I achieve not this young modest girl.","Counsel me, Tranio, for I know thou canst:","Assist me, Tranio, for I know thou wilt."],"id":143,"m":"Oh, Tranio! Until I found it was true, I never thought it possible or likely. But look: while I stood idly watching, I felt love-in-idleness work on me. And now I'll confess plainly to you, who are as trusted and dear to me as Anna was to her sister Dido, Queen of Carthage: Tranio, I burn, I pine, I'll die, Tranio, if I don't win this modest young girl. Advise me, Tranio, because I know you can. Help me, Tranio, because I know you will."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Master, it is no time to chide you now;","Affection is not rated from the heart:","If love have touch'd you, nought remains but so:","Redime te captum quam queas minimo."],"id":144,"m":"Master, this is no time to scold you. You can't drive love out of the heart by telling it off. If love has struck you, there's only one thing to do: buy yourself free from captivity as cheaply as you can."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Gramercies, lad; go forward; this contents;","The rest will comfort, for thy counsel's sound."],"id":145,"m":"Thanks, lad. Go on: this is helpful. The rest will comfort me, because your advice is sound."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Master, you look'd so longly on the maid.","Perhaps you mark'd not what's the pith of all."],"id":146,"m":"Master, you gazed so long at the girl that perhaps you didn't notice the main point of it all."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["O, yes, I saw sweet beauty in her face,","Such as the daughter of Agenor had,","That made great Jove to humble him to her hand,","When with his knees he kiss'd the Cretan strand."],"id":147,"m":"Oh yes, I saw sweet beauty in her face, like the beauty of Europa, Agenor's daughter, which made mighty Jove humble himself to her when he knelt on the shore of Crete."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Saw you no more? mark'd you not how her sister","Began to scold and raise up such a storm","That mortal ears might hardly endure the din?"],"id":148,"m":"Didn't you see anything else? Didn't you notice how her sister began to scold and raised such a storm that human ears could hardly bear the noise?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Tranio, I saw her coral lips to move,","And with her breath she did perfume the air;","Sacred and sweet was all I saw in her."],"id":149,"m":"Tranio, I saw her coral lips move, and her breath perfumed the air. Everything I saw in her was holy and sweet."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Nay, then, 'tis time to stir him from his trance.","I pray, awake, sir: if you love the maid,","Bend thoughts and wits to achieve her. Thus it stands:","Her elder sister is so curst and shrewd,","That till the father rid his hands of her,","Master, your love must live a maid at home;","And therefore has he closely mew'd her up,","Because she will not be annoy'd with suitors."],"id":150,"m":"Well, then, it's time to wake him from his trance. Please wake up, sir. If you love the girl, turn your thoughts and wits to winning her. This is how things stand: her elder sister is so bad-tempered and shrewish that until her father gets her off his hands, master, your love must stay home unmarried. And so he's shut her away, so she won't be bothered by suitors."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Ah, Tranio, what a cruel father's he!","But art thou not advis'd he took some care","To get her cunning schoolmasters to instruct her?"],"id":151,"m":"Ah, Tranio, what a cruel father he is! But did you notice that he's taking care to find skilled schoolmasters to teach her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Ay, marry, am I, sir, and now 'tis plotted."],"id":152,"m":"Yes, indeed I did, sir, and now I've got a plan."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I have it, Tranio."],"id":153,"m":"I've got it, Tranio."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Master, for my hand,","Both our inventions meet and jump in one."],"id":154,"m":"Master, I'll bet both our ideas are exactly the same."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Tell me thine first."],"id":155,"m":"Tell me yours first."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["You will be schoolmaster,","And undertake the teaching of the maid:","That's your device."],"id":156,"m":"You'll be a schoolmaster and take on teaching the girl. That's your plan."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["It is: may it be done?"],"id":157,"m":"It is. Can it be done?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Not possible; for who shall bear your part","And be in Padua here Vincentio's son;","Keep house and ply his book, welcome his friends;","Visit his countrymen, and banquet them?"],"id":158,"m":"Impossible. Who'll play your part and be Vincentio's son here in Padua: keep house, study his books, welcome his friends, visit his fellow citizens and give them dinners?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Basta, content thee, for I have it full.","We have not yet been seen in any house,","Nor can we be distinguish'd by our faces","For man or master: then it follows thus:","Thou shalt be master, Tranio, in my stead,","Keep house and port and servants, as I should;","I will some other be; some Florentine,","Some Neapolitan, or meaner man of Pisa.","'Tis hatch'd, and shall be so: Tranio, at once","Uncase thee; take my colour'd hat and cloak.","When Biondello comes, he waits on thee;","But I will charm him first to keep his tongue."],"id":159,"m":"Enough, don't worry, because I've got it all worked out. We haven't been seen in any house yet, and nobody can tell from our faces which is servant and which is master. So it follows: you'll be the master, Tranio, in my place, keep house, style and servants as I should. I'll be someone else: a Florentine, a Neapolitan, or some poorer man from Pisa. It's hatched, and that's how it'll be. Tranio, undress at once and take my coloured hat and cloak. When Biondello comes, he'll wait on you. But first I'll make him promise to hold his tongue."},{"t":"sd","text":"They exchange habits.","id":160},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["So had you need.","In brief, sir, sith it your pleasure is,","And I am tied to be obedient;","For so your father charg'd me at our parting,","'Be serviceable to my son,' quoth he,","Although I think 'twas in another sense:","I am content to be Lucentio,","Because so well I love Lucentio."],"id":161,"m":"You'll need to. In short, sir, since that's what you want, and I'm bound to obey (because your father ordered me when we parted, \"Be useful to my son,\" he said, though I think he meant it in another sense), I'm happy to be Lucentio, because I love Lucentio so much."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Tranio, be so, because Lucentio loves;","And let me be a slave, to achieve that maid","Whose sudden sight hath thrall'd my wounded eye."],"id":162,"m":"Be him, Tranio, because Lucentio is in love. And let me be a slave, to win that girl whose sudden appearance has captured my wounded eye."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Biondello.","id":163},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes the rogue. Sirrah, where have you been?"],"id":164,"m":"Here comes the rascal. Where have you been, you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Where have I been? Nay, how now! where are you?","Master, has my fellow Tranio stol'n your clothes?","Or you stol'n his? or both? Pray, what's the news?"],"id":165,"m":"Where have I been? No, wait, what's this? Where are you? Master, has my fellow Tranio stolen your clothes? Or have you stolen his? Or both? Please, what's going on?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Sirrah, come hither: 'tis no time to jest,","And therefore frame your manners to the time.","Your fellow Tranio here, to save my life,","Puts my apparel and my count'nance on,","And I for my escape have put on his;","For in a quarrel since I came ashore","I kill'd a man, and fear I was descried.","Wait you on him, I charge you, as becomes,","While I make way from hence to save my life.","You understand me?"],"id":166,"m":"Come here, you. This is no time for jokes, so behave in a way that suits the time. Your fellow Tranio here, to save my life, is putting on my clothes and my appearance, and I've put on his so I can escape. Because in a quarrel since I came ashore, I killed a man, and I'm afraid I was seen. I order you to wait on him properly while I get away from here to save my life. Do you understand me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["I, sir! Ne'er a whit."],"id":167,"m":"Me, sir? Not a bit."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["And not a jot of Tranio in your mouth:","Tranio is changed to Lucentio."],"id":168,"m":"And not a word of Tranio from your mouth. Tranio has turned into Lucentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["The better for him: would I were so too!"],"id":169,"m":"Lucky him! I wish I could too!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["So could I, faith, boy, to have the next wish after,","That Lucentio indeed had Baptista's youngest daughter.","But, sirrah, not for my sake but your master's, I advise","You use your manners discreetly in all kind of companies:","When I am alone, why, then I am Tranio;","But in all places else your master, Lucentio."],"id":170,"m":"So do I, believe me, boy, as long as my next wish came true: that Lucentio really did get Baptista's youngest daughter. But, you, not for my sake but for your master's, I advise you to be careful how you behave in every kind of company. When I'm alone, why then I'm Tranio. But everywhere else I'm your master, Lucentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Tranio, let's go.","One thing more rests, that thyself execute,","To make one among these wooers: if thou ask me why,","Sufficeth my reasons are both good and weighty."],"id":171,"m":"Tranio, let's go. There's one more thing for you to do: to become one of these suitors. If you ask me why, it's enough that my reasons are good and serious."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":172},{"t":"sd","text":"The Presenters above speak.","id":173},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["My lord, you nod; you do not mind the play."],"id":174,"m":"My lord, you're nodding off. You're not watching the play."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["Yes, by Saint Anne, I do. A good matter, surely: comes there any more","of it?"],"id":175,"m":"Yes I am, by Saint Anne. It's good stuff, certainly. Is there any more of it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PAGE","lines":["My lord, 'tis but begun."],"id":176,"m":"My lord, it's only just begun."},{"t":"sp","who":"SLY","lines":["'Tis a very excellent piece of work, madam lady: would 'twere done!"],"id":177,"m":"It's a really excellent piece of work, madam lady. I wish it was over!"},{"t":"sd","text":"They sit and mark.","id":178},{"t":"scene","act":1,"scene":"II","loc":"Padua. Before Hortensio's house.","id":179},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Petruchio and his man Grumio.","id":180},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Verona, for a while I take my leave,","To see my friends in Padua; but of all","My best beloved and approved friend,","Hortensio; and I trow this is his house.","Here, sirrah Grumio, knock, I say."],"id":181,"m":"Verona, I'm leaving you for a while to see my friends in Padua, and above all my dearest and most trusted friend, Hortensio. And I think this is his house. Here, Grumio, knock, I tell you."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Knock, sir? Whom should I knock? Is there any man has rebused your","worship?"],"id":182,"m":"Knock, sir? Who should I knock? Has anybody insulted your worship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Villain, I say, knock me here soundly."],"id":183,"m":"You villain, I'm telling you, knock here for me, hard."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Knock you here, sir? Why, sir, what am I, sir, that I should knock you","here, sir?"],"id":184,"m":"Knock you here, sir? Why, sir, who am I, sir, to knock you here, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Villain, I say, knock me at this gate;","And rap me well, or I'll knock your knave's pate."],"id":185,"m":"Villain, I'm telling you, knock on this gate for me, and rap hard, or I'll knock your rascal's head."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["My master is grown quarrelsome. I should knock you first,","And then I know after who comes by the worst."],"id":186,"m":"My master has turned quarrelsome. If I knocked you first, I know who'd come off worst afterwards."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Will it not be?","Faith, sirrah, and you'll not knock, I'll ring it;","I'll try how you can sol, fa, and sing it."],"id":187,"m":"Won't you do it? Well, you, if you won't knock, I'll ring it. Let's see how well you can sing your scales."},{"t":"sd","text":"He wrings Grumio by the ears.","id":188},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Help, masters, help! my master is mad."],"id":189,"m":"Help, gentlemen, help! My master's gone mad."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Now, knock when I bid you, sirrah villain!"],"id":190,"m":"Now knock when I tell you, you villain!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Hortensio.","id":191},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["How now! what's the matter? My old friend Grumio! and my good friend","Petruchio! How do you all at Verona?"],"id":192,"m":"Hello, what's the matter? My old friend Grumio! And my good friend Petruchio! How is everyone in Verona?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Signior Hortensio, come you to part the fray?","Con tutto il cuore ben trovato, may I say."],"id":193,"m":"Signior Hortensio, have you come to break up the fight? With all my heart, well met, I can say."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Alla nostra casa ben venuto; molto honorato signor mio Petruchio.","Rise, Grumio, rise: we will compound this quarrel."],"id":194,"m":"Welcome to our house, my most honoured Signior Petruchio. Get up, Grumio, get up. We'll settle this quarrel."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Nay, 'tis no matter, sir, what he 'leges in Latin. If this be not a","lawful cause for me to leave his service, look you, sir, he bid me","knock him and rap him soundly, sir: well, was it fit for a servant to","use his master so; being, perhaps, for aught I see, two-and-thirty, a","pip out? Whom would to God I had well knock'd at first, then had not","Grumio come by the worst."],"id":195,"m":"No, it doesn't matter, sir, what he alleges in Latin. If this isn't a legal reason for me to leave his service, look here, sir: he told me to knock him and rap him hard, sir. Well, is it right for a servant to treat his master like that, when he's maybe, as far as I can see, a bit past it? I wish to God I'd knocked him hard at the start; then Grumio wouldn't have come off worst."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A senseless villain! Good Hortensio,","I bade the rascal knock upon your gate,","And could not get him for my heart to do it."],"id":196,"m":"A stupid fool! Good Hortensio, I told the rascal to knock on your gate, and I couldn't for the life of me get him to do it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Knock at the gate! O heavens! Spake you not these words plain: 'Sirrah","knock me here, rap me here, knock me well, and knock me soundly'? And","come you now with 'knocking at the gate'?"],"id":197,"m":"Knock at the gate! Oh heavens! Didn't you say these words plainly: \"Knock me here, you, rap me here, knock me well and knock me hard\"? And now you come out with \"knocking at the gate\"?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Sirrah, be gone, or talk not, I advise you."],"id":198,"m":"Go away, you, or keep quiet, I'm warning you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Petruchio, patience; I am Grumio's pledge;","Why, this's a heavy chance 'twixt him and you,","Your ancient, trusty, pleasant servant Grumio.","And tell me now, sweet friend, what happy gale","Blows you to Padua here from old Verona?"],"id":199,"m":"Patience, Petruchio. I'll vouch for Grumio. Why, this is a sad business between you and your old, trusty, amusing servant Grumio. And tell me now, dear friend, what lucky wind blows you here to Padua from old Verona?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Such wind as scatters young men through the world","To seek their fortunes farther than at home,","Where small experience grows. But in a few,","Signior Hortensio, thus it stands with me:","Antonio, my father, is deceas'd,","And I have thrust myself into this maze,","Haply to wive and thrive as best I may;","Crowns in my purse I have, and goods at home,","And so am come abroad to see the world."],"id":200,"m":"The kind of wind that scatters young men around the world to seek their fortunes further away than home, where you don't learn much. But in short, Signior Hortensio, this is how things stand with me. My father, Antonio, is dead, and I've thrown myself into this maze of the world, hoping to marry and prosper as best I can. I have money in my purse and property at home, and so I've come abroad to see the world."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Petruchio, shall I then come roundly to thee","And wish thee to a shrewd ill-favour'd wife?","Thou'dst thank me but a little for my counsel;","And yet I'll promise thee she shall be rich,","And very rich: but th'art too much my friend,","And I'll not wish thee to her."],"id":201,"m":"Petruchio, shall I be blunt with you and recommend you a shrewish, ugly wife? You wouldn't thank me much for the advice. And yet I promise you she'll be rich, very rich. But you're too good a friend, and I won't recommend her to you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Signior Hortensio, 'twixt such friends as we","Few words suffice; and therefore, if thou know","One rich enough to be Petruchio's wife,","As wealth is burden of my wooing dance,","Be she as foul as was Florentius' love,","As old as Sibyl, and as curst and shrewd","As Socrates' Xanthippe or a worse,","She moves me not, or not removes, at least,","Affection's edge in me, were she as rough","As are the swelling Adriatic seas:","I come to wive it wealthily in Padua;","If wealthily, then happily in Padua."],"id":202,"m":"Signior Hortensio, between friends like us, a few words are enough. So, if you know a woman rich enough to be Petruchio's wife (since wealth is the tune I dance my wooing to), she could be as ugly as the hag Florentius married, as old as the Sibyl, and as bad-tempered and shrewish as Socrates' wife Xanthippe, or worse: she won't move me, or at least she won't blunt the edge of my desire, even if she were as rough as the swelling Adriatic Sea. I've come to marry wealthily in Padua. If wealthily, then happily in Padua."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Nay, look you, sir, he tells you flatly what his mind is: why, give him","gold enough and marry him to a puppet or an aglet-baby; or an old trot","with ne'er a tooth in her head, though she have as many diseases as","two-and-fifty horses: why, nothing comes amiss, so money comes withal."],"id":203,"m":"There, you see, sir, he tells you plainly what he's thinking. Why, give him enough gold and marry him to a puppet, or a tiny doll on a lace-tag, or an old hag without a tooth in her head, even if she has as many diseases as fifty-two horses. Why, nothing comes amiss, as long as money comes with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Petruchio, since we are stepp'd thus far in,","I will continue that I broach'd in jest.","I can, Petruchio, help thee to a wife","With wealth enough, and young and beauteous;","Brought up as best becomes a gentlewoman:","Her only fault,—and that is faults enough,—","Is, that she is intolerable curst,","And shrewd and froward, so beyond all measure,","That, were my state far worser than it is,","I would not wed her for a mine of gold."],"id":204,"m":"Petruchio, since we've gone this far, I'll follow up what I started as a joke. I can help you to a wife, Petruchio, with plenty of money, young and beautiful, brought up the way a gentlewoman should be. Her only fault, and it's fault enough, is that she's unbearably bad-tempered, shrewish and difficult, so far beyond all measure that, even if I were much poorer than I am, I wouldn't marry her for a gold mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Hortensio, peace! thou know'st not gold's effect:","Tell me her father's name, and 'tis enough;","For I will board her, though she chide as loud","As thunder when the clouds in autumn crack."],"id":205,"m":"Quiet, Hortensio! You don't know what gold can do. Tell me her father's name, and that's enough, because I'll board her like a ship, even if she scolds as loud as thunder when the autumn clouds crack."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Her father is Baptista Minola,","An affable and courteous gentleman;","Her name is Katherina Minola,","Renown'd in Padua for her scolding tongue."],"id":206,"m":"Her father is Baptista Minola, a friendly and courteous gentleman. Her name is Katherina Minola, famous in Padua for her scolding tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I know her father, though I know not her;","And he knew my deceased father well.","I will not sleep, Hortensio, till I see her;","And therefore let me be thus bold with you,","To give you over at this first encounter,","Unless you will accompany me thither."],"id":207,"m":"I know her father, though I don't know her, and he knew my late father well. I won't sleep, Hortensio, until I've seen her. So forgive me if I'm rude enough to leave you at our very first meeting, unless you'll come there with me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I pray you, sir, let him go while the humour lasts. O' my word, and she","knew him as well as I do, she would think scolding would do little good","upon him. She may perhaps call him half a score knaves or so; why,","that's nothing; and he begin once, he'll rail in his rope-tricks. I'll","tell you what, sir, and she stand him but a little, he will throw a","figure in her face, and so disfigure her with it that she shall have no","more eyes to see withal than a cat. You know him not, sir."],"id":208,"m":"Please, sir, let him go while the mood lasts. On my word, if she knew him as well as I do, she'd realise scolding will do little good on him. She might call him a dozen rascals or so. Why, that's nothing. Once he starts, he'll rant with tricks of speech fit for the hangman's rope. I'll tell you what, sir: if she stands up to him even a little, he'll throw a figure of speech in her face and disfigure her so badly that she'll have no more eyes left to see with than a cat. You don't know him, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Tarry, Petruchio, I must go with thee,","For in Baptista's keep my treasure is:","He hath the jewel of my life in hold,","His youngest daughter, beautiful Bianca,","And her withholds from me and other more,","Suitors to her and rivals in my love;","Supposing it a thing impossible,","For those defects I have before rehears'd,","That ever Katherina will be woo'd:","Therefore this order hath Baptista ta'en,","That none shall have access unto Bianca","Till Katherine the curst have got a husband."],"id":209,"m":"Wait, Petruchio, I must go with you, because my treasure is in Baptista's keeping. He holds the jewel of my life, his youngest daughter, beautiful Bianca, and keeps her from me and others too, who court her and compete with me for her love. He thinks it impossible, because of the faults I've just described, that Katherina will ever be wooed. So Baptista has given this order: that nobody can visit Bianca until Katherine the shrew has got a husband."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Katherine the curst!","A title for a maid of all titles the worst."],"id":210,"m":"Katherine the shrew! Of all names for a young woman, that's the worst."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Now shall my friend Petruchio do me grace,","And offer me disguis'd in sober robes,","To old Baptista as a schoolmaster","Well seen in music, to instruct Bianca;","That so I may, by this device at least","Have leave and leisure to make love to her,","And unsuspected court her by herself."],"id":211,"m":"Now my friend Petruchio will do me a favour, and present me, disguised in plain clothes, to old Baptista as a schoolmaster skilled in music, to teach Bianca. That way, by this trick at least, I'll have the chance and the time to court her, and woo her alone without being suspected."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Here's no knavery! See, to beguile the old folks, how the young folks","lay their heads together!"],"id":212,"m":"No trickery here! Look how the young folk put their heads together to fool the old folk!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gremio and Lucentio disguised, with books under his arm.","id":213},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","cont":1,"lines":["Master, master, look about you: who goes there, ha?"],"id":214,"m":"Master, master, look around you. Who goes there, eh?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Peace, Grumio! It is the rival of my love. Petruchio, stand by awhile."],"id":215,"m":"Quiet, Grumio! It's my rival in love. Petruchio, stand aside for a while."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["A proper stripling, and an amorous!"],"id":216,"m":"A fine young fellow, and a lover too!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["O! very well; I have perus'd the note.","Hark you, sir; I'll have them very fairly bound:","All books of love, see that at any hand,","And see you read no other lectures to her.","You understand me. Over and beside","Signior Baptista's liberality,","I'll mend it with a largess. Take your papers too,","And let me have them very well perfum'd;","For she is sweeter than perfume itself","To whom they go to. What will you read to her?"],"id":217,"m":"Oh, very good. I've read through the list. Listen, sir, I'll have the books very handsomely bound: all books about love, make sure of that, whatever happens, and make sure you don't give her any other lessons. You understand me. On top of Signior Baptista's generosity, I'll add a tip of my own. Take your papers too, and have them well perfumed, because the lady they're going to is sweeter than perfume itself. What will you read to her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Whate'er I read to her, I'll plead for you,","As for my patron, stand you so assur'd,","As firmly as yourself were still in place;","Yea, and perhaps with more successful words","Than you, unless you were a scholar, sir."],"id":218,"m":"Whatever I read to her, I'll plead for you as my patron, you can be sure of that, as firmly as if you were there yourself. Yes, and perhaps with more successful words than yours, sir, unless you were a scholar."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["O! this learning, what a thing it is."],"id":219,"m":"Oh, this learning, what a thing it is!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["O! this woodcock, what an ass it is."],"id":220,"m":"Oh, this woodcock, what an ass he is!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Peace, sirrah!"],"id":221,"m":"Quiet, you!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Grumio, mum! God save you, Signior Gremio!"],"id":222,"m":"Grumio, hush! God save you, Signior Gremio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["And you are well met, Signior Hortensio.","Trow you whither I am going? To Baptista Minola.","I promis'd to enquire carefully","About a schoolmaster for the fair Bianca;","And by good fortune I have lighted well","On this young man; for learning and behaviour","Fit for her turn, well read in poetry","And other books, good ones, I warrant ye."],"id":223,"m":"And good to meet you, Signior Hortensio. Do you know where I'm going? To Baptista Minola. I promised to look carefully for a schoolmaster for the fair Bianca, and by good luck I've found this young man: in learning and manners just right for her, well read in poetry and other books, good ones, I assure you."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["'Tis well; and I have met a gentleman","Hath promis'd me to help me to another,","A fine musician to instruct our mistress:","So shall I no whit be behind in duty","To fair Bianca, so belov'd of me."],"id":224,"m":"That's good. And I've met a gentleman who has promised to help me to another, a fine musician to teach our mistress. So I won't be one bit behind in serving fair Bianca, whom I love so much."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Belov'd of me, and that my deeds shall prove."],"id":225,"m":"Whom I love, and my deeds will prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["[Aside] And that his bags shall prove."],"id":226,"m":"And his moneybags will prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Gremio, 'tis now no time to vent our love:","Listen to me, and if you speak me fair,","I'll tell you news indifferent good for either.","Here is a gentleman whom by chance I met,","Upon agreement from us to his liking,","Will undertake to woo curst Katherine;","Yea, and to marry her, if her dowry please."],"id":227,"m":"Gremio, this is no time to air our love. Listen to me, and if you speak to me politely, I'll tell you news that's equally good for both of us. Here's a gentleman I met by chance who, if we agree to his terms, will undertake to woo shrewish Katherine. Yes, and marry her, if he likes the dowry."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["So said, so done, is well.","Hortensio, have you told him all her faults?"],"id":228,"m":"No sooner said than done would be good. Hortensio, have you told him all her faults?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I know she is an irksome brawling scold;","If that be all, masters, I hear no harm."],"id":229,"m":"I know she's an annoying, brawling scold. If that's all, gentlemen, I see no harm in it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["No, say'st me so, friend? What countryman?"],"id":230,"m":"No? You say so, friend? Where are you from?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Born in Verona, old Antonio's son.","My father dead, my fortune lives for me;","And I do hope good days and long to see."],"id":231,"m":"I was born in Verona, old Antonio's son. My father's dead, my fortune lives on for me, and I hope to see good days and many of them."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["O sir, such a life, with such a wife, were strange!","But if you have a stomach, to't a God's name;","You shall have me assisting you in all.","But will you woo this wild-cat?"],"id":232,"m":"Oh sir, such a life with such a wife would be strange! But if you have the appetite for it, go ahead in God's name. I'll help you in everything. But will you really woo this wildcat?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Will I live?"],"id":233,"m":"Do I want to live?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Will he woo her? Ay, or I'll hang her."],"id":234,"m":"Will he woo her? Yes, or I'll hang her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why came I hither but to that intent?","Think you a little din can daunt mine ears?","Have I not in my time heard lions roar?","Have I not heard the sea, puff'd up with winds,","Rage like an angry boar chafed with sweat?","Have I not heard great ordnance in the field,","And heaven's artillery thunder in the skies?","Have I not in a pitched battle heard","Loud 'larums, neighing steeds, and trumpets' clang?","And do you tell me of a woman's tongue,","That gives not half so great a blow to hear","As will a chestnut in a farmer's fire?","Tush, tush! fear boys with bugs."],"id":235,"m":"Why else did I come here but for that? Do you think a little noise can scare my ears? Haven't I in my time heard lions roar? Haven't I heard the sea, whipped up by the winds, rage like an angry boar foaming with sweat? Haven't I heard great cannon on the battlefield and heaven's artillery thundering in the sky? Haven't I heard, in a pitched battle, loud alarms, neighing horses and blaring trumpets? And you talk to me about a woman's tongue, which doesn't make half as loud a bang to the ear as a chestnut popping in a farmer's fire? Nonsense! Frighten little boys with bogeymen."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["[Aside] For he fears none."],"id":236,"m":"Because he's afraid of none."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Hortensio, hark:","This gentleman is happily arriv'd,","My mind presumes, for his own good and yours."],"id":237,"m":"Hortensio, listen. I think this gentleman has arrived at a lucky moment, for his own good and yours."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["I promis'd we would be contributors,","And bear his charge of wooing, whatsoe'er."],"id":238,"m":"I promised we would contribute and pay the costs of his wooing, whatever they are."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["And so we will, provided that he win her."],"id":239,"m":"And so we will, as long as he wins her."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I would I were as sure of a good dinner."],"id":240,"m":"I wish I was as sure of a good dinner."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Tranio brave, and Biondello.","id":241},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Gentlemen, God save you! If I may be bold,","Tell me, I beseech you, which is the readiest way","To the house of Signior Baptista Minola?"],"id":242,"m":"Gentlemen, God save you! If I may be so bold, please tell me the quickest way to the house of Signior Baptista Minola."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["He that has the two fair daughters; is't he you mean?"],"id":243,"m":"The one with the two pretty daughters: is he the man you mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Even he, Biondello!"],"id":244,"m":"That's him, Biondello!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Hark you, sir, you mean not her to—"],"id":245,"m":"Listen, sir, you don't mean to court her..."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Perhaps him and her, sir; what have you to do?"],"id":246,"m":"Perhaps him and her, sir. What business is it of yours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Not her that chides, sir, at any hand, I pray."],"id":247,"m":"Not the one who scolds, sir, at any rate, please."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["I love no chiders, sir. Biondello, let's away."],"id":248,"m":"I don't like scolds, sir. Biondello, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["[Aside] Well begun, Tranio."],"id":249,"m":"Well begun, Tranio."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Sir, a word ere you go.","Are you a suitor to the maid you talk of, yea or no?"],"id":250,"m":"Sir, a word before you go. Are you a suitor to the girl you're talking about, yes or no?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["And if I be, sir, is it any offence?"],"id":251,"m":"And if I am, sir, is that a crime?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["No; if without more words you will get you hence."],"id":252,"m":"No, as long as you clear off without another word."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Why, sir, I pray, are not the streets as free","For me as for you?"],"id":253,"m":"Why, sir, please, aren't the streets as free for me as for you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["But so is not she."],"id":254,"m":"Yes, but she isn't."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["For what reason, I beseech you?"],"id":255,"m":"For what reason, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["For this reason, if you'll know,","That she's the choice love of Signior Gremio."],"id":256,"m":"For this reason, if you want to know: she's the chosen love of Signior Gremio."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["That she's the chosen of Signior Hortensio."],"id":257,"m":"She's the chosen one of Signior Hortensio."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Softly, my masters! If you be gentlemen,","Do me this right; hear me with patience.","Baptista is a noble gentleman,","To whom my father is not all unknown;","And were his daughter fairer than she is,","She may more suitors have, and me for one.","Fair Leda's daughter had a thousand wooers;","Then well one more may fair Bianca have;","And so she shall: Lucentio shall make one,","Though Paris came in hope to speed alone."],"id":258,"m":"Gently, gentlemen! If you're gentlemen, do me the courtesy of hearing me out patiently. Baptista is a noble gentleman who knows something of my father. And even if his daughter were more beautiful than she is, she can have more suitors, with me as one of them. Beautiful Helen, Leda's daughter, had a thousand suitors, so fair Bianca can easily have one more. And she shall: Lucentio will be one of them, even if Paris himself came hoping to win her alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["What, this gentleman will out-talk us all."],"id":259,"m":"What! This gentleman will out-talk us all."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Sir, give him head; I know he'll prove a jade."],"id":260,"m":"Sir, give him his head. I know he'll turn out to be a worn-out nag."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Hortensio, to what end are all these words?"],"id":261,"m":"Hortensio, what's the point of all this talk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Sir, let me be so bold as ask you,","Did you yet ever see Baptista's daughter?"],"id":262,"m":"Sir, may I be so bold as to ask: have you ever actually seen Baptista's daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["No, sir, but hear I do that he hath two,","The one as famous for a scolding tongue","As is the other for beauteous modesty."],"id":263,"m":"No, sir, but I hear he has two: one as famous for her scolding tongue as the other is for her beauty and modesty."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Sir, sir, the first's for me; let her go by."],"id":264,"m":"Sir, sir, the first one's for me. Leave her alone."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Yea, leave that labour to great Hercules,","And let it be more than Alcides' twelve."],"id":265,"m":"Yes, leave that job to great Hercules, and make it harder than his twelve labours."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Sir, understand you this of me, in sooth:","The youngest daughter, whom you hearken for,","Her father keeps from all access of suitors,","And will not promise her to any man","Until the elder sister first be wed;","The younger then is free, and not before."],"id":266,"m":"Sir, understand this from me, honestly. The youngest daughter, the one you're after, her father keeps away from all suitors, and won't promise her to any man until the elder sister is married first. Then the younger is free, and not before."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["If it be so, sir, that you are the man","Must stead us all, and me amongst the rest;","And if you break the ice, and do this feat,","Achieve the elder, set the younger free","For our access, whose hap shall be to have her","Will not so graceless be to be ingrate."],"id":267,"m":"If that's so, sir, then you're the man who must help us all, me among the rest. And if you break the ice and do this deed, win the elder and free the younger for us to court, whoever is lucky enough to get her won't be so rude as to be ungrateful."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Sir, you say well, and well you do conceive;","And since you do profess to be a suitor,","You must, as we do, gratify this gentleman,","To whom we all rest generally beholding."],"id":268,"m":"Sir, you speak well and think well. And since you say you're a suitor, you must reward this gentleman, as we will, since we'll all be in his debt."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Sir, I shall not be slack; in sign whereof,","Please ye we may contrive this afternoon,","And quaff carouses to our mistress' health;","And do as adversaries do in law,","Strive mightily, but eat and drink as friends."],"id":269,"m":"Sir, I won't hold back. And as a sign of it, if you agree, let's spend this afternoon drinking toasts to our mistress's health, and do as opponents in a lawsuit do: fight hard, but eat and drink as friends."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO AND BIONDELLO","lines":["O excellent motion! Fellows, let's be gone."],"id":270,"m":"Oh, an excellent idea! Lads, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["The motion's good indeed, and be it so:—","Petruchio, I shall be your ben venuto."],"id":271,"m":"A good idea indeed, so let it be. Petruchio, I'll be your host."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":272},{"t":"scene","act":2,"scene":"I","loc":"Padua. A room in Baptista's house.","id":273},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Katherina and Bianca.","id":274},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Good sister, wrong me not, nor wrong yourself,","To make a bondmaid and a slave of me;","That I disdain; but for these other gawds,","Unbind my hands, I'll pull them off myself,","Yea, all my raiment, to my petticoat;","Or what you will command me will I do,","So well I know my duty to my elders."],"id":275,"m":"Good sister, don't wrong me, or yourself, by making a servant and a slave of me. I hate that. But as for these other trinkets, untie my hands and I'll take them off myself, yes, all my clothes down to my petticoat. Or I'll do whatever you order me, because I know so well my duty to my elders."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Of all thy suitors here I charge thee tell","Whom thou lov'st best: see thou dissemble not."],"id":276,"m":"I order you to tell me which of all your suitors here you love best. Make sure you don't lie."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Believe me, sister, of all the men alive","I never yet beheld that special face","Which I could fancy more than any other."],"id":277,"m":"Believe me, sister, of all the men alive I've never yet seen that special face I could like more than any other."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Minion, thou liest. Is't not Hortensio?"],"id":278,"m":"You little madam, you're lying. Isn't it Hortensio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["If you affect him, sister, here I swear","I'll plead for you myself but you shall have him."],"id":279,"m":"If you like him, sister, I swear here and now I'll plead for you myself, so that you'll have him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["O! then, belike, you fancy riches more:","You will have Gremio to keep you fair."],"id":280,"m":"Oh, then I suppose you prefer riches. You'll have Gremio, to keep you in fine clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Is it for him you do envy me so?","Nay, then you jest; and now I well perceive","You have but jested with me all this while:","I prithee, sister Kate, untie my hands."],"id":281,"m":"Is it because of him that you envy me so much? No, you're joking, and now I can see you've only been joking with me all this time. Please, sister Kate, untie my hands."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["If that be jest, then all the rest was so."],"id":282,"m":"If that's a joke, then so was all the rest."},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes her.","id":283},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Baptista.","id":284},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Why, how now, dame! Whence grows this insolence?","Bianca, stand aside. Poor girl! she weeps.","Go ply thy needle; meddle not with her.","For shame, thou hilding of a devilish spirit,","Why dost thou wrong her that did ne'er wrong thee?","When did she cross thee with a bitter word?"],"id":285,"m":"What's this, madam! Where does this insolence come from? Bianca, stand aside. Poor girl, she's crying. Go and do your sewing; don't have anything to do with her. For shame, you good-for-nothing with a devil's temper! Why do you hurt her, when she never hurt you? When did she ever cross you with a bitter word?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Her silence flouts me, and I'll be reveng'd."],"id":286,"m":"Her silence mocks me, and I'll have my revenge."},{"t":"sd","text":"Flies after Bianca.","id":287},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["What! in my sight? Bianca, get thee in."],"id":288,"m":"What! In front of me? Bianca, go inside."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Bianca.","id":289},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["What! will you not suffer me? Nay, now I see","She is your treasure, she must have a husband;","I must dance bare-foot on her wedding-day,","And, for your love to her, lead apes in hell.","Talk not to me: I will go sit and weep","Till I can find occasion of revenge."],"id":290,"m":"What! You won't put up with me? No, now I see it: she's your treasure, she must have a husband. I must dance barefoot at her wedding, like an older sister left unmarried, and because you love her I'll lead apes in hell as an old maid. Don't talk to me. I'll go and sit and cry until I can find a chance for revenge."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":291},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Was ever gentleman thus griev'd as I?","But who comes here?"],"id":292,"m":"Was any gentleman ever as troubled as I am? But who's this coming?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Gremio, with Lucentio in the habit of a mean man; Petruchio, with Hortensio as a musician; and Tranio, with Biondello bearing a lute and books.","id":293},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Good morrow, neighbour Baptista."],"id":294,"m":"Good morning, neighbour Baptista."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Good morrow, neighbour Gremio. God save you, gentlemen!"],"id":295,"m":"Good morning, neighbour Gremio. God save you, gentlemen!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["And you, good sir! Pray, have you not a daughter","Call'd Katherina, fair and virtuous?"],"id":296,"m":"And you, good sir! Please, don't you have a daughter called Katherina, beautiful and virtuous?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["I have a daughter, sir, call'd Katherina."],"id":297,"m":"I have a daughter, sir, called Katherina."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["You are too blunt: go to it orderly."],"id":298,"m":"You're too blunt. Go about it properly."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["You wrong me, Signior Gremio: give me leave.","I am a gentleman of Verona, sir,","That, hearing of her beauty and her wit,","Her affability and bashful modesty,","Her wondrous qualities and mild behaviour,","Am bold to show myself a forward guest","Within your house, to make mine eye the witness","Of that report which I so oft have heard.","And, for an entrance to my entertainment,","I do present you with a man of mine,"],"id":299,"m":"You're unfair to me, Signior Gremio. Let me do this. I'm a gentleman of Verona, sir, who has heard of her beauty and her wit, her friendliness and shy modesty, her wonderful qualities and mild behaviour, and so I'm bold enough to come as an eager guest to your house, to see with my own eyes the truth of what I've so often heard. And as my ticket of entry, I present you with a man of mine,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Presenting Hortensio.","id":300},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["Cunning in music and the mathematics,","To instruct her fully in those sciences,","Whereof I know she is not ignorant.","Accept of him, or else you do me wrong:","His name is Licio, born in Mantua."],"id":301,"m":"skilled in music and mathematics, to teach her fully in those subjects, which I know she already knows something of. Accept him, or you'll offend me. His name is Licio, born in Mantua."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Y'are welcome, sir, and he for your good sake;","But for my daughter Katherine, this I know,","She is not for your turn, the more my grief."],"id":302,"m":"You're welcome, sir, and so is he, for your sake. But as for my daughter Katherine, this much I know: she isn't the right one for you, which grieves me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I see you do not mean to part with her;","Or else you like not of my company."],"id":303,"m":"I see you don't mean to part with her, or else you don't like my company."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Mistake me not; I speak but as I find.","Whence are you, sir? What may I call your name?"],"id":304,"m":"Don't misunderstand me. I'm only saying what I find to be true. Where are you from, sir? What's your name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Petruchio is my name, Antonio's son;","A man well known throughout all Italy."],"id":305,"m":"Petruchio is my name, Antonio's son, a man well known throughout Italy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["I know him well: you are welcome for his sake."],"id":306,"m":"I knew him well. You're welcome for his sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Saving your tale, Petruchio, I pray,","Let us, that are poor petitioners, speak too.","Backare! you are marvellous forward."],"id":307,"m":"With respect to your story, Petruchio, please let us poor petitioners speak too. Back off! You're amazingly pushy."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["O, pardon me, Signior Gremio; I would fain be doing."],"id":308,"m":"Oh, forgive me, Signior Gremio. I'm keen to get on with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["I doubt it not, sir; but you will curse your wooing. Neighbour, this is","a gift very grateful, I am sure of it. To express the like kindness,","myself, that have been more kindly beholding to you than any, freely","give unto you this young scholar,"],"id":309,"m":"I don't doubt it, sir, but you'll curse your wooing. Neighbour, this is a very welcome gift, I'm sure. To show the same kindness myself, as I've been more kindly treated by you than anyone, I freely give you this young scholar,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Presenting Lucentio.","id":310},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","cont":1,"lines":["that has been long studying at Rheims; as cunning in Greek, Latin, and","other languages, as the other in music and mathematics. His name is","Cambio; pray accept his service."],"id":311,"m":"who has been studying for a long time at Rheims, as skilled in Greek, Latin and other languages as the other is in music and mathematics. His name is Cambio. Please accept his service."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["A thousand thanks, Signior Gremio; welcome, good Cambio. [To Tranio]","But, gentle sir, methinks you walk like a stranger. May I be so bold to","know the cause of your coming?"],"id":312,"m":"A thousand thanks, Signior Gremio. Welcome, good Cambio. But, kind sir, you seem to be a stranger here. May I be so bold as to ask why you've come?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Pardon me, sir, the boldness is mine own,","That, being a stranger in this city here,","Do make myself a suitor to your daughter,","Unto Bianca, fair and virtuous.","Nor is your firm resolve unknown to me,","In the preferment of the eldest sister.","This liberty is all that I request,","That, upon knowledge of my parentage,","I may have welcome 'mongst the rest that woo,","And free access and favour as the rest:","And, toward the education of your daughters,","I here bestow a simple instrument,","And this small packet of Greek and Latin books:","If you accept them, then their worth is great."],"id":313,"m":"Forgive me, sir, the boldness is all mine. Though I'm a stranger in this city, I'm offering myself as a suitor to your daughter, to Bianca, beautiful and virtuous. And I know your firm decision that the eldest sister must marry first. All I ask is this permission: that once you know my family, I may be welcome among the other suitors, with the same free access and favour as they have. And towards your daughters' education, I give you this simple instrument and this small parcel of Greek and Latin books. If you accept them, then they're worth a great deal."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Lucentio is your name, of whence, I pray?"],"id":314,"m":"Lucentio is your name? Where from, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Of Pisa, sir; son to Vincentio."],"id":315,"m":"From Pisa, sir: son of Vincentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["A mighty man of Pisa: by report","I know him well: you are very welcome, sir.","[To Hortensio] Take you the lute,","[To Lucentio] and you the set of books;","You shall go see your pupils presently.","Holla, within!"],"id":316,"m":"A powerful man in Pisa. I know him well by reputation. You're very welcome, sir. You take the lute, and you the set of books. You'll go and see your pupils at once. Hello in there!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":317},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","cont":1,"lines":["Sirrah, lead these gentlemen","To my daughters, and tell them both","These are their tutors: bid them use them well."],"id":318,"m":"You, take these gentlemen to my daughters, and tell them both these are their tutors. Tell them to treat them well."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Servant with Hortensio, Lucentio and Biondello.","id":319},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","cont":1,"lines":["We will go walk a little in the orchard,","And then to dinner. You are passing welcome,","And so I pray you all to think yourselves."],"id":320,"m":"We'll take a little walk in the orchard, and then go in to dinner. You're very welcome, and I ask you all to feel so."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Signior Baptista, my business asketh haste,","And every day I cannot come to woo.","You knew my father well, and in him me,","Left solely heir to all his lands and goods,","Which I have bettered rather than decreas'd:","Then tell me, if I get your daughter's love,","What dowry shall I have with her to wife?"],"id":321,"m":"Signior Baptista, my business needs speed, and I can't come wooing every day. You knew my father well, and through him you know me: the sole heir to all his lands and goods, which I've increased rather than reduced. So tell me, if I win your daughter's love, what dowry will I get with her as my wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["After my death, the one half of my lands,","And in possession twenty thousand crowns."],"id":322,"m":"After my death, half of my lands, and twenty thousand crowns in hand now."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["And, for that dowry, I'll assure her of","Her widowhood, be it that she survive me,","In all my lands and leases whatsoever.","Let specialities be therefore drawn between us,","That covenants may be kept on either hand."],"id":323,"m":"And in return for that dowry, I'll guarantee her, if she outlives me, a widow's share of all my lands and leases. So let's have contracts drawn up between us, so the agreements are kept on both sides."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Ay, when the special thing is well obtain'd,","That is, her love; for that is all in all."],"id":324,"m":"Yes, once the special thing has been won, that is, her love, because that's all that matters."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, that is nothing; for I tell you, father,","I am as peremptory as she proud-minded;","And where two raging fires meet together,","They do consume the thing that feeds their fury:","Though little fire grows great with little wind,","Yet extreme gusts will blow out fire and all;","So I to her, and so she yields to me;","For I am rough and woo not like a babe."],"id":325,"m":"Why, that's nothing. I tell you, father, I'm as determined as she is proud. And when two raging fires meet, they burn up whatever feeds their fury. A little fire grows big with a little wind, but a huge gust blows out the fire and everything. That's what I'll be to her, and so she'll give in to me, because I'm rough and I don't woo like a baby."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Well mayst thou woo, and happy be thy speed!","But be thou arm'd for some unhappy words."],"id":326,"m":"I hope you woo well and succeed! But be armed against some unpleasant words."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Ay, to the proof, as mountains are for winds,","That shake not though they blow perpetually."],"id":327,"m":"Yes, fully armed, as mountains are against the winds, which don't shake though the winds blow for ever."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Hortensio, with his head broke.","id":328},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["How now, my friend! Why dost thou look so pale?"],"id":329,"m":"What's this, my friend? Why do you look so pale?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["For fear, I promise you, if I look pale."],"id":330,"m":"From fear, I promise you, if I look pale."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["What, will my daughter prove a good musician?"],"id":331,"m":"Well, will my daughter turn out to be a good musician?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["I think she'll sooner prove a soldier:","Iron may hold with her, but never lutes."],"id":332,"m":"I think she'll sooner turn out a soldier. Iron weapons might last with her, but never lutes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Why, then thou canst not break her to the lute?"],"id":333,"m":"Why, can't you break her in to the lute, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Why, no; for she hath broke the lute to me.","I did but tell her she mistook her frets,","And bow'd her hand to teach her fingering;","When, with a most impatient devilish spirit,","'Frets, call you these?' quoth she 'I'll fume with them';","And with that word she struck me on the head,","And through the instrument my pate made way;","And there I stood amazed for a while,","As on a pillory, looking through the lute;","While she did call me rascal fiddler,","And twangling Jack, with twenty such vile terms,","As had she studied to misuse me so."],"id":334,"m":"Why, no, because she's broken the lute on me. I only told her she'd got her frets wrong and bent her hand to teach her the fingering, when, with a most impatient, devilish temper, she said, \"Frets, you call these? I'll fret and fume with them!\" And with that she hit me on the head, and my head went through the instrument. And there I stood, dazed for a while, as if in a pillory, looking through the lute, while she called me a rascally fiddler and a twanging fool, with twenty other vile names, as if she'd practised insulting me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Now, by the world, it is a lusty wench!","I love her ten times more than e'er I did:","O! how I long to have some chat with her!"],"id":335,"m":"Now, by the world, she's a lively girl! I love her ten times more than I did before. Oh, how I long to have a chat with her!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["[To Hortensio] Well, go with me, and be not so discomfited;","Proceed in practice with my younger daughter;","She's apt to learn, and thankful for good turns.","Signior Petruchio, will you go with us,","Or shall I send my daughter Kate to you?"],"id":336,"m":"Well, come with me, and don't be so upset. Carry on teaching my younger daughter. She's quick to learn, and grateful for kindness. Signior Petruchio, will you come with us, or shall I send my daughter Kate to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I pray you do."],"id":337,"m":"Please do."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Baptista, Gremio, Tranio and Hortensio.","id":338},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["I will attend her here,","And woo her with some spirit when she comes.","Say that she rail; why, then I'll tell her plain","She sings as sweetly as a nightingale:","Say that she frown; I'll say she looks as clear","As morning roses newly wash'd with dew:","Say she be mute, and will not speak a word;","Then I'll commend her volubility,","And say she uttereth piercing eloquence:","If she do bid me pack, I'll give her thanks,","As though she bid me stay by her a week:","If she deny to wed, I'll crave the day","When I shall ask the banns, and when be married.","But here she comes; and now, Petruchio, speak."],"id":339,"m":"I'll wait for her here, and woo her with some spirit when she comes. Suppose she rants: why, then I'll tell her plainly she sings as sweetly as a nightingale. Suppose she frowns: I'll say she looks as fresh as morning roses newly washed with dew. Suppose she's silent and won't say a word: then I'll praise her talkativeness and say she speaks with stirring eloquence. If she tells me to get out, I'll thank her as if she'd asked me to stay with her a week. If she refuses to marry, I'll ask her which day I should have the banns read, and when we'll be married. But here she comes. And now, Petruchio, speak."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Katherina.","id":340},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["Good morrow, Kate; for that's your name, I hear."],"id":341,"m":"Good morning, Kate, because that's your name, I hear."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Well have you heard, but something hard of hearing:","They call me Katherine that do talk of me."],"id":342,"m":"You've heard well, but you're a bit hard of hearing. People who talk about me call me Katherine."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["You lie, in faith, for you are call'd plain Kate,","And bonny Kate, and sometimes Kate the curst;","But, Kate, the prettiest Kate in Christendom,","Kate of Kate Hall, my super-dainty Kate,","For dainties are all Kates, and therefore, Kate,","Take this of me, Kate of my consolation;","Hearing thy mildness prais'd in every town,","Thy virtues spoke of, and thy beauty sounded,—","Yet not so deeply as to thee belongs,—","Myself am mov'd to woo thee for my wife."],"id":343,"m":"You're lying, honestly, because you're called plain Kate, and pretty Kate, and sometimes Kate the shrew. But Kate, the prettiest Kate in Christendom, Kate of Kate Hall, my extra-dainty Kate (because all delicacies are \"cates\", and so, Kate), hear this from me, Kate of my comfort: hearing your gentleness praised in every town, your virtues talked about and your beauty proclaimed (though not as highly as you deserve), I've been moved myself to woo you as my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Mov'd! in good time: let him that mov'd you hither","Remove you hence. I knew you at the first,","You were a moveable."],"id":344,"m":"Moved! Well, well. Let whoever moved you here move you away again. I could tell straight away you were a piece of furniture."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, what's a moveable?"],"id":345,"m":"Why, what's a piece of furniture?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["A joint-stool."],"id":346,"m":"A wooden stool."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Thou hast hit it: come, sit on me."],"id":347,"m":"You've hit it. Come and sit on me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Asses are made to bear, and so are you."],"id":348,"m":"Donkeys are made to carry loads, and so are you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Women are made to bear, and so are you."],"id":349,"m":"Women are made to bear children, and so are you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["No such jade as bear you, if me you mean."],"id":350,"m":"I'm not a worn-out horse that will carry you, if you mean me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Alas! good Kate, I will not burden thee;","For, knowing thee to be but young and light,—"],"id":351,"m":"Oh dear, good Kate, I won't be a burden to you, because, knowing you're only young and light..."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Too light for such a swain as you to catch;","And yet as heavy as my weight should be."],"id":352,"m":"Too light-footed for a peasant like you to catch, and yet as heavy as I ought to be."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Should be! should buz!"],"id":353,"m":"Ought to be! Ought to buzz!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Well ta'en, and like a buzzard."],"id":354,"m":"Well caught, like a useless hawk."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["O, slow-wing'd turtle! shall a buzzard take thee?"],"id":355,"m":"Oh, slow-winged turtle-dove! Shall a hawk catch you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Ay, for a turtle, as he takes a buzzard."],"id":356,"m":"Yes, he'll take me for a dove, as a dove takes a buzzing beetle."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Come, come, you wasp; i' faith, you are too angry."],"id":357,"m":"Come, come, you wasp. Honestly, you're too angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["If I be waspish, best beware my sting."],"id":358,"m":"If I'm waspish, you'd better watch out for my sting."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["My remedy is then to pluck it out."],"id":359,"m":"Then my cure is to pull it out."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Ay, if the fool could find it where it lies."],"id":360,"m":"Yes, if the fool could find where it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Who knows not where a wasp does wear his sting?","In his tail."],"id":361,"m":"Who doesn't know where a wasp keeps its sting? In its tail."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["In his tongue."],"id":362,"m":"In its tongue."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Whose tongue?"],"id":363,"m":"Whose tongue?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Yours, if you talk of tales; and so farewell."],"id":364,"m":"Yours, if you talk about tails. And so goodbye."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["What! with my tongue in your tail? Nay, come again,","Good Kate; I am a gentleman."],"id":365,"m":"What, with my tongue in your tail? No, come back, good Kate. I'm a gentleman."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["That I'll try."],"id":366,"m":"I'll test that."},{"t":"sd","text":"Striking him.","id":367},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I swear I'll cuff you if you strike again."],"id":368,"m":"I swear I'll hit you if you strike me again."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["So may you lose your arms:","If you strike me, you are no gentleman;","And if no gentleman, why then no arms."],"id":369,"m":"Then you'll lose your coat of arms. If you hit me, you're no gentleman, and if you're no gentleman, then you have no arms."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A herald, Kate? O! put me in thy books."],"id":370,"m":"An expert in heraldry, Kate? Oh, put me in your good books."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["What is your crest? a coxcomb?"],"id":371,"m":"What's your crest? A fool's cap?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A combless cock, so Kate will be my hen."],"id":372,"m":"A gentle cock without a comb, if Kate will be my hen."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["No cock of mine; you crow too like a craven."],"id":373,"m":"You're no cock of mine. You crow like a cowardly bird."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Nay, come, Kate, come; you must not look so sour."],"id":374,"m":"No, come on, Kate, come on. You mustn't look so sour."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["It is my fashion when I see a crab."],"id":375,"m":"It's my habit when I see a crab apple."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, here's no crab, and therefore look not sour."],"id":376,"m":"Why, there's no crab apple here, so don't look sour."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["There is, there is."],"id":377,"m":"There is, there is."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Then show it me."],"id":378,"m":"Then show it to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Had I a glass I would."],"id":379,"m":"If I had a mirror I would."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["What, you mean my face?"],"id":380,"m":"What, you mean my face?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Well aim'd of such a young one."],"id":381,"m":"Good aim, for such a youngster."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Now, by Saint George, I am too young for you."],"id":382,"m":"Now, by Saint George, I'm too young for you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Yet you are wither'd."],"id":383,"m":"And yet you're wrinkled."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["'Tis with cares."],"id":384,"m":"It's from worries."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I care not."],"id":385,"m":"I don't care."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Nay, hear you, Kate: in sooth, you 'scape not so."],"id":386,"m":"No, listen, Kate. Honestly, you won't get away like that."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I chafe you, if I tarry; let me go."],"id":387,"m":"I'll only annoy you if I stay. Let me go."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["No, not a whit; I find you passing gentle.","'Twas told me you were rough, and coy, and sullen,","And now I find report a very liar;","For thou art pleasant, gamesome, passing courteous,","But slow in speech, yet sweet as spring-time flowers.","Thou canst not frown, thou canst not look askance,","Nor bite the lip, as angry wenches will,","Nor hast thou pleasure to be cross in talk;","But thou with mildness entertain'st thy wooers;","With gentle conference, soft and affable.","Why does the world report that Kate doth limp?","O sland'rous world! Kate like the hazel-twig","Is straight and slender, and as brown in hue","As hazel-nuts, and sweeter than the kernels.","O! let me see thee walk: thou dost not halt."],"id":388,"m":"No, not a bit. I find you very gentle. I was told you were rough, standoffish and sulky, and now I find the rumours were downright lies. You're pleasant, playful, extremely polite, slow to speak, but sweet as springtime flowers. You can't frown, you can't look sideways at anyone, or bite your lip the way angry girls do, and you don't enjoy arguing. Instead you treat your suitors gently, with mild, soft and friendly talk. Why do people say Kate limps? Oh, lying world! Kate is as straight and slender as a hazel twig, as brown as hazelnuts and sweeter than their kernels. Oh, let me see you walk. You don't limp."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Go, fool, and whom thou keep'st command."],"id":389,"m":"Go away, fool, and give orders to your own servants."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Did ever Dian so become a grove","As Kate this chamber with her princely gait?","O! be thou Dian, and let her be Kate,","And then let Kate be chaste, and Dian sportful!"],"id":390,"m":"Did Diana ever grace a forest as Kate graces this room with her royal walk? Oh, you be Diana, and let her be Kate, and then let Kate be chaste and Diana playful!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Where did you study all this goodly speech?"],"id":391,"m":"Where did you learn all these fine speeches?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["It is extempore, from my mother-wit."],"id":392,"m":"They're made up on the spot, from my natural wit."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["A witty mother! witless else her son."],"id":393,"m":"A witty mother! Her son would be witless without her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Am I not wise?"],"id":394,"m":"Aren't I clever?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Yes; keep you warm."],"id":395,"m":"Yes, clever enough to keep yourself warm."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Marry, so I mean, sweet Katherine, in thy bed;","And therefore, setting all this chat aside,","Thus in plain terms: your father hath consented","That you shall be my wife your dowry 'greed on;","And will you, nill you, I will marry you.","Now, Kate, I am a husband for your turn;","For, by this light, whereby I see thy beauty,—","Thy beauty that doth make me like thee well,—","Thou must be married to no man but me;","For I am he am born to tame you, Kate,","And bring you from a wild Kate to a Kate","Conformable as other household Kates."],"id":396,"m":"Indeed, that's what I mean to do, sweet Katherine, in your bed. And so, putting all this chatter aside, here it is in plain terms. Your father has agreed that you'll be my wife. Your dowry is agreed. And whether you like it or not, I'll marry you. Now, Kate, I'm the right husband for you, because, by this daylight in which I see your beauty (the beauty that makes me like you so much), you must marry no man but me. I'm the man born to tame you, Kate, and turn you from a wild Kate into a Kate as obedient as other household cats."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Baptista, Gremio and Tranio.","id":397},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["Here comes your father. Never make denial;","I must and will have Katherine to my wife."],"id":398,"m":"Here comes your father. Don't say no. I must and will have Katherine as my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Now, Signior Petruchio, how speed you with my daughter?"],"id":399,"m":"Well, Signior Petruchio, how are you getting on with my daughter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["How but well, sir? how but well?","It were impossible I should speed amiss."],"id":400,"m":"How else but well, sir? How else but well? It would be impossible for me to fail."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Why, how now, daughter Katherine, in your dumps?"],"id":401,"m":"Why, what's this, daughter Katherine? In a sulk?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Call you me daughter? Now I promise you","You have show'd a tender fatherly regard","To wish me wed to one half lunatic,","A mad-cap ruffian and a swearing Jack,","That thinks with oaths to face the matter out."],"id":402,"m":"You call me daughter? Well, I promise you, you've shown great fatherly care in wanting me to marry a half-lunatic, a crazy thug and a swearing lout, who thinks he can bluff his way through with oaths."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Father, 'tis thus: yourself and all the world","That talk'd of her have talk'd amiss of her:","If she be curst, it is for policy,","For she's not froward, but modest as the dove;","She is not hot, but temperate as the morn;","For patience she will prove a second Grissel,","And Roman Lucrece for her chastity;","And to conclude, we have 'greed so well together","That upon Sunday is the wedding-day."],"id":403,"m":"Father, it's like this: you and everyone who has talked about her have got her wrong. If she's shrewish, it's a tactic, because she isn't stubborn, but as gentle as a dove. She isn't hot-tempered, but as calm as the morning. In patience she'll be a second Griselda, and a Roman Lucrece in chastity. And to sum up, we've got on so well together that Sunday is the wedding day."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I'll see thee hang'd on Sunday first."],"id":404,"m":"I'll see you hanged on Sunday first."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Hark, Petruchio; she says she'll see thee hang'd first."],"id":405,"m":"Listen, Petruchio: she says she'll see you hanged first."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Is this your speeding? Nay, then good-night our part!"],"id":406,"m":"Is this your success? Well, then, goodbye to our hopes!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Be patient, gentlemen. I choose her for myself;","If she and I be pleas'd, what's that to you?","'Tis bargain'd 'twixt us twain, being alone,","That she shall still be curst in company.","I tell you, 'tis incredible to believe","How much she loves me: O! the kindest Kate","She hung about my neck, and kiss on kiss","She vied so fast, protesting oath on oath,","That in a twink she won me to her love.","O! you are novices: 'tis a world to see,","How tame, when men and women are alone,","A meacock wretch can make the curstest shrew.","Give me thy hand, Kate; I will unto Venice,","To buy apparel 'gainst the wedding-day.","Provide the feast, father, and bid the guests;","I will be sure my Katherine shall be fine."],"id":407,"m":"Be patient, gentlemen. I'm choosing her for myself. If she and I are happy, what's it to you? We agreed between the two of us, when we were alone, that she would go on being shrewish in company. I tell you, you wouldn't believe how much she loves me. Oh, the kindest Kate! She hung round my neck and piled kiss on kiss so fast, swearing vow after vow, that in a moment she won me to love her. Oh, you're beginners! It's a wonder to see how tame, when men and women are alone, a feeble coward can make the worst shrew. Give me your hand, Kate. I'll go to Venice to buy clothes for the wedding day. Get the feast ready, father, and invite the guests. I'll make sure my Katherine is finely dressed."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["I know not what to say; but give me your hands.","God send you joy, Petruchio! 'Tis a match."],"id":408,"m":"I don't know what to say. But give me your hands. God give you joy, Petruchio! It's a match."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO AND TRANIO","lines":["Amen, say we; we will be witnesses."],"id":409,"m":"Amen, we say. We'll be witnesses."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Father, and wife, and gentlemen, adieu.","I will to Venice; Sunday comes apace;","We will have rings and things, and fine array;","And kiss me, Kate; we will be married o' Sunday."],"id":410,"m":"Father, and wife, and gentlemen, goodbye. I'm off to Venice. Sunday is coming fast. We'll have rings and things and fine clothes. And kiss me, Kate: we'll be married on Sunday."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Petruchio and Katherina, severally.","id":411},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Was ever match clapp'd up so suddenly?"],"id":412,"m":"Was a marriage ever agreed so quickly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Faith, gentlemen, now I play a merchant's part,","And venture madly on a desperate mart."],"id":413,"m":"Well, gentlemen, now I'm playing the merchant, and taking a mad gamble on a risky deal."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["'Twas a commodity lay fretting by you;","'Twill bring you gain, or perish on the seas."],"id":414,"m":"It was goods that were spoiling on your hands. Now it will make you a profit, or be lost at sea."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["The gain I seek is, quiet in the match."],"id":415,"m":"The profit I'm looking for is peace in the marriage."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["No doubt but he hath got a quiet catch.","But now, Baptista, to your younger daughter:","Now is the day we long have looked for;","I am your neighbour, and was suitor first."],"id":416,"m":"No doubt he's made a peaceful catch! But now, Baptista, about your younger daughter. This is the day we've long waited for. I'm your neighbour, and I was her suitor first."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["And I am one that love Bianca more","Than words can witness or your thoughts can guess."],"id":417,"m":"And I'm someone who loves Bianca more than words can say or your thoughts can guess."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Youngling, thou canst not love so dear as I."],"id":418,"m":"Youngster, you can't love her as dearly as I do."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Greybeard, thy love doth freeze."],"id":419,"m":"Greybeard, your love is frozen."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["But thine doth fry.","Skipper, stand back; 'tis age that nourisheth."],"id":420,"m":"But yours is frying. Stand back, you young show-off. It's age that provides for a wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["But youth in ladies' eyes that flourisheth."],"id":421,"m":"But it's youth that blooms in ladies' eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Content you, gentlemen; I'll compound this strife:","'Tis deeds must win the prize, and he of both","That can assure my daughter greatest dower","Shall have my Bianca's love.","Say, Signior Gremio, what can you assure her?"],"id":422,"m":"Calm down, gentlemen. I'll settle this argument. It's deeds that must win the prize, and whichever of you can guarantee my daughter the bigger settlement will have my Bianca's love. Tell me, Signior Gremio, what can you guarantee her?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["First, as you know, my house within the city","Is richly furnished with plate and gold:","Basins and ewers to lave her dainty hands;","My hangings all of Tyrian tapestry;","In ivory coffers I have stuff'd my crowns;","In cypress chests my arras counterpoints,","Costly apparel, tents, and canopies,","Fine linen, Turkey cushions boss'd with pearl,","Valance of Venice gold in needlework;","Pewter and brass, and all things that belong","To house or housekeeping: then, at my farm","I have a hundred milch-kine to the pail,","Six score fat oxen standing in my stalls,","And all things answerable to this portion.","Myself am struck in years, I must confess;","And if I die tomorrow this is hers,","If whilst I live she will be only mine."],"id":423,"m":"First, as you know, my house in the city is richly furnished with silver and gold plate: basins and jugs to wash her dainty hands; my wall-hangings all of crimson Tyrian tapestry. I've stuffed my money into ivory boxes. In cypress-wood chests I keep my woven quilts, expensive clothes, bed-hangings and canopies, fine linen, Turkish cushions studded with pearl, Venetian bed-borders embroidered with gold thread, pewter and brass, and everything that goes with a house and housekeeping. Then at my farm I have a hundred milking cows, a hundred and twenty fat oxen in my stalls, and everything else in proportion. I'm getting on in years, I must admit, and if I die tomorrow all this is hers, as long as she'll be mine only while I'm alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["That 'only' came well in. Sir, list to me:","I am my father's heir and only son;","If I may have your daughter to my wife,","I'll leave her houses three or four as good","Within rich Pisa's walls as anyone","Old Signior Gremio has in Padua;","Besides two thousand ducats by the year","Of fruitful land, all which shall be her jointure.","What, have I pinch'd you, Signior Gremio?"],"id":424,"m":"That \"only\" came in nicely. Sir, listen to me. I'm my father's heir and only son. If I can have your daughter as my wife, I'll leave her three or four houses inside the walls of rich Pisa, every bit as good as any old Signior Gremio has in Padua, besides two thousand ducats a year from fertile land. All of this will be her widow's settlement. What, have I pinched you, Signior Gremio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Two thousand ducats by the year of land!","My land amounts not to so much in all:","That she shall have, besides an argosy","That now is lying in Marseilles' road.","What, have I chok'd you with an argosy?"],"id":425,"m":"Two thousand ducats a year from land! My land isn't worth that much altogether. Still, she'll have it all, plus a merchant ship that's now lying at anchor off Marseilles. What, have I choked you with a merchant ship?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Gremio, 'tis known my father hath no less","Than three great argosies, besides two galliasses,","And twelve tight galleys; these I will assure her,","And twice as much, whate'er thou offer'st next."],"id":426,"m":"Gremio, everyone knows my father has no fewer than three great merchant ships, besides two big galleys and twelve sturdy smaller ones. I'll guarantee her these, and twice as much as whatever you offer next."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Nay, I have offer'd all; I have no more;","And she can have no more than all I have;","If you like me, she shall have me and mine."],"id":427,"m":"No, I've offered everything. I've nothing more. And she can't have more than everything I have. If you like me, she'll have me and all that's mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Why, then the maid is mine from all the world,","By your firm promise; Gremio is out-vied."],"id":428,"m":"Why, then the girl is mine above everyone in the world, by your firm promise. Gremio is outbid."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["I must confess your offer is the best;","And let your father make her the assurance,","She is your own; else, you must pardon me;","If you should die before him, where's her dower?"],"id":429,"m":"I must admit your offer is the best. And if your father guarantees it to her, she's yours. Otherwise, you must forgive me: if you died before him, where would her settlement be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["That's but a cavil; he is old, I young."],"id":430,"m":"That's just a quibble. He's old, and I'm young."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["And may not young men die as well as old?"],"id":431,"m":"And can't young men die as well as old ones?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Well, gentlemen,","I am thus resolv'd. On Sunday next, you know,","My daughter Katherine is to be married;","Now, on the Sunday following, shall Bianca","Be bride to you, if you make this assurance;","If not, to Signior Gremio.","And so I take my leave, and thank you both."],"id":432,"m":"Well, gentlemen, this is what I've decided. Next Sunday, as you know, my daughter Katherine is to be married. Now, on the following Sunday, Bianca will be your bride, if you make this guarantee. If not, she'll be Signior Gremio's. And so I'll say goodbye, and thank you both."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Adieu, good neighbour."],"id":433,"m":"Goodbye, good neighbour."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Baptista.","id":434},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","cont":1,"lines":["Now, I fear thee not:","Sirrah young gamester, your father were a fool","To give thee all, and in his waning age","Set foot under thy table. Tut! a toy!","An old Italian fox is not so kind, my boy."],"id":435,"m":"Now I'm not afraid of you. You young gambler, your father would be a fool to give you everything and then, in his old age, live off your charity. Nonsense! A joke! An old Italian fox isn't so generous, my boy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":436},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["A vengeance on your crafty wither'd hide!","Yet I have fac'd it with a card of ten.","'Tis in my head to do my master good:","I see no reason but suppos'd Lucentio","Must get a father, call'd suppos'd Vincentio;","And that's a wonder: fathers commonly","Do get their children; but in this case of wooing","A child shall get a sire, if I fail not of my cunning."],"id":437,"m":"Curse your sly, wrinkled skin! Still, I've bluffed it out with a ten. I've got an idea to help my master. I see no other way: the supposed Lucentio must produce a father, called the supposed Vincentio. And that's a wonder: usually fathers produce their children, but in this case of wooing, a child will produce a father, if my cunning doesn't fail me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":438},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"I","loc":"Padua. A room in Baptista's house.","id":439},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Lucentio, Hortensio and Bianca.","id":440},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Fiddler, forbear; you grow too forward, sir.","Have you so soon forgot the entertainment","Her sister Katherine welcome'd you withal?"],"id":441,"m":"Fiddler, stop it. You're getting too pushy, sir. Have you forgotten so soon the welcome her sister Katherine gave you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["But, wrangling pedant, this is","The patroness of heavenly harmony:","Then give me leave to have prerogative;","And when in music we have spent an hour,","Your lecture shall have leisure for as much."],"id":442,"m":"But, you quarrelsome schoolteacher, this lady is the patron of heavenly harmony. So let me go first, and when we've spent an hour on music, your lesson can have the same amount of time."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Preposterous ass, that never read so far","To know the cause why music was ordain'd!","Was it not to refresh the mind of man","After his studies or his usual pain?","Then give me leave to read philosophy,","And while I pause serve in your harmony."],"id":443,"m":"You absurd ass, you never read enough to know why music was created! Wasn't it to refresh the human mind after study or ordinary work? So let me teach philosophy, and when I stop, you can serve up your harmony."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Sirrah, I will not bear these braves of thine."],"id":444,"m":"You, I won't put up with your insults."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Why, gentlemen, you do me double wrong,","To strive for that which resteth in my choice.","I am no breeching scholar in the schools,","I'll not be tied to hours nor 'pointed times,","But learn my lessons as I please myself.","And, to cut off all strife, here sit we down;","Take you your instrument, play you the whiles;","His lecture will be done ere you have tun'd."],"id":445,"m":"Why, gentlemen, you do me a double wrong in fighting over something that's for me to choose. I'm not a schoolboy who can be whipped. I won't be tied to timetables and set hours; I'll learn my lessons as I please. And to end all this argument, let's sit down here. You take your instrument and play meanwhile. His lesson will be over before you've tuned up."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["You'll leave his lecture when I am in tune?"],"id":446,"m":"You'll stop his lesson when I'm in tune?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Retires.","id":447},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["That will be never: tune your instrument."],"id":448,"m":"That will be never. Tune your instrument."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Where left we last?"],"id":449,"m":"Where did we stop last time?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Here, madam:—","Hic ibat Simois; hic est Sigeia tellus;","Hic steterat Priami regia celsa senis."],"id":450,"m":"Here, madam: \"Here flowed the river Simois; here is the land of Sigeia; here stood the lofty palace of old Priam.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Construe them."],"id":451,"m":"Translate it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Hic ibat, as I told you before, Simois, I am Lucentio, hic est,","son unto Vincentio of Pisa, Sigeia tellus, disguised thus to get your","love, Hic steterat, and that Lucentio that comes a-wooing, Priami,","is my man Tranio, regia, bearing my port, celsa senis, that we","might beguile the old pantaloon."],"id":452,"m":"\"Hic ibat\", as I told you before, \"Simois\", I am Lucentio, \"hic est\", son of Vincentio of Pisa, \"Sigeia tellus\", disguised like this to win your love, \"Hic steterat\", and that Lucentio who comes wooing, \"Priami\", is my servant Tranio, \"regia\", playing my part, \"celsa senis\", so that we could fool the old dotard."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["[Returning]","Madam, my instrument's in tune."],"id":453,"m":"Madam, my instrument's in tune."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Let's hear.—"],"id":454,"m":"Let's hear it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Hortensio plays.","id":455},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","cont":1,"lines":["O fie! the treble jars."],"id":456,"m":"Oh dear! The top string is out of tune."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Spit in the hole, man, and tune again."],"id":457,"m":"Spit in the peg-hole, man, and tune it again."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Now let me see if I can construe it: Hic ibat Simois, I know you not;","hic est Sigeia tellus, I trust you not; Hic steterat Priami, take","heed he hear us not; regia, presume not; celsa senis, despair not."],"id":458,"m":"Now let me see if I can translate it. \"Hic ibat Simois\", I don't know you; \"hic est Sigeia tellus\", I don't trust you; \"Hic steterat Priami\", be careful he doesn't hear us; \"regia\", don't presume; \"celsa senis\", don't despair."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Madam, 'tis now in tune."],"id":459,"m":"Madam, it's in tune now."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["All but the base."],"id":460,"m":"All except the bass."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["The base is right; 'tis the base knave that jars.","[Aside] How fiery and forward our pedant is!","Now, for my life, the knave doth court my love:","Pedascule, I'll watch you better yet."],"id":461,"m":"The bass is right. It's the base rascal that's out of tune. How fiery and pushy our schoolteacher is! Now, on my life, the scoundrel is courting my love. Little pedant, I'll watch you more closely yet."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["In time I may believe, yet I mistrust."],"id":462,"m":"In time I may believe it, but for now I don't trust it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Mistrust it not; for sure, Æacides","Was Ajax, call'd so from his grandfather."],"id":463,"m":"Don't doubt it, because Aeacides was certainly Ajax, named after his grandfather."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["I must believe my master; else, I promise you,","I should be arguing still upon that doubt;","But let it rest. Now, Licio, to you.","Good master, take it not unkindly, pray,","That I have been thus pleasant with you both."],"id":464,"m":"I must believe my teacher. Otherwise, I promise you, I'd still be arguing about that question. But let it be. Now, Licio, your turn. Good teachers, please don't be offended that I've been so playful with you both."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["[To Lucentio] You may go walk and give me leave a while;","My lessons make no music in three parts."],"id":465,"m":"You can go for a walk and leave me alone for a while. My lessons aren't music for three voices."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Are you so formal, sir? Well, I must wait,","[Aside] And watch withal; for, but I be deceiv'd,","Our fine musician groweth amorous."],"id":466,"m":"Are you so particular, sir? Well, I must wait, and keep watch too, because unless I'm mistaken, our fine musician is falling in love."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Madam, before you touch the instrument,","To learn the order of my fingering,","I must begin with rudiments of art;","To teach you gamut in a briefer sort,","More pleasant, pithy, and effectual,","Than hath been taught by any of my trade:","And there it is in writing, fairly drawn."],"id":467,"m":"Madam, before you touch the instrument and learn how I place my fingers, I must start with the basics of the art, to teach you the scale in a shorter way, more pleasant, concise and effective than anyone in my trade has taught it. And there it is, written out neatly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Why, I am past my gamut long ago."],"id":468,"m":"Why, I left the scale behind long ago."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Yet read the gamut of Hortensio."],"id":469,"m":"Still, read Hortensio's scale."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["[Reads] Gamut I am, the ground of all accord,","A re, to plead Hortensio's passion;","B mi, Bianca, take him for thy lord,","C fa ut, that loves with all affection:","D sol re, one clef, two notes have I","E la mi, show pity or I die.","Call you this gamut? Tut, I like it not:","Old fashions please me best; I am not so nice,","To change true rules for odd inventions."],"id":470,"m":"\"Gamut, I am the foundation of all harmony. A re: to plead Hortensio's passion. B mi: Bianca, take him for your husband. C fa ut: who loves you with all his heart. D sol re: one clef, two notes I have. E la mi: show pity or I'll die.\" You call this a scale? Nonsense, I don't like it. The old ways please me best. I'm not so silly as to swap true rules for strange new inventions."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Servant.","id":471},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANT","lines":["Mistress, your father prays you leave your books,","And help to dress your sister's chamber up:","You know tomorrow is the wedding-day."],"id":472,"m":"Mistress, your father asks you to leave your books and help to decorate your sister's room. You know tomorrow is the wedding day."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Farewell, sweet masters, both: I must be gone."],"id":473,"m":"Goodbye, dear teachers, both of you. I must go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bianca and Servant.","id":474},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Faith, mistress, then I have no cause to stay."],"id":475,"m":"Well, mistress, then I have no reason to stay."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":476},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["But I have cause to pry into this pedant:","Methinks he looks as though he were in love.","Yet if thy thoughts, Bianca, be so humble","To cast thy wand'ring eyes on every stale,","Seize thee that list: if once I find thee ranging,","Hortensio will be quit with thee by changing."],"id":477,"m":"But I have reason to look into this schoolteacher. I think he looks as if he's in love. Still, Bianca, if your thoughts are so low that you cast your wandering eyes on any decoy, let whoever wants you have you. If I ever find you straying, Hortensio will get even with you by changing to someone else."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":478},{"t":"scene","act":3,"scene":"II","loc":"Padua. Before Baptista's house.","id":479},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Baptista, Gremio, Tranio, Katherina, Bianca, Lucentio and Attendants.","id":480},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["[To Tranio]","Signior Lucentio, this is the 'pointed day","That Katherine and Petruchio should be married,","And yet we hear not of our son-in-law.","What will be said? What mockery will it be","To want the bridegroom when the priest attends","To speak the ceremonial rites of marriage!","What says Lucentio to this shame of ours?"],"id":481,"m":"Signior Lucentio, this is the day appointed for Katherine and Petruchio's wedding, and yet we've heard nothing from our son-in-law. What will people say? What a mockery it will be to have no bridegroom when the priest is ready to perform the marriage ceremony! What does Lucentio say to this shame of ours?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["No shame but mine; I must, forsooth, be forc'd","To give my hand, oppos'd against my heart,","Unto a mad-brain rudesby, full of spleen;","Who woo'd in haste and means to wed at leisure.","I told you, I, he was a frantic fool,","Hiding his bitter jests in blunt behaviour;","And to be noted for a merry man,","He'll woo a thousand, 'point the day of marriage,","Make friends, invite, and proclaim the banns;","Yet never means to wed where he hath woo'd.","Now must the world point at poor Katherine,","And say 'Lo! there is mad Petruchio's wife,","If it would please him come and marry her.'"],"id":482,"m":"The shame is mine alone. I must, indeed, be forced to give my hand against my heart's wishes to a mad, rude, hot-tempered man who wooed in a hurry and means to marry at his leisure. I told you, I did, that he was a raving fool, hiding his bitter jokes behind rough manners. And to be known as a merry fellow, he'll woo a thousand women, set the wedding day, make friends, invite guests and announce the banns, and yet never mean to marry the woman he's wooed. Now the world will point at poor Katherine and say, \"Look, there's mad Petruchio's wife, if he'd only come and marry her.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Patience, good Katherine, and Baptista too.","Upon my life, Petruchio means but well,","Whatever fortune stays him from his word:","Though he be blunt, I know him passing wise;","Though he be merry, yet withal he's honest."],"id":483,"m":"Patience, good Katherine, and Baptista too. On my life, Petruchio means well, whatever accident is keeping him from his word. Though he's blunt, I know he's very wise; though he's merry, he's honest too."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Would Katherine had never seen him though!"],"id":484,"m":"I wish Katherine had never seen him!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit weeping, followed by Bianca and others.","id":485},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Go, girl, I cannot blame thee now to weep,","For such an injury would vex a very saint;","Much more a shrew of thy impatient humour."],"id":486,"m":"Go, girl. I can't blame you for crying now, because such an insult would annoy a saint, let alone a shrew with your impatient temper."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Biondello.","id":487},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Master, master! News! old news, and such news as you never heard of!"],"id":488,"m":"Master, master! News! Old news, and news like you've never heard!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Is it new and old too? How may that be?"],"id":489,"m":"New and old at once? How can that be?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Why, is it not news to hear of Petruchio's coming?"],"id":490,"m":"Why, isn't it news to hear that Petruchio is coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Is he come?"],"id":491,"m":"Has he come?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Why, no, sir."],"id":492,"m":"Why, no, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["What then?"],"id":493,"m":"What, then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["He is coming."],"id":494,"m":"He's coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["When will he be here?"],"id":495,"m":"When will he be here?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["When he stands where I am and sees you there."],"id":496,"m":"When he's standing where I am and sees you there."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["But say, what to thine old news?"],"id":497,"m":"But tell us, what about your old news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Why, Petruchio is coming, in a new hat and an old jerkin; a pair of old","breeches thrice turned; a pair of boots that have been candle-cases,","one buckled, another laced; an old rusty sword ta'en out of the town","armoury, with a broken hilt, and chapeless; with two broken points: his","horse hipped with an old mothy saddle and stirrups of no kindred;","besides, possessed with the glanders and like to mose in the chine;","troubled with the lampass, infected with the fashions, full of","windgalls, sped with spavins, rayed with the yellows, past cure of the","fives, stark spoiled with the staggers, begnawn with the bots, swayed","in the back and shoulder-shotten; near-legged before, and with a","half-checked bit, and a head-stall of sheep's leather, which, being","restrained to keep him from stumbling, hath been often burst, and now","repaired with knots; one girth six times pieced, and a woman's crupper","of velure, which hath two letters for her name fairly set down in","studs, and here and there pieced with pack-thread."],"id":498,"m":"Why, Petruchio is coming in a new hat and an old jacket; a pair of old breeches turned inside out three times; a pair of boots that have been used to keep candle-ends in, one buckled, the other laced; an old rusty sword taken from the town armoury, with a broken handle and no tip to its sheath, and two broken laces. His horse is lame in the hip, with an old moth-eaten saddle and stirrups that don't match. What's more, it has glanders and is likely to have a rotting spine; it's troubled with swollen gums, infected with farcy, full of swollen leg joints, ruined by spavin, discoloured with jaundice, past any cure for the vives, completely wrecked by staggers, gnawed by worms, sway-backed, with a dislocated shoulder and knock-kneed in front. It has a half-checked bit and a head-strap of sheep's leather which, having been pulled tight to stop the horse stumbling, has often broken and is now mended with knots; one belly-strap has been patched six times, and it has a woman's velvet crupper, with her two initials neatly set out in studs, and patched here and there with string."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Who comes with him?"],"id":499,"m":"Who's coming with him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["O, sir! his lackey, for all the world caparisoned like the horse; with","a linen stock on one leg and a kersey boot-hose on the other, gartered","with a red and blue list; an old hat, and the humour of forty fancies","prick'd in't for a feather: a monster, a very monster in apparel, and","not like a Christian footboy or a gentleman's lackey."],"id":500,"m":"Oh, sir, his servant, decked out exactly like the horse: a linen stocking on one leg and a coarse woollen sock on the other, tied with red and blue strips of cloth; an old hat with some crazy decoration stuck in it for a feather. A monster, a real monster in his clothes, not like any Christian page or a gentleman's servant."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["'Tis some odd humour pricks him to this fashion;","Yet oftentimes he goes but mean-apparell'd."],"id":501,"m":"It's some strange whim that drives him to dress like this. Still, he often goes about in plain clothes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["I am glad he's come, howsoe'er he comes."],"id":502,"m":"I'm glad he's come, however he comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Why, sir, he comes not."],"id":503,"m":"Why, sir, he isn't coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Didst thou not say he comes?"],"id":504,"m":"Didn't you say he's coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Who? that Petruchio came?"],"id":505,"m":"Who? That Petruchio was coming?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Ay, that Petruchio came."],"id":506,"m":"Yes, that Petruchio was coming."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["No, sir; I say his horse comes, with him on his back."],"id":507,"m":"No, sir. I said his horse is coming, with him on its back."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Why, that's all one."],"id":508,"m":"Why, that's the same thing."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["~Nay, by Saint Jamy,","~I hold you a penny,","~A horse and a man","~Is more than one,","~And yet not many."],"id":509,"m":"No, by Saint James, I'll bet you a penny, a horse and a man are more than one, and yet not many."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Petruchio and Grumio.","id":510},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Come, where be these gallants? Who is at home?"],"id":511,"m":"Come on, where are these fine gentlemen? Who's at home?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["You are welcome, sir."],"id":512,"m":"You're welcome, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["And yet I come not well."],"id":513,"m":"And yet I don't come well."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["And yet you halt not."],"id":514,"m":"And yet you're not limping."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Not so well apparell'd as I wish you were."],"id":515,"m":"Not as well dressed as I'd like you to be."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Were it better, I should rush in thus.","But where is Kate? Where is my lovely bride?","How does my father? Gentles, methinks you frown;","And wherefore gaze this goodly company,","As if they saw some wondrous monument,","Some comet or unusual prodigy?"],"id":516,"m":"Even if I were better dressed, I'd rush in like this. But where's Kate? Where's my lovely bride? How is my father? Gentlemen, you seem to be frowning. And why is this fine company staring as if they'd seen some amazing omen, some comet or strange portent?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Why, sir, you know this is your wedding-day:","First were we sad, fearing you would not come;","Now sadder, that you come so unprovided.","Fie! doff this habit, shame to your estate,","An eye-sore to our solemn festival."],"id":517,"m":"Why, sir, you know this is your wedding day. First we were sad, afraid you wouldn't come; now we're sadder that you've come so badly prepared. Shame on you! Take off these clothes. They're a disgrace to your position and an eyesore at our solemn celebration."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["And tell us what occasion of import","Hath all so long detain'd you from your wife,","And sent you hither so unlike yourself?"],"id":518,"m":"And tell us what important business has kept you so long from your wife, and sent you here so unlike yourself."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Tedious it were to tell, and harsh to hear;","Sufficeth I am come to keep my word,","Though in some part enforced to digress;","Which at more leisure I will so excuse","As you shall well be satisfied withal.","But where is Kate? I stay too long from her;","The morning wears, 'tis time we were at church."],"id":519,"m":"It would be tedious to tell and unpleasant to hear. It's enough that I've come to keep my word, though I was forced to turn aside for a while. I'll explain it later in a way that will satisfy you. But where's Kate? I've been away from her too long. The morning's passing; it's time we were at church."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["See not your bride in these unreverent robes;","Go to my chamber, put on clothes of mine."],"id":520,"m":"Don't see your bride in these disrespectful clothes. Go to my room and put on some of mine."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Not I, believe me: thus I'll visit her."],"id":521,"m":"Not me, believe me. I'll visit her like this."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["But thus, I trust, you will not marry her."],"id":522,"m":"But you won't marry her like this, I hope."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Good sooth, even thus; therefore ha' done with words;","To me she's married, not unto my clothes.","Could I repair what she will wear in me","As I can change these poor accoutrements,","'Twere well for Kate and better for myself.","But what a fool am I to chat with you","When I should bid good morrow to my bride,","And seal the title with a lovely kiss!"],"id":523,"m":"Indeed I will, just like this. So stop talking. She's marrying me, not my clothes. If I could repair what she'll wear out in me as easily as I can change these poor outfits, it would be good for Kate and better for me. But what a fool I am to chat with you when I should be saying good morning to my bride and sealing my claim with a lovely kiss!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Petruchio, Grumio and Biondello.","id":524},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["He hath some meaning in his mad attire.","We will persuade him, be it possible,","To put on better ere he go to church."],"id":525,"m":"There's some meaning behind his mad clothes. We'll persuade him, if we can, to put on something better before he goes to church."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["I'll after him and see the event of this."],"id":526,"m":"I'll follow him and see how this turns out."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Baptista, Gremio and Attendants.","id":527},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["But, sir, to love concerneth us to add","Her father's liking; which to bring to pass,","As I before imparted to your worship,","I am to get a man,—whate'er he be","It skills not much; we'll fit him to our turn,—","And he shall be Vincentio of Pisa,","And make assurance here in Padua,","Of greater sums than I have promised.","So shall you quietly enjoy your hope,","And marry sweet Bianca with consent."],"id":528,"m":"But, sir, to her love we need to add her father's approval. To bring that about, as I told your worship before, I'm going to find a man (it doesn't matter much who he is; we'll train him for our purpose), and he'll pretend to be Vincentio of Pisa and guarantee here in Padua even bigger sums than I've promised. That way you'll calmly get what you hope for and marry sweet Bianca with her father's consent."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Were it not that my fellow schoolmaster","Doth watch Bianca's steps so narrowly,","'Twere good, methinks, to steal our marriage;","Which once perform'd, let all the world say no,","I'll keep mine own despite of all the world."],"id":529,"m":"If it weren't that my fellow schoolteacher watches Bianca's every step so closely, I think it would be a good idea to marry her in secret. Once it's done, let the whole world say no, I'll keep what's mine in spite of the whole world."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["That by degrees we mean to look into,","And watch our vantage in this business.","We'll over-reach the greybeard, Gremio,","The narrow-prying father, Minola,","The quaint musician, amorous Licio;","All for my master's sake, Lucentio."],"id":530,"m":"We'll look into that step by step and watch for our chance in this business. We'll outwit the greybeard Gremio, the nosy father Minola and the crafty musician, lovesick Licio, all for my master Lucentio's sake."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Gremio.","id":531},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","cont":1,"lines":["Signior Gremio, came you from the church?"],"id":532,"m":"Signior Gremio, have you come from the church?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["As willingly as e'er I came from school."],"id":533,"m":"As gladly as I ever came out of school."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["And is the bride and bridegroom coming home?"],"id":534,"m":"And are the bride and bridegroom coming home?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["A bridegroom, say you? 'Tis a groom indeed,","A grumbling groom, and that the girl shall find."],"id":535,"m":"A bridegroom, you say? He's a stable-groom, more like, a grumbling groom, and the girl will find that out."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Curster than she? Why, 'tis impossible."],"id":536,"m":"Worse-tempered than her? Why, that's impossible."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Why, he's a devil, a devil, a very fiend."],"id":537,"m":"Why, he's a devil, a devil, a real fiend."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Why, she's a devil, a devil, the devil's dam."],"id":538,"m":"Why, she's a devil, a devil, the devil's mother."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Tut! she's a lamb, a dove, a fool, to him.","I'll tell you, Sir Lucentio: when the priest","Should ask if Katherine should be his wife,","'Ay, by gogs-wouns' quoth he, and swore so loud","That, all amaz'd, the priest let fall the book;","And as he stoop'd again to take it up,","The mad-brain'd bridegroom took him such a cuff","That down fell priest and book, and book and priest:","'Now take them up,' quoth he 'if any list.'"],"id":539,"m":"Nonsense! She's a lamb, a dove, a fool compared with him. I'll tell you, Signior Lucentio: when the priest asked whether Katherine should be his wife, \"Yes, by God's wounds!\" he said, and swore so loud that the priest, astonished, dropped the book. And as he bent down to pick it up, the mad bridegroom gave him such a cuff that down fell priest and book, book and priest. \"Now pick them up,\" he said, \"if anyone wants to.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["What said the wench, when he rose again?"],"id":540,"m":"What did the girl say when he got up again?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Trembled and shook, for why, he stamp'd and swore","As if the vicar meant to cozen him.","But after many ceremonies done,","He calls for wine: 'A health!' quoth he, as if","He had been abroad, carousing to his mates","After a storm; quaff'd off the muscadel,","And threw the sops all in the sexton's face,","Having no other reason","But that his beard grew thin and hungerly","And seem'd to ask him sops as he was drinking.","This done, he took the bride about the neck,","And kiss'd her lips with such a clamorous smack","That at the parting all the church did echo.","And I, seeing this, came thence for very shame;","And after me, I know, the rout is coming.","Such a mad marriage never was before.","Hark, hark! I hear the minstrels play."],"id":541,"m":"She trembled and shook, because he stamped and swore as if the vicar meant to cheat him. But after all the ceremonies were done, he called for wine. \"A toast!\" he said, as if he'd been on board ship drinking with his shipmates after a storm. He gulped down the sweet wine and threw the bread soaked in it right in the sexton's face, for no other reason than that the sexton's beard grew thin and hungry-looking and seemed to be begging him for the bread while he was drinking. That done, he took the bride round the neck and kissed her lips with such a loud smack that the whole church echoed as they parted. And when I saw this, I left out of sheer embarrassment. And after me, I know, the crowd is coming. There's never been such a mad wedding. Listen, listen! I can hear the musicians playing."},{"t":"sd","text":"Music plays.","id":542},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Petruchio, Katherina, Bianca, Baptista, Hortensio, Grumio and Train.","id":543},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Gentlemen and friends, I thank you for your pains:","I know you think to dine with me today,","And have prepar'd great store of wedding cheer","But so it is, my haste doth call me hence,","And therefore here I mean to take my leave."],"id":544,"m":"Gentlemen and friends, thank you for your trouble. I know you expect to dine with me today, and have prepared plenty of wedding food. But the fact is, my urgent business calls me away, and so I mean to say goodbye here."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Is't possible you will away tonight?"],"id":545,"m":"Is it possible you'll leave tonight?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I must away today before night come.","Make it no wonder: if you knew my business,","You would entreat me rather go than stay.","And, honest company, I thank you all,","That have beheld me give away myself","To this most patient, sweet, and virtuous wife.","Dine with my father, drink a health to me.","For I must hence; and farewell to you all."],"id":546,"m":"I must leave today, before night comes. Don't be surprised. If you knew my business, you'd beg me to go rather than stay. And, good company, thank you all for watching me give myself away to this most patient, sweet and virtuous wife. Dine with my father-in-law and drink a toast to me, because I must go. And goodbye to you all."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Let us entreat you stay till after dinner."],"id":547,"m":"Let us beg you to stay until after dinner."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["It may not be."],"id":548,"m":"It can't be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Let me entreat you."],"id":549,"m":"Let me beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["It cannot be."],"id":550,"m":"It can't be done."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Let me entreat you."],"id":551,"m":"Let me beg you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I am content."],"id":552,"m":"I'm content."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Are you content to stay?"],"id":553,"m":"Content to stay?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I am content you shall entreat me stay;","But yet not stay, entreat me how you can."],"id":554,"m":"I'm content for you to beg me to stay. But I won't stay, however you beg me."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Now, if you love me, stay."],"id":555,"m":"Now, if you love me, stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Grumio, my horse!"],"id":556,"m":"Grumio, my horse!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Ay, sir, they be ready; the oats have eaten the horses."],"id":557,"m":"Yes, sir, they're ready. The oats have eaten the horses."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Nay, then,","Do what thou canst, I will not go today;","No, nor tomorrow, not till I please myself.","The door is open, sir; there lies your way;","You may be jogging whiles your boots are green;","For me, I'll not be gone till I please myself.","'Tis like you'll prove a jolly surly groom","That take it on you at the first so roundly."],"id":558,"m":"Well, then, do what you like, I won't go today. No, nor tomorrow, not until I please. The door's open, sir; there's your way out. You can be trotting off while your boots are still fresh. As for me, I won't go until I please. You'll likely turn out a fine, surly husband, since you take charge so bluntly from the start."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["O Kate! content thee: prithee be not angry."],"id":559,"m":"Oh, Kate! Calm down. Please don't be angry."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I will be angry: what hast thou to do?","Father, be quiet; he shall stay my leisure."],"id":560,"m":"I will be angry. What business is it of yours? Father, keep quiet. He'll stay until I'm ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Ay, marry, sir, now it begins to work."],"id":561,"m":"Yes, indeed, sir, now it's starting to work."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Gentlemen, forward to the bridal dinner:","I see a woman may be made a fool,","If she had not a spirit to resist."],"id":562,"m":"Gentlemen, go on in to the wedding dinner. I see a woman can be made a fool of, if she hasn't the spirit to resist."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["They shall go forward, Kate, at thy command.","Obey the bride, you that attend on her;","Go to the feast, revel and domineer,","Carouse full measure to her maidenhead,","Be mad and merry, or go hang yourselves:","But for my bonny Kate, she must with me.","Nay, look not big, nor stamp, nor stare, nor fret;","I will be master of what is mine own.","She is my goods, my chattels; she is my house,","My household stuff, my field, my barn,","My horse, my ox, my ass, my anything;","And here she stands, touch her whoever dare;","I'll bring mine action on the proudest he","That stops my way in Padua. Grumio,","Draw forth thy weapon; we are beset with thieves;","Rescue thy mistress, if thou be a man.","Fear not, sweet wench; they shall not touch thee, Kate;","I'll buckler thee against a million."],"id":563,"m":"They shall go forward, Kate, at your command. Obey the bride, all you who serve her. Go to the feast, celebrate and live it up, drink deep toasts to her virginity, be mad and merry, or go and hang yourselves. But as for my pretty Kate, she must come with me. No, don't look angry, or stamp, or stare, or fret. I'll be master of what's my own. She's my goods, my possessions. She's my house, my household furniture, my field, my barn, my horse, my ox, my donkey, my anything. And here she stands: let anyone touch her who dares. I'll take the law on the proudest man who blocks my way in Padua. Grumio, draw your weapon. We're surrounded by thieves. Rescue your mistress, if you're a man. Don't be afraid, sweet girl. They won't touch you, Kate. I'll shield you against a million."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Petruchio, Katherina and Grumio.","id":564},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Nay, let them go, a couple of quiet ones."],"id":565,"m":"No, let them go, a quiet pair."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Went they not quickly, I should die with laughing."],"id":566,"m":"If they hadn't gone so quickly, I'd have died laughing."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Of all mad matches, never was the like."],"id":567,"m":"Of all mad marriages, there's never been one like it."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Mistress, what's your opinion of your sister?"],"id":568,"m":"Mistress, what do you think of your sister?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["That, being mad herself, she's madly mated."],"id":569,"m":"That, being mad herself, she's madly matched."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["I warrant him, Petruchio is Kated."],"id":570,"m":"I'll bet Petruchio has been Kated."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Neighbours and friends, though bride and bridegroom wants","For to supply the places at the table,","You know there wants no junkets at the feast.","Lucentio, you shall supply the bridegroom's place;","And let Bianca take her sister's room."],"id":571,"m":"Neighbours and friends, though the bride and groom aren't here to fill their places at the table, you know there's no shortage of sweets at the feast. Lucentio, you take the bridegroom's place, and let Bianca take her sister's."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Shall sweet Bianca practise how to bride it?"],"id":572,"m":"Shall sweet Bianca practise being a bride?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["She shall, Lucentio. Come, gentlemen, let's go."],"id":573,"m":"She shall, Lucentio. Come, gentlemen, let's go."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":574},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"I","loc":"A hall in Petruchio's country house.","id":575},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Grumio.","id":576},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Fie, fie on all tired jades, on all mad masters, and all foul ways! Was","ever man so beaten? Was ever man so ray'd? Was ever man so weary? I am","sent before to make a fire, and they are coming after to warm them.","Now, were not I a little pot and soon hot, my very lips might freeze to","my teeth, my tongue to the roof of my mouth, my heart in my belly, ere","I should come by a fire to thaw me. But I with blowing the fire shall","warm myself; for, considering the weather, a taller man than I will","take cold. Holla, ho! Curtis!"],"id":577,"m":"Curse all worn-out nags, all mad masters and all filthy roads! Was any man ever so beaten? Was any man ever so covered in mud? Was any man ever so tired? I'm sent ahead to make a fire, and they're coming after to warm themselves. Now, if I weren't a little pot that heats up quickly, my lips might freeze to my teeth, my tongue to the roof of my mouth and my heart to my belly before I could get to a fire to thaw me. But I'll warm myself by blowing on the fire, because in this weather a taller man than me would catch cold. Hello there! Curtis!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Curtis.","id":578},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Who is that calls so coldly?"],"id":579,"m":"Who's that calling so coldly?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["A piece of ice: if thou doubt it, thou mayst slide from my shoulder to","my heel with no greater a run but my head and my neck. A fire, good","Curtis."],"id":580,"m":"A lump of ice. If you doubt it, you can slide from my shoulder to my heel with no bigger run-up than my head and neck. A fire, good Curtis."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Is my master and his wife coming, Grumio?"],"id":581,"m":"Are my master and his wife coming, Grumio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["O, ay! Curtis, ay; and therefore fire, fire; cast on no water."],"id":582,"m":"Oh yes, Curtis, yes. And so, fire, fire. Don't throw any water on it."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Is she so hot a shrew as she's reported?"],"id":583,"m":"Is she as hot-tempered a shrew as people say?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["She was, good Curtis, before this frost; but thou knowest winter tames","man, woman, and beast; for it hath tamed my old master, and my new","mistress, and myself, fellow Curtis."],"id":584,"m":"She was, good Curtis, before this frost. But you know winter tames man, woman and beast. It has tamed my old master, my new mistress and myself, friend Curtis."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Away, you three-inch fool! I am no beast."],"id":585,"m":"Get away, you three-inch fool! I'm no beast."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Am I but three inches? Why, thy horn is a foot; and so long am I at the","least. But wilt thou make a fire, or shall I complain on thee to our","mistress, whose hand,—she being now at hand,— thou shalt soon feel, to","thy cold comfort, for being slow in thy hot office?"],"id":586,"m":"Am I only three inches? Why, your cuckold's horn is a foot long, and I'm at least that long. But will you make a fire, or shall I complain about you to our mistress? You'll soon feel her hand, now she's close at hand, and it'll be cold comfort to you for being slow at your hot job."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["I prithee, good Grumio, tell me, how goes the world?"],"id":587,"m":"Please, good Grumio, tell me, how are things?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["A cold world, Curtis, in every office but thine; and therefore fire. Do","thy duty, and have thy duty, for my master and mistress are almost","frozen to death."],"id":588,"m":"It's a cold world, Curtis, in every job except yours, so: fire. Do your duty and get your due, because my master and mistress are almost frozen to death."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["There's fire ready; and therefore, good Grumio, the news."],"id":589,"m":"The fire's ready. So, good Grumio, the news."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Why, 'Jack boy! ho, boy!' and as much news as wilt thou."],"id":590,"m":"Why, \"Jack, boy! Ho, boy!\", as in the song, and as much news as you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Come, you are so full of cony-catching."],"id":591,"m":"Come on, you're so full of tricks."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Why, therefore, fire; for I have caught extreme cold. Where's the cook?","Is supper ready, the house trimmed, rushes strewed, cobwebs swept, the","servingmen in their new fustian, their white stockings, and every","officer his wedding-garment on? Be the Jacks fair within, the Jills","fair without, and carpets laid, and everything in order?"],"id":592,"m":"Why, then, fire, because I've caught a terrible cold. Where's the cook? Is supper ready, the house tidied, fresh rushes laid on the floor, cobwebs swept, the servants in their new work clothes and white stockings, and every officer of the house in his wedding outfit? Are the jacks clean inside and the jills clean outside, the tablecloths laid and everything in order?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["All ready; and therefore, I pray thee, news."],"id":593,"m":"All ready. So, please, the news."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["First, know my horse is tired; my master and mistress fallen out."],"id":594,"m":"First, you should know my horse is tired, and my master and mistress have fallen out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["How?"],"id":595,"m":"How?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Out of their saddles into the dirt; and thereby hangs a tale."],"id":596,"m":"Out of their saddles into the mud. And that's the start of a story."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Let's ha't, good Grumio."],"id":597,"m":"Let's have it, good Grumio."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Lend thine ear."],"id":598,"m":"Lend me your ear."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Here."],"id":599,"m":"Here."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["[Striking him] There."],"id":600,"m":"There."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["This 'tis to feel a tale, not to hear a tale."],"id":601,"m":"This is feeling a story, not hearing one."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["And therefore 'tis called a sensible tale; and this cuff was but to","knock at your ear and beseech listening. Now I begin: Imprimis, we","came down a foul hill, my master riding behind my mistress,—"],"id":602,"m":"And so it's called a story you can feel. And that cuff was only to knock on your ear and ask it to listen. Now I'll begin. Firstly, we came down a muddy hill, my master riding behind my mistress..."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Both of one horse?"],"id":603,"m":"Both on one horse?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["What's that to thee?"],"id":604,"m":"What's that to you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Why, a horse."],"id":605,"m":"Why, a horse."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Tell thou the tale: but hadst thou not crossed me, thou shouldst have","heard how her horse fell, and she under her horse; thou shouldst have","heard in how miry a place, how she was bemoiled; how he left her with","the horse upon her; how he beat me because her horse stumbled; how she","waded through the dirt to pluck him off me: how he swore; how she","prayed, that never prayed before; how I cried; how the horses ran away;","how her bridle was burst; how I lost my crupper; with many things of","worthy memory, which now shall die in oblivion, and thou return","unexperienced to thy grave."],"id":606,"m":"You tell the story, then. But if you hadn't interrupted me, you'd have heard how her horse fell, and she was under her horse; you'd have heard what a muddy place it was, how she got covered in mud, how he left her with the horse on top of her, how he beat me because her horse stumbled, how she waded through the mud to pull him off me, how he swore, how she prayed, who never prayed before, how I cried, how the horses ran away, how her bridle broke, how I lost my crupper, and many other things worth remembering, which will now die forgotten, and you'll go to your grave without knowing them."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["By this reckoning he is more shrew than she."],"id":607,"m":"By this account, he's more of a shrew than she is."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Ay; and that thou and the proudest of you all shall find when he comes","home. But what talk I of this? Call forth Nathaniel, Joseph, Nicholas,","Philip, Walter, Sugarsop, and the rest; let their heads be sleekly","combed, their blue coats brush'd and their garters of an indifferent","knit; let them curtsy with their left legs, and not presume to touch a","hair of my master's horse-tail till they kiss their hands. Are they all","ready?"],"id":608,"m":"Yes, and you and the proudest of you will find that out when he gets home. But why am I talking about this? Call out Nathaniel, Joseph, Nicholas, Philip, Walter, Sugarsop and the rest. Make sure their hair is combed smooth, their blue coats brushed and their garters matching. Have them bow with their left legs and not dare to touch a hair of my master's horse's tail until they've kissed their hands. Are they all ready?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["They are."],"id":609,"m":"They are."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Call them forth."],"id":610,"m":"Call them out."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Do you hear? ho! You must meet my master to countenance my mistress."],"id":611,"m":"Do you hear? Hey! You must go and meet my master to give my mistress a proper welcome."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Why, she hath a face of her own."],"id":612,"m":"Why, she has a face of her own."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["Who knows not that?"],"id":613,"m":"Who doesn't know that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Thou, it seems, that calls for company to countenance her."],"id":614,"m":"You, it seems, if you're calling for people to give her a face."},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["I call them forth to credit her."],"id":615,"m":"I'm calling them out to do her credit."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Why, she comes to borrow nothing of them."],"id":616,"m":"Why, she isn't coming to borrow anything from them."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter four or five Servants.","id":617},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Welcome home, Grumio!"],"id":618,"m":"Welcome home, Grumio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PHILIP","lines":["How now, Grumio!"],"id":619,"m":"Hello, Grumio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"JOSEPH","lines":["What, Grumio!"],"id":620,"m":"Well, Grumio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NICHOLAS","lines":["Fellow Grumio!"],"id":621,"m":"Friend Grumio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["How now, old lad!"],"id":622,"m":"Hello, old lad!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Welcome, you; how now, you; what, you; fellow, you; and thus much for","greeting. Now, my spruce companions, is all ready, and all things neat?"],"id":623,"m":"Welcome, you; hello, you; well, you; friend, you. And that's enough greetings. Now, my smart friends, is everything ready and everything neat?"},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["All things is ready. How near is our master?"],"id":624,"m":"Everything's ready. How close is our master?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["E'en at hand, alighted by this; and therefore be not,—","Cock's passion, silence! I hear my master."],"id":625,"m":"Almost here; he's dismounted by now. And so don't be... By God's passion, quiet! I can hear my master."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Petruchio and Katherina.","id":626},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Where be these knaves? What! no man at door","To hold my stirrup nor to take my horse?","Where is Nathaniel, Gregory, Philip?—"],"id":627,"m":"Where are these rascals? What, no man at the door to hold my stirrup or take my horse? Where are Nathaniel, Gregory, Philip?"},{"t":"sp","who":"SERVANTS","lines":["Here, here, sir; here, sir."],"id":628,"m":"Here, here, sir. Here, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Here, sir! here, sir! here, sir! here, sir!","You logger-headed and unpolish'd grooms!","What, no attendance? no regard? no duty?","Where is the foolish knave I sent before?"],"id":629,"m":"Here, sir! Here, sir! Here, sir! Here, sir! You blockheaded, uncouth stable-boys! What, no service? No respect? No duty? Where's the foolish rascal I sent ahead?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Here, sir; as foolish as I was before."],"id":630,"m":"Here, sir, as foolish as I was before."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["You peasant swain! you whoreson malt-horse drudge!","Did I not bid thee meet me in the park,","And bring along these rascal knaves with thee?"],"id":631,"m":"You peasant clod! You bastard, you cart-horse drudge! Didn't I tell you to meet me in the park and bring these rascals with you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Nathaniel's coat, sir, was not fully made,","And Gabriel's pumps were all unpink'd i' the heel;","There was no link to colour Peter's hat,","And Walter's dagger was not come from sheathing;","There was none fine but Adam, Ralph, and Gregory;","The rest were ragged, old, and beggarly;","Yet, as they are, here are they come to meet you."],"id":632,"m":"Nathaniel's coat, sir, wasn't finished, and Gabriel's shoes weren't decorated at the heel. There was no soot to blacken Peter's hat, and Walter's dagger hadn't come back from having its sheath made. None of them were smart except Adam, Ralph and Gregory. The rest were ragged, old and shabby. Still, such as they are, here they are to meet you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Go, rascals, go and fetch my supper in."],"id":633,"m":"Go, you rascals, go and bring my supper in."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt some of the Servants.","id":634},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["~Where is the life that late I led?","~Where are those—?","Sit down, Kate, and welcome.","Food, food, food, food!"],"id":635,"m":"\"Where is the life that I led lately? Where are those...?\" Sit down, Kate, and welcome. Food, food, food, food!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Servants with supper.","id":636},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["Why, when, I say?—Nay, good sweet Kate, be merry.—","Off with my boots, you rogues! you villains! when?","~It was the friar of orders grey,","~As he forth walked on his way:","Out, you rogue! you pluck my foot awry:"],"id":637,"m":"Why, when will it come, I say? No, good sweet Kate, be cheerful. Get my boots off, you rogues! You villains! When? \"It was the grey-robed friar, as he walked along his way.\" Get out, you rogue! You're twisting my foot."},{"t":"sd","text":"Strikes him.","id":638},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["Take that, and mend the plucking off the other.","Be merry, Kate. Some water, here; what, ho!","Where's my spaniel Troilus? Sirrah, get you hence","And bid my cousin Ferdinand come hither:"],"id":639,"m":"Take that, and do a better job pulling off the other one. Be cheerful, Kate. Some water here! Hey there! Where's my spaniel Troilus? You, get out of here and tell my cousin Ferdinand to come here."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Servant.","id":640},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["One, Kate, that you must kiss and be acquainted with.","Where are my slippers? Shall I have some water?","Come, Kate, and wash, and welcome heartily.—"],"id":641,"m":"One you must kiss and get to know, Kate. Where are my slippers? Will I get some water? Come, Kate, wash your hands, and a hearty welcome to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Servant lets the ewer fall. Petruchio strikes him.","id":642},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["You whoreson villain! will you let it fall?"],"id":643,"m":"You bastard villain! Will you drop it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Patience, I pray you; 'twas a fault unwilling."],"id":644,"m":"Patience, please. It was an accident."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A whoreson, beetle-headed, flap-ear'd knave!","Come, Kate, sit down; I know you have a stomach.","Will you give thanks, sweet Kate, or else shall I?—","What's this? Mutton?"],"id":645,"m":"A bastard, blockheaded, floppy-eared rascal! Come, Kate, sit down. I know you're hungry. Will you say grace, sweet Kate, or shall I? What's this? Mutton?"},{"t":"sp","who":"FIRST SERVANT","lines":["Ay."],"id":646,"m":"Yes."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Who brought it?"],"id":647,"m":"Who brought it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["I."],"id":648,"m":"I did."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["'Tis burnt; and so is all the meat.","What dogs are these! Where is the rascal cook?","How durst you, villains, bring it from the dresser,","And serve it thus to me that love it not?"],"id":649,"m":"It's burnt, and so is all the meat. What dogs these are! Where's that rascal of a cook? How dare you villains bring it from the kitchen and serve it like this to me, when I don't like it?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Throws the meat, etc., at them.","id":650},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["There, take it to you, trenchers, cups, and all.","You heedless joltheads and unmanner'd slaves!","What! do you grumble? I'll be with you straight."],"id":651,"m":"There, take it all back, plates, cups and everything. You thoughtless blockheads and ill-mannered slaves! What, are you muttering? I'll deal with you in a moment."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I pray you, husband, be not so disquiet;","The meat was well, if you were so contented."],"id":652,"m":"Please, husband, don't be so upset. The meat was fine, if you'd been happy with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I tell thee, Kate, 'twas burnt and dried away,","And I expressly am forbid to touch it;","For it engenders choler, planteth anger;","And better 'twere that both of us did fast,","Since, of ourselves, ourselves are choleric,","Than feed it with such over-roasted flesh.","Be patient; tomorrow 't shall be mended.","And for this night we'll fast for company:","Come, I will bring thee to thy bridal chamber."],"id":653,"m":"I tell you, Kate, it was burnt and dried up, and I'm strictly forbidden to touch it, because it produces bile and breeds anger. And it would be better for both of us to go hungry, since we're both naturally hot-tempered, than to feed that temper with overcooked meat. Be patient. Tomorrow it will be put right, and for tonight we'll both go without, to keep each other company. Come, I'll take you to your bridal chamber."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Petruchio, Katherina and Curtis.","id":654},{"t":"sp","who":"NATHANIEL","lines":["Peter, didst ever see the like?"],"id":655,"m":"Peter, did you ever see anything like it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETER","lines":["He kills her in her own humour."],"id":656,"m":"He's beating her at her own game."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Curtis.","id":657},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Where is he?"],"id":658,"m":"Where is he?"},{"t":"sp","who":"CURTIS","lines":["In her chamber, making a sermon of continency to her;","And rails, and swears, and rates, that she, poor soul,","Knows not which way to stand, to look, to speak,","And sits as one new risen from a dream.","Away, away! for he is coming hither."],"id":659,"m":"In her room, preaching her a sermon on self-control. And he rants and swears and scolds, so that she, poor soul, doesn't know which way to stand, to look, or to speak, and sits like someone just woken from a dream. Away, away! He's coming this way."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":660},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Petruchio.","id":661},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Thus have I politicly begun my reign,","And 'tis my hope to end successfully.","My falcon now is sharp and passing empty.","And till she stoop she must not be full-gorg'd,","For then she never looks upon her lure.","Another way I have to man my haggard,","To make her come, and know her keeper's call,","That is, to watch her, as we watch these kites","That bate and beat, and will not be obedient.","She eat no meat today, nor none shall eat;","Last night she slept not, nor tonight she shall not;","As with the meat, some undeserved fault","I'll find about the making of the bed;","And here I'll fling the pillow, there the bolster,","This way the coverlet, another way the sheets;","Ay, and amid this hurly I intend","That all is done in reverend care of her;","And, in conclusion, she shall watch all night:","And if she chance to nod I'll rail and brawl,","And with the clamour keep her still awake.","This is a way to kill a wife with kindness;","And thus I'll curb her mad and headstrong humour.","He that knows better how to tame a shrew,","Now let him speak; 'tis charity to show."],"id":662,"m":"So I've begun my reign with cunning, and I hope to end it successfully. My falcon is now keen and very hungry, and until she comes down to the lure she mustn't be fully fed, because then she never looks at the lure. I have another way to tame my wild hawk, to make her come and know her keeper's call: that is, to keep her awake, as we do with those hawks that flap and struggle and won't obey. She ate no food today, and she won't eat any. Last night she didn't sleep, and tonight she won't either. As with the meat, I'll find some made-up fault in the way the bed is made, and I'll throw the pillow here, the bolster there, the bedspread this way and the sheets that way. Yes, and in all this commotion I'll pretend it's all done out of loving care for her. In the end, she'll stay awake all night. And if she happens to nod off, I'll rant and shout and keep her awake with the noise. This is the way to kill a wife with kindness, and so I'll curb her mad and headstrong temper. If anyone knows a better way to tame a shrew, let him speak now. It would be a kindness to share it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":663},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"II","loc":"Padua. Before Baptista's house.","id":664},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Tranio and Hortensio.","id":665},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Is 't possible, friend Licio, that Mistress Bianca","Doth fancy any other but Lucentio?","I tell you, sir, she bears me fair in hand."],"id":666,"m":"Is it possible, friend Licio, that Mistress Bianca loves anyone but Lucentio? I tell you, sir, she's encouraging me."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Sir, to satisfy you in what I have said,","Stand by and mark the manner of his teaching."],"id":667,"m":"Sir, to prove what I've said, stand aside and watch how he teaches."},{"t":"sd","text":"They stand aside.","id":668},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Bianca and Lucentio.","id":669},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Now, mistress, profit you in what you read?"],"id":670,"m":"Now, mistress, are you getting anything out of what you're reading?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["What, master, read you? First resolve me that."],"id":671,"m":"What are you reading, master? Answer me that first."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I read that I profess, The Art to Love."],"id":672,"m":"I'm reading what I practise: The Art of Love."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["And may you prove, sir, master of your art!"],"id":673,"m":"And may you prove a master of your art, sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["While you, sweet dear, prove mistress of my heart."],"id":674,"m":"While you, sweet darling, prove the mistress of my heart."},{"t":"sd","text":"They retire.","id":675},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Quick proceeders, marry! Now tell me, I pray,","You that durst swear that your Mistress Bianca","Lov'd none in the world so well as Lucentio."],"id":676,"m":"Quick learners, indeed! Now tell me, please, you who dared to swear that your Mistress Bianca loved nobody in the world as much as Lucentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["O despiteful love! unconstant womankind!","I tell thee, Licio, this is wonderful."],"id":677,"m":"Oh, spiteful love! Fickle women! I tell you, Licio, this is astonishing."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Mistake no more; I am not Licio.","Nor a musician as I seem to be;","But one that scorn to live in this disguise","For such a one as leaves a gentleman","And makes a god of such a cullion:","Know, sir, that I am call'd Hortensio."],"id":678,"m":"Don't be mistaken any longer. I'm not Licio, or a musician as I seem to be, but someone who refuses to go on in this disguise for a woman who leaves a gentleman and makes a god of a low rascal like that. Know, sir, that my name is Hortensio."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Signior Hortensio, I have often heard","Of your entire affection to Bianca;","And since mine eyes are witness of her lightness,","I will with you, if you be so contented,","Forswear Bianca and her love for ever."],"id":679,"m":"Signior Hortensio, I've often heard of your complete devotion to Bianca. And since my own eyes have seen her flightiness, I'll join you, if you agree, in renouncing Bianca and her love for ever."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["See, how they kiss and court! Signior Lucentio,","Here is my hand, and here I firmly vow","Never to woo her more, but do forswear her,","As one unworthy all the former favours","That I have fondly flatter'd her withal."],"id":680,"m":"Look how they kiss and court! Signior Lucentio, here's my hand, and here I firmly swear never to woo her again, but to renounce her, as unworthy of all the favours I foolishly flattered her with before."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["And here I take the like unfeigned oath,","Never to marry with her though she would entreat;","Fie on her! See how beastly she doth court him!"],"id":681,"m":"And here I take the same sincere oath: never to marry her, even if she begged me. Shame on her! Look how shamelessly she courts him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Would all the world but he had quite forsworn!","For me, that I may surely keep mine oath,","I will be married to a wealthy widow","Ere three days pass, which hath as long lov'd me","As I have lov'd this proud disdainful haggard.","And so farewell, Signior Lucentio.","Kindness in women, not their beauteous looks,","Shall win my love; and so I take my leave,","In resolution as I swore before."],"id":682,"m":"I wish everyone in the world except him had given her up! As for me, to make sure I keep my oath, I'll marry a rich widow before three days are out, who has loved me as long as I've loved this proud, scornful wild hawk. And so goodbye, Signior Lucentio. Kindness in women, not their beautiful looks, will win my love. And so I'll say goodbye, as determined as I swore before."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Hortensio. Lucentio and Bianca advance.","id":683},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Mistress Bianca, bless you with such grace","As 'longeth to a lover's blessed case!","Nay, I have ta'en you napping, gentle love,","And have forsworn you with Hortensio."],"id":684,"m":"Mistress Bianca, may you be blessed with all the grace that belongs to a lover's happy state! Yes, I've caught you napping, sweet love, and have renounced you along with Hortensio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Tranio, you jest; but have you both forsworn me?"],"id":685,"m":"You're joking, Tranio. But have you both really renounced me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Mistress, we have."],"id":686,"m":"We have, mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Then we are rid of Licio."],"id":687,"m":"Then we're rid of Licio."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["I' faith, he'll have a lusty widow now,","That shall be woo'd and wedded in a day."],"id":688,"m":"Honestly, he'll have a lively widow now, who'll be wooed and married in a day."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["God give him joy!"],"id":689,"m":"God give him joy!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Ay, and he'll tame her."],"id":690,"m":"Yes, and he'll tame her."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["He says so, Tranio."],"id":691,"m":"So he says, Tranio."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Faith, he is gone unto the taming-school."],"id":692,"m":"Honestly, he's gone to the taming school."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["The taming-school! What, is there such a place?"],"id":693,"m":"The taming school! What, is there such a place?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Ay, mistress; and Petruchio is the master,","That teacheth tricks eleven and twenty long,","To tame a shrew and charm her chattering tongue."],"id":694,"m":"Yes, mistress, and Petruchio is the headmaster. He teaches every trick in the book to tame a shrew and silence her chattering tongue."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Biondello, running.","id":695},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["O master, master! I have watch'd so long","That I am dog-weary; but at last I spied","An ancient angel coming down the hill","Will serve the turn."],"id":696,"m":"Oh master, master! I've been watching so long that I'm dog-tired, but at last I've spotted an old fellow coming down the hill who'll do the job."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["What is he, Biondello?"],"id":697,"m":"What is he, Biondello?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Master, a mercatante or a pedant,","I know not what; but formal in apparel,","In gait and countenance surely like a father."],"id":698,"m":"Master, a merchant or a schoolmaster, I don't know which, but he's formally dressed, and in his walk and his face he certainly looks like a father."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["And what of him, Tranio?"],"id":699,"m":"And what about him, Tranio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["If he be credulous and trust my tale,","I'll make him glad to seem Vincentio,","And give assurance to Baptista Minola,","As if he were the right Vincentio.","Take in your love, and then let me alone."],"id":700,"m":"If he's gullible and believes my story, I'll make him glad to pretend to be Vincentio and give guarantees to Baptista Minola as if he were the real Vincentio. Take your love inside, and then leave it to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lucentio and Bianca.","id":701},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter a Pedant.","id":702},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["God save you, sir!"],"id":703,"m":"God save you, sir!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["And you, sir! you are welcome.","Travel you far on, or are you at the farthest?"],"id":704,"m":"And you, sir! You're welcome. Are you travelling far, or have you reached the end of your journey?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Sir, at the farthest for a week or two;","But then up farther, and as far as Rome;","And so to Tripoli, if God lend me life."],"id":705,"m":"Sir, this is as far as I go for a week or two, but then I go further, as far as Rome, and on to Tripoli, if God lets me live."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["What countryman, I pray?"],"id":706,"m":"Where are you from, may I ask?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Of Mantua."],"id":707,"m":"From Mantua."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Of Mantua, sir? Marry, God forbid,","And come to Padua, careless of your life!"],"id":708,"m":"From Mantua, sir? God forbid! And you've come to Padua, so careless of your life!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["My life, sir! How, I pray? for that goes hard."],"id":709,"m":"My life, sir! How, please? That sounds serious."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["'Tis death for anyone in Mantua","To come to Padua. Know you not the cause?","Your ships are stay'd at Venice; and the Duke,—","For private quarrel 'twixt your Duke and him,—","Hath publish'd and proclaim'd it openly.","'Tis marvel, but that you are but newly come","You might have heard it else proclaim'd about."],"id":710,"m":"It's death for anyone from Mantua to come to Padua. Don't you know why? Your ships are being held at Venice, and the Duke, because of a private quarrel between your Duke and him, has publicly announced it. It's a wonder, though since you've only just arrived, you might not have heard it proclaimed around."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Alas, sir! it is worse for me than so;","For I have bills for money by exchange","From Florence, and must here deliver them."],"id":711,"m":"Oh dear, sir! It's worse for me than that, because I'm carrying bills of exchange from Florence that I must cash here."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Well, sir, to do you courtesy,","This will I do, and this I will advise you:","First, tell me, have you ever been at Pisa?"],"id":712,"m":"Well, sir, to do you a kindness, this is what I'll do, and this is my advice. First, tell me, have you ever been to Pisa?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Ay, sir, in Pisa have I often been,","Pisa renowned for grave citizens."],"id":713,"m":"Yes, sir, I've often been to Pisa, Pisa famous for its serious citizens."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Among them know you one Vincentio?"],"id":714,"m":"Do you know a man called Vincentio among them?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["I know him not, but I have heard of him,","A merchant of incomparable wealth."],"id":715,"m":"I don't know him, but I've heard of him: a merchant of unequalled wealth."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["He is my father, sir; and, sooth to say,","In countenance somewhat doth resemble you."],"id":716,"m":"He's my father, sir. And to tell the truth, he looks a little like you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["[Aside] As much as an apple doth an oyster, and all one."],"id":717,"m":"As much as an apple looks like an oyster, it's all the same."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["To save your life in this extremity,","This favour will I do you for his sake;","And think it not the worst of all your fortunes","That you are like to Sir Vincentio.","His name and credit shall you undertake,","And in my house you shall be friendly lodg'd;","Look that you take upon you as you should!","You understand me, sir; so shall you stay","Till you have done your business in the city.","If this be courtesy, sir, accept of it."],"id":718,"m":"To save your life in this emergency, I'll do you this favour for his sake, and you'll count it not the worst of your luck that you look like Signior Vincentio. You'll take on his name and reputation, and you'll be put up as a friend in my house. Make sure you play the part properly! You understand me, sir. You'll stay like that until you've done your business in the city. If this is a kindness, sir, accept it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["O, sir, I do; and will repute you ever","The patron of my life and liberty."],"id":719,"m":"Oh, sir, I do, and I'll always think of you as the saviour of my life and freedom."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Then go with me to make the matter good.","This, by the way, I let you understand:","My father is here look'd for every day","To pass assurance of a dower in marriage","'Twixt me and one Baptista's daughter here:","In all these circumstances I'll instruct you.","Go with me to clothe you as becomes you."],"id":720,"m":"Then come with me and we'll put it into action. And by the way, I should tell you: my father is expected here any day, to guarantee a marriage settlement between me and the daughter of a man called Baptista here. I'll teach you all the details. Come with me and we'll dress you as you should be."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":721},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"III","loc":"A room in Petruchio's house.","id":722},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Katherina and Grumio.","id":723},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["No, no, forsooth; I dare not for my life."],"id":724,"m":"No, no, really. I wouldn't dare, for my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["The more my wrong, the more his spite appears.","What, did he marry me to famish me?","Beggars that come unto my father's door","Upon entreaty have a present alms;","If not, elsewhere they meet with charity;","But I, who never knew how to entreat,","Nor never needed that I should entreat,","Am starv'd for meat, giddy for lack of sleep;","With oaths kept waking, and with brawling fed.","And that which spites me more than all these wants,","He does it under name of perfect love;","As who should say, if I should sleep or eat","'Twere deadly sickness, or else present death.","I prithee go and get me some repast;","I care not what, so it be wholesome food."],"id":725,"m":"The more I'm wronged, the more his spite shows. What, did he marry me to starve me? Beggars who come to my father's door get charity straight away when they ask for it; if not, they find charity somewhere else. But I, who never knew how to beg and never needed to, am starved of food, dizzy from lack of sleep, kept awake by swearing and fed on shouting. And what annoys me more than all I'm going without, he does it in the name of perfect love, as if to say that if I slept or ate it would make me deathly ill, or kill me at once. Please go and get me something to eat. I don't care what, as long as it's wholesome food."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["What say you to a neat's foot?"],"id":726,"m":"What do you say to a calf's foot?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["'Tis passing good; I prithee let me have it."],"id":727,"m":"That's very good. Please let me have it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I fear it is too choleric a meat.","How say you to a fat tripe finely broil'd?"],"id":728,"m":"I'm afraid it's too bile-making a food. What do you say to a fat tripe, nicely grilled?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I like it well; good Grumio, fetch it me."],"id":729,"m":"I'd like that. Good Grumio, fetch it for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I cannot tell; I fear 'tis choleric.","What say you to a piece of beef and mustard?"],"id":730,"m":"I don't know. I'm afraid it's bile-making. What do you say to a piece of beef and mustard?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["A dish that I do love to feed upon."],"id":731,"m":"A dish I love to eat."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Ay, but the mustard is too hot a little."],"id":732,"m":"Yes, but the mustard is a little too hot."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Why then the beef, and let the mustard rest."],"id":733,"m":"Why then, the beef, and forget the mustard."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Nay, then I will not: you shall have the mustard,","Or else you get no beef of Grumio."],"id":734,"m":"No, then I won't. You'll have the mustard, or you'll get no beef from Grumio."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Then both, or one, or anything thou wilt."],"id":735,"m":"Then both, or one, or anything you like."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Why then the mustard without the beef."],"id":736,"m":"Why then, the mustard without the beef."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Go, get thee gone, thou false deluding slave,"],"id":737,"m":"Go on, get out, you lying, cheating slave,"},{"t":"sd","text":"Beats him.","id":738},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","cont":1,"lines":["That feed'st me with the very name of meat.","Sorrow on thee and all the pack of you","That triumph thus upon my misery!","Go, get thee gone, I say."],"id":739,"m":"who feeds me on nothing but the names of food! Curse you and the whole pack of you who gloat like this over my misery! Go, get out, I tell you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Petruchio with a dish of meat; and Hortensio.","id":740},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["How fares my Kate? What, sweeting, all amort?"],"id":741,"m":"How is my Kate? What, sweetheart, so downcast?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Mistress, what cheer?"],"id":742,"m":"Mistress, how are you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Faith, as cold as can be."],"id":743,"m":"Honestly, as cold as can be."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Pluck up thy spirits; look cheerfully upon me.","Here, love; thou seest how diligent I am,","To dress thy meat myself, and bring it thee:"],"id":744,"m":"Cheer up. Look at me cheerfully. Here, love: you see how attentive I am, cooking your food myself and bringing it to you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Sets the dish on a table.","id":745},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["I am sure, sweet Kate, this kindness merits thanks.","What! not a word? Nay, then thou lov'st it not,","And all my pains is sorted to no proof.","Here, take away this dish."],"id":746,"m":"I'm sure, sweet Kate, this kindness deserves thanks. What, not a word? No, then you don't like it, and all my trouble has come to nothing. Here, take this dish away."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I pray you, let it stand."],"id":747,"m":"Please, let it stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["The poorest service is repaid with thanks;","And so shall mine, before you touch the meat."],"id":748,"m":"The smallest service is repaid with thanks, and so shall mine be, before you touch the food."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I thank you, sir."],"id":749,"m":"Thank you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Signior Petruchio, fie! you are to blame.","Come, Mistress Kate, I'll bear you company."],"id":750,"m":"Signior Petruchio, shame on you! You're in the wrong. Come, Mistress Kate, I'll keep you company."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["[Aside] Eat it up all, Hortensio, if thou lovest me.","Much good do it unto thy gentle heart!","Kate, eat apace: and now, my honey love,","Will we return unto thy father's house","And revel it as bravely as the best,","With silken coats and caps, and golden rings,","With ruffs and cuffs and farthingales and things;","With scarfs and fans and double change of bravery,","With amber bracelets, beads, and all this knavery.","What! hast thou din'd? The tailor stays thy leisure,","To deck thy body with his ruffling treasure."],"id":751,"m":"Eat it all up, Hortensio, if you love me. Much good may it do your kind heart! Kate, eat quickly. And now, my honey love, we'll go back to your father's house and celebrate as splendidly as anyone, with silk coats and caps and golden rings, with ruffs and cuffs and hooped petticoats and things, with scarves and fans and two changes of fine clothes, with amber bracelets, beads and all this frippery. What, have you finished eating? The tailor is waiting for you, to dress your body in his frilly treasures."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Tailor.","id":752},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["Come, tailor, let us see these ornaments;","Lay forth the gown.—"],"id":753,"m":"Come, tailor, let's see these decorations. Lay out the gown."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Haberdasher.","id":754},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["What news with you, sir?"],"id":755,"m":"What do you want, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HABERDASHER","lines":["Here is the cap your worship did bespeak."],"id":756,"m":"Here's the cap your worship ordered."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, this was moulded on a porringer;","A velvet dish: fie, fie! 'tis lewd and filthy:","Why, 'tis a cockle or a walnut-shell,","A knack, a toy, a trick, a baby's cap:","Away with it! come, let me have a bigger."],"id":757,"m":"Why, this was shaped on a soup bowl: a velvet dish. Ugh, ugh! It's vulgar and nasty. Why, it's a cockleshell or a walnut shell, a knick-knack, a toy, a trinket, a baby's cap. Take it away! Come on, give me a bigger one."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I'll have no bigger; this doth fit the time,","And gentlewomen wear such caps as these."],"id":758,"m":"I won't have a bigger one. This one is in fashion, and gentlewomen wear caps like these."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["When you are gentle, you shall have one too,","And not till then."],"id":759,"m":"When you're gentle, you'll have one too, and not till then."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["[Aside] That will not be in haste."],"id":760,"m":"That won't be any time soon."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Why, sir, I trust I may have leave to speak;","And speak I will. I am no child, no babe.","Your betters have endur'd me say my mind,","And if you cannot, best you stop your ears.","My tongue will tell the anger of my heart,","Or else my heart, concealing it, will break;","And rather than it shall, I will be free","Even to the uttermost, as I please, in words."],"id":761,"m":"Why, sir, I trust I'm allowed to speak, and speak I will. I'm no child, no baby. Better men than you have put up with me speaking my mind, and if you can't, you'd better block your ears. My tongue will express the anger in my heart, or else my heart will break from hiding it. And rather than let that happen, I'll be free to say exactly what I please, to the very limit."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, thou say'st true; it is a paltry cap,","A custard-coffin, a bauble, a silken pie;","I love thee well in that thou lik'st it not."],"id":762,"m":"Why, you're right. It's a paltry cap, a pie crust, a trinket, a silk pie. I love you all the more for not liking it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Love me or love me not, I like the cap;","And it I will have, or I will have none."],"id":763,"m":"Love me or don't love me, I like the cap, and I'll have it or none at all."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Haberdasher.","id":764},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Thy gown? Why, ay: come, tailor, let us see't.","O mercy, God! what masquing stuff is here?","What's this? A sleeve? 'Tis like a demi-cannon.","What, up and down, carv'd like an apple tart?","Here's snip and nip and cut and slish and slash,","Like to a censer in a barber's shop.","Why, what i' devil's name, tailor, call'st thou this?"],"id":765,"m":"Your gown? Why, yes. Come, tailor, let's see it. Oh God have mercy! What fancy-dress costume is this? What's this? A sleeve? It's like a cannon. What, carved up and down like an apple tart? Here's snipping and nipping and cutting and slitting and slashing, like an incense burner in a barber's shop. Why, what in the devil's name, tailor, do you call this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["[Aside] I see she's like to have neither cap nor gown."],"id":766,"m":"I can see she's likely to get neither cap nor gown."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["You bid me make it orderly and well,","According to the fashion and the time."],"id":767,"m":"You told me to make it properly and well, in the latest fashion."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Marry, and did; but if you be remember'd,","I did not bid you mar it to the time.","Go, hop me over every kennel home,","For you shall hop without my custom, sir.","I'll none of it: hence! make your best of it."],"id":768,"m":"Indeed I did. But if you remember, I didn't tell you to ruin it for the sake of fashion. Go on, hop home over every gutter, because you'll be hopping without my business, sir. I'll have none of it. Get out! Do what you like with it."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I never saw a better fashion'd gown,","More quaint, more pleasing, nor more commendable;","Belike you mean to make a puppet of me."],"id":769,"m":"I never saw a better-made gown, more elegant, more pleasing or more praiseworthy. I suppose you mean to make a puppet of me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, true; he means to make a puppet of thee."],"id":770,"m":"Why, that's right: he means to make a puppet of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["She says your worship means to make a puppet of her."],"id":771,"m":"She says your worship means to make a puppet of her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["O monstrous arrogance! Thou liest, thou thread,","Thou thimble,","Thou yard, three-quarters, half-yard, quarter, nail!","Thou flea, thou nit, thou winter-cricket thou!","Brav'd in mine own house with a skein of thread!","Away! thou rag, thou quantity, thou remnant,","Or I shall so be-mete thee with thy yard","As thou shalt think on prating whilst thou liv'st!","I tell thee, I, that thou hast marr'd her gown."],"id":772,"m":"Oh, outrageous arrogance! You liar, you thread, you thimble, you yard, three-quarters, half-yard, quarter, inch! You flea, you nit, you winter cricket! Insulted in my own house by a reel of thread! Get out, you rag, you scrap, you leftover, or I'll measure you with your own yardstick so that you'll think twice about chattering for the rest of your life! I tell you, I do, you've ruined her gown."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["Your worship is deceiv'd: the gown is made","Just as my master had direction.","Grumio gave order how it should be done."],"id":773,"m":"Your worship is mistaken. The gown is made exactly as my master was instructed. Grumio gave orders how it should be made."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I gave him no order; I gave him the stuff."],"id":774,"m":"I gave him no orders. I gave him the material."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["But how did you desire it should be made?"],"id":775,"m":"But how did you want it made?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Marry, sir, with needle and thread."],"id":776,"m":"Why, sir, with needle and thread."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["But did you not request to have it cut?"],"id":777,"m":"But didn't you ask to have it cut?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Thou hast faced many things."],"id":778,"m":"You've trimmed plenty of things."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["I have."],"id":779,"m":"I have."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Face not me. Thou hast braved many men; brave not me: I will neither be","fac'd nor brav'd. I say unto thee, I bid thy master cut out the gown;","but I did not bid him cut it to pieces: ergo, thou liest."],"id":780,"m":"Don't try to face me down. You've dressed up plenty of men; don't try to dress me down. I won't be faced or braved. I tell you, I told your master to cut out the gown, but I didn't tell him to cut it to pieces. Therefore, you're lying."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["Why, here is the note of the fashion to testify."],"id":781,"m":"Why, here's the written order to prove it."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Read it."],"id":782,"m":"Read it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["The note lies in 's throat, if he say I said so."],"id":783,"m":"The order is a downright liar if it says I said that."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["'Imprimis, a loose-bodied gown.'"],"id":784,"m":"\"First, a loose-fitting gown.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Master, if ever I said loose-bodied gown, sew me in the skirts of it","and beat me to death with a bottom of brown thread; I said, a gown."],"id":785,"m":"Master, if I ever said a loose gown, sew me up in its skirts and beat me to death with a reel of brown thread. I said a gown."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Proceed."],"id":786,"m":"Go on."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["'With a small compassed cape.'"],"id":787,"m":"\"With a small round cape.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I confess the cape."],"id":788,"m":"I admit the cape."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["'With a trunk sleeve.'"],"id":789,"m":"\"With a wide sleeve.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I confess two sleeves."],"id":790,"m":"I admit two sleeves."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["'The sleeves curiously cut.'"],"id":791,"m":"\"The sleeves elaborately cut.\""},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Ay, there's the villainy."],"id":792,"m":"Yes, that's the villainy."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Error i' the bill, sir; error i' the bill. I commanded the sleeves","should be cut out, and sew'd up again; and that I'll prove upon thee,","though thy little finger be armed in a thimble."],"id":793,"m":"A mistake in the order, sir, a mistake in the order. I ordered the sleeves to be cut out and sewn up again, and I'll prove it on you in a fight, even if your little finger is armed with a thimble."},{"t":"sp","who":"TAILOR","lines":["This is true that I say; and I had thee in place where thou shouldst","know it."],"id":794,"m":"What I say is true. And if I had you in the right place, you'd know it."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["I am for thee straight; take thou the bill, give me thy mete-yard, and","spare not me."],"id":795,"m":"I'm ready for you now. You take the order, give me your yardstick, and don't hold back."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["God-a-mercy, Grumio! Then he shall have no odds."],"id":796,"m":"God have mercy, Grumio! Then he'll have no chance."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Well, sir, in brief, the gown is not for me."],"id":797,"m":"Well, sir, in short, the gown is not for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["You are i' the right, sir; 'tis for my mistress."],"id":798,"m":"You're right, sir. It's for my mistress."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Go, take it up unto thy master's use."],"id":799,"m":"Go, take it away for your master to use."},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["Villain, not for thy life! Take up my mistress' gown for thy master's","use!"],"id":800,"m":"Villain, not for your life! Lift up my mistress's gown for your master to use!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, sir, what's your conceit in that?"],"id":801,"m":"Why, sir, what do you mean by that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GRUMIO","lines":["O, sir, the conceit is deeper than you think for.","Take up my mistress' gown to his master's use!","O fie, fie, fie!"],"id":802,"m":"Oh, sir, the meaning is deeper than you think. Lift up my mistress's gown for his master's use! Oh, shame, shame, shame!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["[Aside] Hortensio, say thou wilt see the tailor paid.","[To Tailor] Go take it hence; be gone, and say no more."],"id":803,"m":"Hortensio, say you'll see the tailor gets paid. Go, take it away. Clear off and say no more."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["[Aside to Tailor] Tailor, I'll pay thee for thy gown tomorrow;","Take no unkindness of his hasty words.","Away, I say! commend me to thy master."],"id":804,"m":"Tailor, I'll pay you for your gown tomorrow. Don't be offended by his hasty words. Go, I tell you. Give my regards to your master."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Tailor.","id":805},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Well, come, my Kate; we will unto your father's","Even in these honest mean habiliments.","Our purses shall be proud, our garments poor","For 'tis the mind that makes the body rich;","And as the sun breaks through the darkest clouds,","So honour peereth in the meanest habit.","What, is the jay more precious than the lark","Because his feathers are more beautiful?","Or is the adder better than the eel","Because his painted skin contents the eye?","O no, good Kate; neither art thou the worse","For this poor furniture and mean array.","If thou account'st it shame, lay it on me;","And therefore frolic; we will hence forthwith,","To feast and sport us at thy father's house.","Go call my men, and let us straight to him;","And bring our horses unto Long-lane end;","There will we mount, and thither walk on foot.","Let's see; I think 'tis now some seven o'clock,","And well we may come there by dinner-time."],"id":806,"m":"Well, come on, my Kate. We'll go to your father's in these honest, plain clothes. Our purses will be proud and our clothes poor, because it's the mind that makes the body rich. And as the sun breaks through the darkest clouds, so honour shines through the plainest clothes. What, is the jay more precious than the lark because its feathers are more beautiful? Or is the adder better than the eel because its patterned skin pleases the eye? Oh no, good Kate. You aren't any the worse for these poor clothes and plain outfit. If you think it shameful, blame me. So cheer up. We'll leave at once to feast and enjoy ourselves at your father's house. Go and call my men, and let's go to him straight away. Bring our horses to the end of Long Lane. We'll mount there, and walk on foot till then. Let's see. I think it's about seven o'clock now, and we can easily get there by lunchtime."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I dare assure you, sir, 'tis almost two,","And 'twill be supper-time ere you come there."],"id":807,"m":"I assure you, sir, it's almost two, and it'll be suppertime before you get there."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["It shall be seven ere I go to horse.","Look what I speak, or do, or think to do,","You are still crossing it. Sirs, let 't alone:","I will not go today; and ere I do,","It shall be what o'clock I say it is."],"id":808,"m":"It will be seven before I get on my horse. Look, whatever I say, or do, or think of doing, you're always contradicting it. Gentlemen, forget it. I won't go today, and before I do, it'll be whatever time I say it is."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Why, so this gallant will command the sun."],"id":809,"m":"Why, at this rate this fine fellow will give orders to the sun."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":810},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"IV","loc":"Padua. Before Baptista's house.","id":811},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Tranio and the Pedant dressed like Vincentio.","id":812},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Sir, this is the house; please it you that I call?"],"id":813,"m":"Sir, this is the house. Shall I call?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Ay, what else? and, but I be deceived,","Signior Baptista may remember me,","Near twenty years ago in Genoa,","Where we were lodgers at the Pegasus."],"id":814,"m":"Yes, what else? And unless I'm mistaken, Signior Baptista may remember me from nearly twenty years ago in Genoa, when we were both staying at the Pegasus inn."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["'Tis well; and hold your own, in any case,","With such austerity as 'longeth to a father."],"id":815,"m":"Good. And in any case, play your part with the seriousness that suits a father."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["I warrant you. But, sir, here comes your boy;","'Twere good he were school'd."],"id":816,"m":"I promise you. But, sir, here comes your servant. It would be good to coach him."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Biondello.","id":817},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Fear you not him. Sirrah Biondello,","Now do your duty throughly, I advise you.","Imagine 'twere the right Vincentio."],"id":818,"m":"Don't worry about him. Biondello, now do your job thoroughly, I advise you. Imagine this is the real Vincentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Tut! fear not me."],"id":819,"m":"Pooh! Don't worry about me."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["But hast thou done thy errand to Baptista?"],"id":820,"m":"But have you done your errand to Baptista?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["I told him that your father was at Venice,","And that you look'd for him this day in Padua."],"id":821,"m":"I told him your father was in Venice, and that you expected him in Padua today."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Th'art a tall fellow; hold thee that to drink.","Here comes Baptista. Set your countenance, sir."],"id":822,"m":"You're a fine fellow. Take that and buy yourself a drink. Here comes Baptista. Put on the right expression, sir."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Baptista and Lucentio.","id":823},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","cont":1,"lines":["Signior Baptista, you are happily met.","[To the Pedant] Sir, this is the gentleman I told you of;","I pray you stand good father to me now;","Give me Bianca for my patrimony."],"id":824,"m":"Signior Baptista, how lucky to meet you. Sir, this is the gentleman I told you about. Please be a good father to me now. Give me Bianca in exchange for my inheritance."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Soft, son!","Sir, by your leave: having come to Padua","To gather in some debts, my son Lucentio","Made me acquainted with a weighty cause","Of love between your daughter and himself:","And,—for the good report I hear of you,","And for the love he beareth to your daughter,","And she to him,—to stay him not too long,","I am content, in a good father's care,","To have him match'd; and, if you please to like","No worse than I, upon some agreement","Me shall you find ready and willing","With one consent to have her so bestow'd;","For curious I cannot be with you,","Signior Baptista, of whom I hear so well."],"id":825,"m":"Gently, son! Sir, with your permission: having come to Padua to collect some debts, my son Lucentio told me about a serious matter of love between your daughter and himself. And because of the good reports I hear of you, and because of the love he has for your daughter and she for him, so as not to keep him waiting too long, I'm happy, as a caring father, to see him married. And if you like it no less than I do, then once we reach some agreement you'll find me ready and willing to agree to her being matched with him. I can't be fussy with you, Signior Baptista, when I hear such good things of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Sir, pardon me in what I have to say.","Your plainness and your shortness please me well.","Right true it is your son Lucentio here","Doth love my daughter, and she loveth him,","Or both dissemble deeply their affections;","And therefore, if you say no more than this,","That like a father you will deal with him,","And pass my daughter a sufficient dower,","The match is made, and all is done:","Your son shall have my daughter with consent."],"id":826,"m":"Sir, forgive me for what I have to say. Your frankness and brevity please me. It's quite true that your son Lucentio here loves my daughter, and she loves him, or else they're both very good at hiding their feelings. And so, if you'll say no more than this, that you'll treat him like a father and guarantee my daughter a sufficient settlement, the match is made and everything is done. Your son will have my daughter with my consent."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["I thank you, sir. Where then do you know best","We be affied, and such assurance ta'en","As shall with either part's agreement stand?"],"id":827,"m":"Thank you, sir. Where do you think best for us to be formally engaged and have the guarantees drawn up that both parties will agree to?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Not in my house, Lucentio, for you know","Pitchers have ears, and I have many servants;","Besides, old Gremio is hearkening still,","And happily we might be interrupted."],"id":828,"m":"Not in my house, Lucentio, because, you know, walls have ears, and I have many servants. Besides, old Gremio is always listening, and we might be interrupted."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Then at my lodging, and it like you:","There doth my father lie; and there this night","We'll pass the business privately and well.","Send for your daughter by your servant here;","My boy shall fetch the scrivener presently.","The worst is this, that at so slender warning","You are like to have a thin and slender pittance."],"id":829,"m":"Then at my lodgings, if you like. My father is staying there, and tonight we'll settle the business privately and properly. Send for your daughter by your servant here. My boy will fetch the lawyer at once. The worst of it is that with such short notice you're likely to get a poor, meagre meal."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["It likes me well. Cambio, hie you home,","And bid Bianca make her ready straight;","And, if you will, tell what hath happened:","Lucentio's father is arriv'd in Padua,","And how she's like to be Lucentio's wife."],"id":830,"m":"That suits me. Cambio, hurry home and tell Bianca to get ready at once. And if you like, tell her what has happened: that Lucentio's father has arrived in Padua, and that she's likely to be Lucentio's wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I pray the gods she may, with all my heart!"],"id":831,"m":"I pray to the gods she will, with all my heart!"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Dally not with the gods, but get thee gone.","Signior Baptista, shall I lead the way?","Welcome! One mess is like to be your cheer;","Come, sir; we will better it in Pisa."],"id":832,"m":"Don't mess about with the gods; just get going. Signior Baptista, shall I lead the way? Welcome! One dish is likely to be your whole meal. Come, sir, we'll do better for you in Pisa."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["I follow you."],"id":833,"m":"I'll follow you."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Tranio, Pedant and Baptista.","id":834},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Cambio!"],"id":835,"m":"Cambio!"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["What say'st thou, Biondello?"],"id":836,"m":"What is it, Biondello?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["You saw my master wink and laugh upon you?"],"id":837,"m":"Did you see my master wink and smile at you?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Biondello, what of that?"],"id":838,"m":"Biondello, what about it?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Faith, nothing; but has left me here behind to expound the meaning or","moral of his signs and tokens."],"id":839,"m":"Honestly, nothing. But he's left me behind to explain the meaning, or moral, of his signs and signals."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I pray thee moralize them."],"id":840,"m":"Please explain them, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Then thus: Baptista is safe, talking with the deceiving father of a","deceitful son."],"id":841,"m":"Like this: Baptista is safely out of the way, talking with the fake father of a deceitful son."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["And what of him?"],"id":842,"m":"And what about him?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["His daughter is to be brought by you to the supper."],"id":843,"m":"You're to bring his daughter to the supper."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["And then?"],"id":844,"m":"And then?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["The old priest at Saint Luke's church is at your command at all hours."],"id":845,"m":"The old priest at Saint Luke's church is at your service at any hour."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["And what of all this?"],"id":846,"m":"And what does all this mean?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["I cannot tell, except they are busied about a counterfeit assurance.","Take your assurance of her, cum privilegio ad imprimendum solum; to","the church! take the priest, clerk, and some sufficient honest","witnesses. If this be not that you look for, I have more to say, But","bid Bianca farewell for ever and a day."],"id":847,"m":"I can't say, except that they're busy with a fake marriage settlement. You make your own settlement on her, with the sole right to print, as the books say. To the church! Take the priest, the clerk and some reliable, honest witnesses. If this isn't what you're looking for, I've nothing more to say, except: say goodbye to Bianca for ever and a day."},{"t":"sd","text":"Going.","id":848},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Hear'st thou, Biondello?"],"id":849,"m":"Are you listening, Biondello?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["I cannot tarry: I knew a wench married in an afternoon as she went to","the garden for parsley to stuff a rabbit; and so may you, sir; and so","adieu, sir. My master hath appointed me to go to Saint Luke's to bid","the priest be ready to come against you come with your appendix."],"id":850,"m":"I can't stay. I knew a girl who got married in an afternoon when she went to the garden to fetch parsley to stuff a rabbit, and so could you, sir. And so goodbye, sir. My master has told me to go to Saint Luke's and tell the priest to be ready for when you come with your appendage."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":851},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I may, and will, if she be so contented.","She will be pleas'd; then wherefore should I doubt?","Hap what hap may, I'll roundly go about her;","It shall go hard if Cambio go without her:"],"id":852,"m":"I can, and I will, if she agrees. She'll be pleased, so why should I doubt it? Whatever happens, I'll go straight at it with her. It'll be hard luck if Cambio goes without her."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":853},{"t":"scene","act":4,"scene":"V","loc":"A public road.","id":854},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Petruchio, Katherina, Hortensio and Servants.","id":855},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Come on, i' God's name; once more toward our father's.","Good Lord, how bright and goodly shines the moon!"],"id":856,"m":"Come on, in God's name, once more towards our father's house. Good Lord, how bright and beautifully the moon is shining!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["The moon! The sun; it is not moonlight now."],"id":857,"m":"The moon! It's the sun. It isn't moonlight now."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I say it is the moon that shines so bright."],"id":858,"m":"I say it's the moon that's shining so bright."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I know it is the sun that shines so bright."],"id":859,"m":"I know it's the sun that's shining so bright."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Now by my mother's son, and that's myself,","It shall be moon, or star, or what I list,","Or ere I journey to your father's house.","Go on and fetch our horses back again.","Evermore cross'd and cross'd; nothing but cross'd!"],"id":860,"m":"Now, by my mother's son, and that's me, it will be the moon, or a star, or whatever I like, before I travel to your father's house. Go and bring our horses back again. Always contradicted, contradicted, nothing but contradicted!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Say as he says, or we shall never go."],"id":861,"m":"Say what he says, or we'll never get there."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Forward, I pray, since we have come so far,","And be it moon, or sun, or what you please;","And if you please to call it a rush-candle,","Henceforth I vow it shall be so for me."],"id":862,"m":"Go on, please, since we've come so far, and let it be the moon, or the sun, or whatever you like. And if you choose to call it a cheap candle, I swear from now on it will be one for me."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I say it is the moon."],"id":863,"m":"I say it's the moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["I know it is the moon."],"id":864,"m":"I know it's the moon."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Nay, then you lie; it is the blessed sun."],"id":865,"m":"No, then you're lying. It's the blessed sun."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Then, God be bless'd, it is the blessed sun;","But sun it is not when you say it is not,","And the moon changes even as your mind.","What you will have it nam'd, even that it is,","And so it shall be so for Katherine."],"id":866,"m":"Then, God be blessed, it's the blessed sun. But it isn't the sun when you say it isn't, and the moon changes just like your mind. Whatever you want it called, that's what it is, and that's what it will be for Katherine."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Petruchio, go thy ways; the field is won."],"id":867,"m":"Petruchio, carry on. The battle is won."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Well, forward, forward! thus the bowl should run,","And not unluckily against the bias.","But, soft! Company is coming here."],"id":868,"m":"Well, onward, onward! This is how the bowl should run, and not perversely against its natural curve. But wait! Someone's coming."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Vincentio, in a travelling dress.","id":869},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["[To Vincentio] Good morrow, gentle mistress; where away?","Tell me, sweet Kate, and tell me truly too,","Hast thou beheld a fresher gentlewoman?","Such war of white and red within her cheeks!","What stars do spangle heaven with such beauty","As those two eyes become that heavenly face?","Fair lovely maid, once more good day to thee.","Sweet Kate, embrace her for her beauty's sake."],"id":870,"m":"Good morning, gentle lady. Where are you going? Tell me, sweet Kate, and tell me honestly, have you ever seen a fresher young lady? Such a battle of white and red in her cheeks! What stars light up the sky as beautifully as those two eyes suit that heavenly face? Beautiful, lovely girl, once more good day to you. Sweet Kate, embrace her for her beauty's sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["A will make the man mad, to make a woman of him."],"id":871,"m":"He'll drive the man mad, making a woman of him."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Young budding virgin, fair and fresh and sweet,","Whither away, or where is thy abode?","Happy the parents of so fair a child;","Happier the man whom favourable stars","Allot thee for his lovely bedfellow."],"id":872,"m":"Young, budding girl, fair and fresh and sweet, where are you going, and where do you live? Happy are the parents of so lovely a child, and happier the man whom kind stars give you to as his lovely bedfellow."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, how now, Kate! I hope thou art not mad:","This is a man, old, wrinkled, faded, wither'd,","And not a maiden, as thou sayst he is."],"id":873,"m":"Why, what's this, Kate? I hope you're not mad. This is a man: old, wrinkled, faded and withered, not a girl, as you say he is."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Pardon, old father, my mistaking eyes,","That have been so bedazzled with the sun","That everything I look on seemeth green:","Now I perceive thou art a reverend father;","Pardon, I pray thee, for my mad mistaking."],"id":874,"m":"Forgive my mistaken eyes, old father. They've been so dazzled by the sun that everything I look at seems green and young. Now I see you're a respected old man. Please forgive me for my mad mistake."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Do, good old grandsire, and withal make known","Which way thou travellest: if along with us,","We shall be joyful of thy company."],"id":875,"m":"Do forgive her, good old grandfather, and tell us which way you're travelling. If it's our way, we'll be glad of your company."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Fair sir, and you my merry mistress,","That with your strange encounter much amaz'd me,","My name is called Vincentio; my dwelling Pisa;","And bound I am to Padua, there to visit","A son of mine, which long I have not seen."],"id":876,"m":"Good sir, and you, my merry lady, who astonished me with your strange greeting: my name is Vincentio, I live in Pisa, and I'm on my way to Padua to visit a son of mine whom I haven't seen for a long time."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["What is his name?"],"id":877,"m":"What's his name?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Lucentio, gentle sir."],"id":878,"m":"Lucentio, kind sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Happily met; the happier for thy son.","And now by law, as well as reverend age,","I may entitle thee my loving father:","The sister to my wife, this gentlewoman,","Thy son by this hath married. Wonder not,","Nor be not griev'd: she is of good esteem,","Her dowry wealthy, and of worthy birth;","Beside, so qualified as may beseem","The spouse of any noble gentleman.","Let me embrace with old Vincentio;","And wander we to see thy honest son,","Who will of thy arrival be full joyous."],"id":879,"m":"Happily met, and happier for your son. And now by law, as well as by your respected age, I can call you my loving father. Your son has by now married this lady's sister, my wife's sister. Don't be surprised or upset: she has a good reputation, a rich dowry and good birth, and besides, she has all the qualities fit for the wife of any noble gentleman. Let me embrace old Vincentio, and let's travel on to see your honest son, who'll be overjoyed at your arrival."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["But is this true? or is it else your pleasure,","Like pleasant travellers, to break a jest","Upon the company you overtake?"],"id":880,"m":"But is this true? Or are you just enjoying yourself, like playful travellers, by playing a joke on the people you meet on the road?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["I do assure thee, father, so it is."],"id":881,"m":"I assure you, father, it's true."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Come, go along, and see the truth hereof;","For our first merriment hath made thee jealous."],"id":882,"m":"Come along and see the truth of it for yourself, because our first joke has made you suspicious."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt all but Hortensio.","id":883},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Well, Petruchio, this has put me in heart.","Have to my widow! and if she be froward,","Then hast thou taught Hortensio to be untoward."],"id":884,"m":"Well, Petruchio, this has given me heart. Off to my widow! And if she's stubborn, then you've taught Hortensio to be rough."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":885},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"I","loc":"Padua. Before Lucentio's house.","id":886},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter on one side Biondello, Lucentio and Bianca; Gremio walking on other side.","id":887},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Softly and swiftly, sir, for the priest is ready."],"id":888,"m":"Quietly and quickly, sir, because the priest is ready."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I fly, Biondello; but they may chance to need thee at home, therefore","leave us."],"id":889,"m":"I'm flying, Biondello. But they might need you at home, so leave us."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Nay, faith, I'll see the church o' your back; and then come back to my","master's as soon as I can."],"id":890,"m":"No, honestly, I'll see the church at your back, and then come back to my master's as soon as I can."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lucentio, Bianca and Biondello.","id":891},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["I marvel Cambio comes not all this while."],"id":892,"m":"I'm surprised Cambio hasn't come all this time."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Petruchio, Katherina, Vincentio and Attendants.","id":893},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Sir, here's the door; this is Lucentio's house:","My father's bears more toward the market-place;","Thither must I, and here I leave you, sir."],"id":894,"m":"Sir, here's the door. This is Lucentio's house. My father-in-law's is nearer the market square. I must go there, and here I'll leave you, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["You shall not choose but drink before you go.","I think I shall command your welcome here,","And by all likelihood some cheer is toward."],"id":895,"m":"You must have a drink before you go. I think I can make you welcome here, and very likely there's some celebration going on."},{"t":"sd","text":"Knocks.","id":896},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["They're busy within; you were best knock louder."],"id":897,"m":"They're busy inside. You'd better knock louder."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Pedant above, at a window.","id":898},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["What's he that knocks as he would beat down the gate?"],"id":899,"m":"Who's that knocking as if he wants to beat down the door?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Is Signior Lucentio within, sir?"],"id":900,"m":"Is Signior Lucentio in, sir?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["He's within, sir, but not to be spoken withal."],"id":901,"m":"He's in, sir, but he can't be spoken to."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["What if a man bring him a hundred pound or two to make merry withal?"],"id":902,"m":"What if someone brings him a hundred pounds or two to enjoy himself with?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Keep your hundred pounds to yourself: he shall need none so long as I","live."],"id":903,"m":"Keep your hundred pounds to yourself. He won't need any as long as I'm alive."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Nay, I told you your son was well beloved in Padua. Do you hear, sir?","To leave frivolous circumstances, I pray you tell Signior Lucentio that","his father is come from Pisa, and is here at the door to speak with","him."],"id":904,"m":"There, I told you your son was well loved in Padua. Do you hear, sir? Leaving aside all this nonsense, please tell Signior Lucentio that his father has come from Pisa and is here at the door to speak to him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Thou liest: his father is come from Padua, and here looking out at the","window."],"id":905,"m":"You're lying. His father has come from Padua, and is here looking out of the window."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Art thou his father?"],"id":906,"m":"Are you his father?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Ay, sir; so his mother says, if I may believe her."],"id":907,"m":"Yes, sir, so his mother says, if I can believe her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["[To Vincentio] Why, how now, gentleman! why, this is flat knavery to","take upon you another man's name."],"id":908,"m":"Why, what's this, sir? Why, it's downright fraud to take another man's name."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Lay hands on the villain: I believe a means to cozen somebody in this","city under my countenance."],"id":909,"m":"Seize the villain. I believe he means to cheat somebody in this city by pretending to be me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Biondello.","id":910},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["I have seen them in the church together: God send 'em good shipping!","But who is here? Mine old master, Vincentio! Now we are undone and","brought to nothing."],"id":911,"m":"I've seen them in the church together. God send them a good voyage! But who's this? My old master, Vincentio! Now we're ruined and done for."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["[Seeing Biondello] Come hither, crack-hemp."],"id":912,"m":"Come here, you gallows-bird."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["I hope I may choose, sir."],"id":913,"m":"I hope I can do as I like, sir."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Come hither, you rogue. What, have you forgot me?"],"id":914,"m":"Come here, you rogue. What, have you forgotten me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Forgot you! No, sir: I could not forget you, for I never saw you before","in all my life."],"id":915,"m":"Forgotten you! No, sir. I couldn't forget you, because I've never seen you before in my life."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["What, you notorious villain! didst thou never see thy master's father,","Vincentio?"],"id":916,"m":"What, you notorious villain! Have you never seen your master's father, Vincentio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["What, my old worshipful old master? Yes, marry, sir; see where he looks","out of the window."],"id":917,"m":"What, my old, honoured old master? Yes, indeed, sir. Look, there he is, looking out of the window."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Is't so, indeed?"],"id":918,"m":"Is that so, indeed?"},{"t":"sd","text":"He beats Biondello.","id":919},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Help, help, help! here's a madman will murder me."],"id":920,"m":"Help, help, help! Here's a madman who'll murder me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":921},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Help, son! help, Signior Baptista!"],"id":922,"m":"Help, son! Help, Signior Baptista!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit from the window.","id":923},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Prithee, Kate, let's stand aside and see the end of this controversy."],"id":924,"m":"Please, Kate, let's stand aside and see how this argument ends."},{"t":"sd","text":"They retire.","id":925},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Pedant, below; Baptista, Tranio and Servants.","id":926},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Sir, what are you that offer to beat my servant?"],"id":927,"m":"Sir, who are you to try to beat my servant?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["What am I, sir! nay, what are you, sir? O immortal gods! O fine","villain! A silken doublet, a velvet hose, a scarlet cloak, and a","copatain hat! O, I am undone! I am undone! While I play the good","husband at home, my son and my servant spend all at the university."],"id":928,"m":"Who am I, sir? No, who are you, sir? Oh immortal gods! Oh, you fine villain! A silk jacket, velvet breeches, a scarlet cloak and a tall pointed hat! Oh, I'm ruined! I'm ruined! While I'm being careful with money at home, my son and my servant are spending it all at the university."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["How now! what's the matter?"],"id":929,"m":"What's this? What's the matter?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["What, is the man lunatic?"],"id":930,"m":"What, is the man mad?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Sir, you seem a sober ancient gentleman by your habit, but your words","show you a madman. Why, sir, what 'cerns it you if I wear pearl and","gold? I thank my good father, I am able to maintain it."],"id":931,"m":"Sir, from your clothes you look like a sensible old gentleman, but your words show you're a madman. Why, sir, what's it to you if I wear pearls and gold? Thanks to my good father, I can afford it."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Thy father! O villain! he is a sailmaker in Bergamo."],"id":932,"m":"Your father! Oh, you villain! He's a sailmaker in Bergamo."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["You mistake, sir; you mistake, sir. Pray, what do you think is his","name?"],"id":933,"m":"You're mistaken, sir, you're mistaken, sir. Please, what do you think his name is?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["His name! As if I knew not his name! I have brought him up ever since","he was three years old, and his name is Tranio."],"id":934,"m":"His name! As if I didn't know his name! I've brought him up since he was three years old, and his name is Tranio."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Away, away, mad ass! His name is Lucentio; and he is mine only son, and","heir to the lands of me, Signior Vincentio."],"id":935,"m":"Get away, get away, you mad ass! His name is Lucentio, and he's my only son and heir to my lands, mine, Signior Vincentio's."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Lucentio! O, he hath murdered his master! Lay hold on him, I charge","you, in the Duke's name. O, my son, my son! Tell me, thou villain,","where is my son, Lucentio?"],"id":936,"m":"Lucentio! Oh, he has murdered his master! Seize him, I order you, in the Duke's name. Oh, my son, my son! Tell me, you villain, where is my son Lucentio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Call forth an officer."],"id":937,"m":"Call an officer."},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter one with an Officer.","id":938},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","cont":1,"lines":["Carry this mad knave to the gaol. Father Baptista, I charge you see","that he be forthcoming."],"id":939,"m":"Take this mad rascal to prison. Father Baptista, I ask you to make sure he's kept for trial."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Carry me to the gaol!"],"id":940,"m":"Take me to prison!"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Stay, officer; he shall not go to prison."],"id":941,"m":"Wait, officer. He won't go to prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Talk not, Signior Gremio; I say he shall go to prison."],"id":942,"m":"Don't interfere, Signior Gremio. I say he will go to prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Take heed, Signior Baptista, lest you be cony-catched in this business;","I dare swear this is the right Vincentio."],"id":943,"m":"Be careful, Signior Baptista, that you're not swindled in this business. I'd swear this is the real Vincentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"PEDANT","lines":["Swear if thou darest."],"id":944,"m":"Swear it if you dare."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Nay, I dare not swear it."],"id":945,"m":"No, I don't dare swear it."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["Then thou wert best say that I am not Lucentio."],"id":946,"m":"Then you'd better say I'm not Lucentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Yes, I know thee to be Signior Lucentio."],"id":947,"m":"Yes, I know you're Signior Lucentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Away with the dotard! to the gaol with him!"],"id":948,"m":"Away with the old fool! To prison with him!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Thus strangers may be haled and abus'd: O monstrous villain!"],"id":949,"m":"So strangers can be dragged off and mistreated. Oh, monstrous villain!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Biondello, with Lucentio and Bianca.","id":950},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["O! we are spoiled; and yonder he is: deny him, forswear him, or else we","are all undone."],"id":951,"m":"Oh, we're ruined, and there he is! Deny him, disown him, or we're all done for."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["[Kneeling] Pardon, sweet father."],"id":952,"m":"Forgive me, dear father."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Lives my sweetest son?"],"id":953,"m":"Is my dearest son alive?"},{"t":"sd","text":"Biondello, Tranio and Pedant run out.","id":954},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["[Kneeling] Pardon, dear father."],"id":955,"m":"Forgive me, dear father."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["How hast thou offended?","Where is Lucentio?"],"id":956,"m":"What have you done wrong? Where is Lucentio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Here's Lucentio,","Right son to the right Vincentio;","That have by marriage made thy daughter mine,","While counterfeit supposes blear'd thine eyne."],"id":957,"m":"Here's Lucentio, true son of the true Vincentio, who has made your daughter mine by marriage while fake impostors blinded your eyes."},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Here 's packing, with a witness, to deceive us all!"],"id":958,"m":"Here's a conspiracy, and no mistake, to deceive us all!"},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Where is that damned villain, Tranio,","That fac'd and brav'd me in this matter so?"],"id":959,"m":"Where's that damned villain Tranio, who defied and insulted me to my face in this business?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Why, tell me, is not this my Cambio?"],"id":960,"m":"Why, tell me, isn't this my Cambio?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Cambio is chang'd into Lucentio."],"id":961,"m":"Cambio has changed into Lucentio."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Love wrought these miracles. Bianca's love","Made me exchange my state with Tranio,","While he did bear my countenance in the town;","And happily I have arriv'd at the last","Unto the wished haven of my bliss.","What Tranio did, myself enforc'd him to;","Then pardon him, sweet father, for my sake."],"id":962,"m":"Love worked these miracles. Love for Bianca made me exchange places with Tranio while he played me in the town, and happily I have at last reached the harbour of happiness I wished for. What Tranio did, I made him do, so forgive him, dear father, for my sake."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["I'll slit the villain's nose that would have sent me to the gaol."],"id":963,"m":"I'll slit the nose of the villain who would have sent me to prison."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["[To Lucentio] But do you hear, sir? Have you married my daughter","without asking my good will?"],"id":964,"m":"But listen, sir: have you married my daughter without asking my permission?"},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Fear not, Baptista; we will content you, go to: but I will in, to be","revenged for this villainy."],"id":965,"m":"Don't worry, Baptista. We'll satisfy you, come on. But I'll go in and get my revenge for this villainy."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":966},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["And I to sound the depth of this knavery."],"id":967,"m":"And I'll go and get to the bottom of this trickery."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":968},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Look not pale, Bianca; thy father will not frown."],"id":969,"m":"Don't look pale, Bianca. Your father won't stay angry."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Lucentio and Bianca.","id":970},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["My cake is dough, but I'll in among the rest;","Out of hope of all but my share of the feast."],"id":971,"m":"My cake is dough: I've failed. But I'll go in with the rest, hoping for nothing but my share of the feast."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":972},{"t":"sd","text":"Petruchio and Katherina advance.","id":973},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Husband, let's follow to see the end of this ado."],"id":974,"m":"Husband, let's follow and see how all this fuss ends."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["First kiss me, Kate, and we will."],"id":975,"m":"Kiss me first, Kate, and we will."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["What! in the midst of the street?"],"id":976,"m":"What! In the middle of the street?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["What! art thou ashamed of me?"],"id":977,"m":"What! Are you ashamed of me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["No, sir; God forbid; but ashamed to kiss."],"id":978,"m":"No, sir, God forbid, but ashamed to kiss."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, then, let's home again. Come, sirrah, let's away."],"id":979,"m":"Why, then, let's go home again. Come on, you, let's go."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Nay, I will give thee a kiss: now pray thee, love, stay."],"id":980,"m":"No, I'll give you a kiss. Now please, love, stay."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Is not this well? Come, my sweet Kate:","Better once than never, for never too late."],"id":981,"m":"Isn't this good? Come, my sweet Kate. Better once than never, because it's never too late."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":982},{"t":"scene","act":5,"scene":"II","loc":"A room in Lucentio's house.","id":983},{"t":"sd","text":"Enter Baptista, Vincentio, Gremio, the Pedant, Lucentio, Bianca, Petruchio, Katherina, Hortensio and Widow. Tranio, Biondello and Grumio and Others, attending.","id":984},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["At last, though long, our jarring notes agree:","And time it is when raging war is done,","To smile at 'scapes and perils overblown.","My fair Bianca, bid my father welcome,","While I with self-same kindness welcome thine.","Brother Petruchio, sister Katherina,","And thou, Hortensio, with thy loving widow,","Feast with the best, and welcome to my house:","My banquet is to close our stomachs up,","After our great good cheer. Pray you, sit down;","For now we sit to chat as well as eat."],"id":985,"m":"At last, after a long time, our clashing notes are in harmony, and now that the raging war is over, it's time to smile at narrow escapes and dangers that have passed. My lovely Bianca, welcome my father, while I welcome yours with the same kindness. Brother Petruchio, sister Katherina, and you, Hortensio, with your loving widow, enjoy the best food, and welcome to my house. This banquet is a dessert to round off our great feast. Please sit down, because now we're sitting to chat as well as to eat."},{"t":"sd","text":"They sit at table.","id":986},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Nothing but sit and sit, and eat and eat!"],"id":987,"m":"Nothing but sitting and sitting, and eating and eating!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Padua affords this kindness, son Petruchio."],"id":988,"m":"Padua offers this kindness, son Petruchio."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Padua affords nothing but what is kind."],"id":989,"m":"Padua offers nothing that isn't kind."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["For both our sakes I would that word were true."],"id":990,"m":"For both our sakes, I wish that were true."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Now, for my life, Hortensio fears his widow."],"id":991,"m":"Now, on my life, Hortensio is afraid of his widow."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Then never trust me if I be afeard."],"id":992,"m":"Then never trust me if I'm afraid."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["You are very sensible, and yet you miss my sense:","I mean Hortensio is afeard of you."],"id":993,"m":"You're very sensible, and yet you miss my sense. I mean Hortensio is afraid of you."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["He that is giddy thinks the world turns round."],"id":994,"m":"Someone who's dizzy thinks the world is spinning round."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Roundly replied."],"id":995,"m":"A well-rounded reply."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Mistress, how mean you that?"],"id":996,"m":"Madam, what do you mean by that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Thus I conceive by him."],"id":997,"m":"That's what I gather from him."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Conceives by me! How likes Hortensio that?"],"id":998,"m":"Conceive by me! How does Hortensio like that?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["My widow says thus she conceives her tale."],"id":999,"m":"My widow means that's how she understands his remark."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Very well mended. Kiss him for that, good widow."],"id":1000,"m":"Very well rescued. Kiss him for that, good widow."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["'He that is giddy thinks the world turns round':","I pray you tell me what you meant by that."],"id":1001,"m":"\"Someone who's dizzy thinks the world is spinning round.\" Please tell me what you meant by that."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Your husband, being troubled with a shrew,","Measures my husband's sorrow by his woe;","And now you know my meaning."],"id":1002,"m":"Your husband, being troubled with a shrew, judges my husband's sorrow by his own misery. And now you know my meaning."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["A very mean meaning."],"id":1003,"m":"A very mean meaning."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Right, I mean you."],"id":1004,"m":"Yes, I mean you."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["And I am mean, indeed, respecting you."],"id":1005,"m":"And I'm being mild, indeed, compared with you."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["To her, Kate!"],"id":1006,"m":"At her, Kate!"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["To her, widow!"],"id":1007,"m":"At her, widow!"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A hundred marks, my Kate does put her down."],"id":1008,"m":"A hundred marks says my Kate will floor her."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["That's my office."],"id":1009,"m":"That's my job."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Spoke like an officer: ha' to thee, lad."],"id":1010,"m":"Spoken like a man on duty. Here's to you, lad."},{"t":"sd","text":"Drinks to Hortensio.","id":1011},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["How likes Gremio these quick-witted folks?"],"id":1012,"m":"How does Gremio like these quick-witted people?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Believe me, sir, they butt together well."],"id":1013,"m":"Believe me, sir, they butt heads well together."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Head and butt! An hasty-witted body","Would say your head and butt were head and horn."],"id":1014,"m":"Head and butt! A quick-witted person would say your head and butt were a head and a horn."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["Ay, mistress bride, hath that awaken'd you?"],"id":1015,"m":"Yes, madam bride, has that woken you up?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Ay, but not frighted me; therefore I'll sleep again."],"id":1016,"m":"Yes, but it hasn't frightened me, so I'll go back to sleep."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Nay, that you shall not; since you have begun,","Have at you for a bitter jest or two."],"id":1017,"m":"No, you won't. Since you've started, I'll have a go at you with a sharp joke or two."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Am I your bird? I mean to shift my bush,","And then pursue me as you draw your bow.","You are welcome all."],"id":1018,"m":"Am I your bird? I mean to move to another bush, and then you can chase me while you draw your bow. You're all welcome."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Bianca, Katherina and Widow.","id":1019},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["She hath prevented me. Here, Signior Tranio;","This bird you aim'd at, though you hit her not:","Therefore a health to all that shot and miss'd."],"id":1020,"m":"She's got away from me. Here, Signior Tranio: this is the bird you aimed at, though you didn't hit her. So here's a toast to everyone who shot and missed."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["O, sir! Lucentio slipp'd me like his greyhound,","Which runs himself, and catches for his master."],"id":1021,"m":"Oh, sir, Lucentio let me off the leash like his greyhound, which runs for itself and catches for its master."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A good swift simile, but something currish."],"id":1022,"m":"A good quick comparison, but a bit doggy."},{"t":"sp","who":"TRANIO","lines":["'Tis well, sir, that you hunted for yourself:","'Tis thought your deer does hold you at a bay."],"id":1023,"m":"It's a good thing, sir, that you hunted for yourself. People think your deer is keeping you at bay."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["O ho, Petruchio! Tranio hits you now."],"id":1024,"m":"Oh ho, Petruchio! Tranio has hit you now."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I thank thee for that gird, good Tranio."],"id":1025,"m":"Thank you for that dig, good Tranio."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Confess, confess; hath he not hit you here?"],"id":1026,"m":"Admit it, admit it. Hasn't he hit you there?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A has a little gall'd me, I confess;","And as the jest did glance away from me,","'Tis ten to one it maim'd you two outright."],"id":1027,"m":"He's stung me a little, I admit. And as the joke glanced off me, it's ten to one it wounded you two badly."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Now, in good sadness, son Petruchio,","I think thou hast the veriest shrew of all."],"id":1028,"m":"Now, in all seriousness, son Petruchio, I think you've got the worst shrew of all."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Well, I say no; and therefore, for assurance,","Let's each one send unto his wife,","And he whose wife is most obedient,","To come at first when he doth send for her,","Shall win the wager which we will propose."],"id":1029,"m":"Well, I say no. And so, to prove it, let's each send for his wife, and the man whose wife is most obedient, and comes first when he sends for her, will win the bet we're going to make."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Content. What's the wager?"],"id":1030,"m":"Agreed. What's the bet?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Twenty crowns."],"id":1031,"m":"Twenty crowns."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Twenty crowns!","I'll venture so much of my hawk or hound,","But twenty times so much upon my wife."],"id":1032,"m":"Twenty crowns! I'd bet that much on my hawk or hound, but twenty times as much on my wife."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["A hundred then."],"id":1033,"m":"A hundred, then."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Content."],"id":1034,"m":"Agreed."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["A match! 'tis done."],"id":1035,"m":"It's a bet! It's done."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Who shall begin?"],"id":1036,"m":"Who'll go first?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["That will I.","Go, Biondello, bid your mistress come to me."],"id":1037,"m":"I will. Go, Biondello, and tell your mistress to come to me."},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["I go."],"id":1038,"m":"I'm going."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit.","id":1039},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Son, I'll be your half, Bianca comes."],"id":1040,"m":"Son, I'll go halves with you that Bianca comes."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I'll have no halves; I'll bear it all myself."],"id":1041,"m":"I won't have halves. I'll take it all on myself."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Biondello.","id":1042},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","cont":1,"lines":["How now! what news?"],"id":1043,"m":"Well? What news?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["Sir, my mistress sends you word","That she is busy and she cannot come."],"id":1044,"m":"Sir, my mistress sends you word that she's busy and can't come."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["How! She's busy, and she cannot come!","Is that an answer?"],"id":1045,"m":"What! She's busy and can't come! Is that an answer?"},{"t":"sp","who":"GREMIO","lines":["Ay, and a kind one too:","Pray God, sir, your wife send you not a worse."],"id":1046,"m":"Yes, and a kind one too. Pray God, sir, your wife doesn't send you a worse one."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I hope better."],"id":1047,"m":"I expect a better one."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Sirrah Biondello, go and entreat my wife","To come to me forthwith."],"id":1048,"m":"Biondello, go and ask my wife politely to come to me at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Biondello.","id":1049},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["O, ho! entreat her!","Nay, then she must needs come."],"id":1050,"m":"Oh ho! Ask her politely! No, then she's bound to come."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["I am afraid, sir,","Do what you can, yours will not be entreated."],"id":1051,"m":"I'm afraid, sir, whatever you do, yours won't be persuaded."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Biondello.","id":1052},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","cont":1,"lines":["Now, where's my wife?"],"id":1053,"m":"Now, where's my wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIONDELLO","lines":["She says you have some goodly jest in hand:","She will not come; she bids you come to her."],"id":1054,"m":"She says you have some fine joke going on. She won't come. She tells you to come to her."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Worse and worse; she will not come! O vile,","Intolerable, not to be endur'd!","Sirrah Grumio, go to your mistress,","Say I command her come to me."],"id":1055,"m":"Worse and worse. She won't come! Oh, vile, intolerable, not to be put up with! Grumio, go to your mistress and say I order her to come to me."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Grumio.","id":1056},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["I know her answer."],"id":1057,"m":"I know her answer."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["What?"],"id":1058,"m":"What?"},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["She will not."],"id":1059,"m":"She won't come."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["The fouler fortune mine, and there an end."],"id":1060,"m":"Then it's my bad luck, and that's the end of it."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Katherina.","id":1061},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Now, by my holidame, here comes Katherina!"],"id":1062,"m":"Now, by Our Lady, here comes Katherina!"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["What is your will sir, that you send for me?"],"id":1063,"m":"What do you want, sir, that you've sent for me?"},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Where is your sister, and Hortensio's wife?"],"id":1064,"m":"Where's your sister, and Hortensio's wife?"},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["They sit conferring by the parlour fire."],"id":1065,"m":"They're sitting chatting by the parlour fire."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Go fetch them hither; if they deny to come,","Swinge me them soundly forth unto their husbands.","Away, I say, and bring them hither straight."],"id":1066,"m":"Go and fetch them here. If they refuse to come, give them a good beating and drive them out to their husbands. Go, I tell you, and bring them here at once."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exit Katherina.","id":1067},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Here is a wonder, if you talk of a wonder."],"id":1068,"m":"Here's a wonder, if you want to talk about wonders."},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["And so it is. I wonder what it bodes."],"id":1069,"m":"So it is. I wonder what it means."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Marry, peace it bodes, and love, and quiet life,","An awful rule, and right supremacy;","And, to be short, what not that's sweet and happy."],"id":1070,"m":"Why, it means peace, and love, and a quiet life, rule that commands respect and proper authority. In short, everything that's sweet and happy."},{"t":"sp","who":"BAPTISTA","lines":["Now fair befall thee, good Petruchio!","The wager thou hast won; and I will add","Unto their losses twenty thousand crowns;","Another dowry to another daughter,","For she is chang'd, as she had never been."],"id":1071,"m":"Now good luck to you, good Petruchio! You've won the bet, and I'll add twenty thousand crowns to their losses: another dowry for another daughter, because she's changed as if she'd never been the same person."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Nay, I will win my wager better yet,","And show more sign of her obedience,","Her new-built virtue and obedience.","See where she comes, and brings your froward wives","As prisoners to her womanly persuasion."],"id":1072,"m":"No, I'll win my bet even more clearly, and show more evidence of her obedience, her newly built virtue and obedience. Look, here she comes, bringing your stubborn wives as prisoners of her womanly persuasion."},{"t":"sd","text":"Re-enter Katherina with Bianca and Widow.","id":1073},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","cont":1,"lines":["Katherine, that cap of yours becomes you not:","Off with that bauble, throw it underfoot."],"id":1074,"m":"Katherine, that cap doesn't suit you. Take that trinket off and throw it under your foot."},{"t":"sd","text":"Katherina pulls off her cap and throws it down.","id":1075},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Lord, let me never have a cause to sigh","Till I be brought to such a silly pass!"],"id":1076,"m":"Lord, never let me have reason to sigh until I'm brought to such a silly state!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["Fie! what a foolish duty call you this?"],"id":1077,"m":"Shame! What kind of foolish duty do you call this?"},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["I would your duty were as foolish too;","The wisdom of your duty, fair Bianca,","Hath cost me a hundred crowns since supper-time!"],"id":1078,"m":"I wish your duty were as foolish too. The wisdom of your duty, lovely Bianca, has cost me a hundred crowns since supper!"},{"t":"sp","who":"BIANCA","lines":["The more fool you for laying on my duty."],"id":1079,"m":"The more fool you for betting on my duty."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Katherine, I charge thee, tell these headstrong women","What duty they do owe their lords and husbands."],"id":1080,"m":"Katherine, I order you, tell these headstrong women what duty they owe their lords and husbands."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["Come, come, you're mocking; we will have no telling."],"id":1081,"m":"Come on, come on, you're mocking us. We won't have any lecture."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Come on, I say; and first begin with her."],"id":1082,"m":"Go on, I tell you, and start with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"WIDOW","lines":["She shall not."],"id":1083,"m":"She won't."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["I say she shall: and first begin with her."],"id":1084,"m":"I say she will. And start with her."},{"t":"sp","who":"KATHERINA","lines":["Fie, fie! unknit that threatening unkind brow,","And dart not scornful glances from those eyes","To wound thy lord, thy king, thy governor:","It blots thy beauty as frosts do bite the meads,","Confounds thy fame as whirlwinds shake fair buds,","And in no sense is meet or amiable.","A woman mov'd is like a fountain troubled,","Muddy, ill-seeming, thick, bereft of beauty;","And while it is so, none so dry or thirsty","Will deign to sip or touch one drop of it.","Thy husband is thy lord, thy life, thy keeper,","Thy head, thy sovereign; one that cares for thee,","And for thy maintenance commits his body","To painful labour both by sea and land,","To watch the night in storms, the day in cold,","Whilst thou liest warm at home, secure and safe;","And craves no other tribute at thy hands","But love, fair looks, and true obedience;","Too little payment for so great a debt.","Such duty as the subject owes the prince,","Even such a woman oweth to her husband;","And when she is froward, peevish, sullen, sour,","And not obedient to his honest will,","What is she but a foul contending rebel","And graceless traitor to her loving lord?—","I am asham'd that women are so simple","To offer war where they should kneel for peace,","Or seek for rule, supremacy, and sway,","When they are bound to serve, love, and obey.","Why are our bodies soft and weak and smooth,","Unapt to toil and trouble in the world,","But that our soft conditions and our hearts","Should well agree with our external parts?","Come, come, you froward and unable worms!","My mind hath been as big as one of yours,","My heart as great, my reason haply more,","To bandy word for word and frown for frown;","But now I see our lances are but straws,","Our strength as weak, our weakness past compare,","That seeming to be most which we indeed least are.","Then vail your stomachs, for it is no boot,","And place your hands below your husband's foot:","In token of which duty, if he please,","My hand is ready; may it do him ease."],"id":1085,"m":"Shame, shame! Smooth out that threatening, unkind frown, and don't dart scornful looks from those eyes to wound your lord, your king, your governor. It spoils your beauty as frost nips the meadows, ruins your reputation as whirlwinds shake lovely buds, and is in no way fitting or attractive. An angry woman is like a fountain that has been stirred up: muddy, ugly, thick, robbed of its beauty. And while it's like that, nobody, however dry or thirsty, will stoop to sip or touch a drop of it. Your husband is your lord, your life, your keeper, your head, your sovereign: someone who cares for you, and to support you puts his body through hard work on sea and land, staying awake at night in storms and by day in the cold, while you lie warm at home, safe and secure. And he asks no other payment from you but love, kind looks and true obedience: too little payment for so great a debt. The same duty a subject owes his ruler, a woman owes her husband. And when she's stubborn, irritable, sulky, sour, and doesn't obey his honest wishes, what is she but a vile, quarrelling rebel and a wicked traitor to her loving lord? I'm ashamed that women are so foolish as to declare war when they should kneel for peace, or seek to rule, dominate and control, when they're bound to serve, love and obey. Why are our bodies soft and weak and smooth, unfit for hard work and trouble in the world, unless it's so that our gentle natures and our hearts should match our outward parts? Come, come, you stubborn, feeble worms! My spirit has been as proud as either of yours, my heart as great, my reason perhaps greater, to trade word for word and frown for frown. But now I see our lances are only straws, our strength as weak as straw, our weakness beyond compare, so that we seem to be most what in fact we are least. So lower your pride, because it's no use, and place your hands beneath your husband's foot. As a sign of that duty, if he wishes, my hand is ready. May it give him pleasure."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Why, there's a wench! Come on, and kiss me, Kate."],"id":1086,"m":"Why, there's a girl! Come on and kiss me, Kate."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["Well, go thy ways, old lad, for thou shalt ha't."],"id":1087,"m":"Well, good for you, old lad, because you've won it."},{"t":"sp","who":"VINCENTIO","lines":["'Tis a good hearing when children are toward."],"id":1088,"m":"It's good to hear when children are obedient."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["But a harsh hearing when women are froward."],"id":1089,"m":"But hard to hear when women are stubborn."},{"t":"sp","who":"PETRUCHIO","lines":["Come, Kate, we'll to bed.","We three are married, but you two are sped.","'Twas I won the wager,","[To Lucentio] though you hit the white;","And being a winner, God give you good night!"],"id":1090,"m":"Come, Kate, we'll go to bed. Three of us are married, but you two are done for. It was I who won the bet, though you hit the bullseye. And as the winner, God give you good night!"},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt Petruchio and Katherina.","id":1091},{"t":"sp","who":"HORTENSIO","lines":["Now go thy ways; thou hast tam'd a curst shrew."],"id":1092,"m":"Well, good for you. You've tamed a cursed shrew."},{"t":"sp","who":"LUCENTIO","lines":["'Tis a wonder, by your leave, she will be tam'd so."],"id":1093,"m":"It's a wonder, if you'll pardon me saying so, that she lets herself be tamed like this."},{"t":"sd","text":"Exeunt.","id":1094}];
const STUDY={"chars":[{"name":"Christopher Sly","group":"The Induction","desc":"A drunken tinker from near Stratford, thrown out of an alehouse for breaking glasses. A lord finds him asleep on the heath and, as a joke, has him carried to his house, dressed in fine clothes and told he is a lord who has been mad for fifteen years. Sly refuses to believe it until he hears he has a beautiful wife. He watches the play of Katherine and Petruchio, but after a few comments at the end of 1.1 he is never heard from again."},{"name":"A Lord","group":"The Induction","desc":"A nobleman returning from hunting who finds Sly drunk and asleep. He plans the whole joke, instructs his servants and his page, disguises himself as a servant, and arranges for a company of travelling players to perform for Sly."},{"name":"Bartholomew, the Page","group":"The Induction","desc":"The Lord's page, dressed as a lady to play Sly's devoted wife. He calls Sly 'my husband and my lord' and, when Sly orders him to bed, invents a doctor's ban to put it off."},{"name":"The Hostess","group":"The Induction","desc":"The landlady of the alehouse, who demands payment for the glasses Sly has broken and goes for the constable."},{"name":"Katherina","group":"Baptista's household","desc":"Baptista's elder daughter, known all over Padua as Katherine the curst. She mocks her sister's suitors, ties up and strikes Bianca, and breaks a lute over Hortensio's head. Petruchio woos her with a storm of contradiction, announces their wedding against her protests, arrives late and absurdly dressed, and carries her off before the feast. At his country house he starves her, keeps her awake and denies her a cap and gown, all in the name of love. On the road back to Padua she agrees the sun is the moon. At the final feast she alone comes when called, and gives a long speech on the duty wives owe their husbands."},{"name":"Bianca","group":"Baptista's household","desc":"Baptista's younger daughter, admired for her mildness and courted by Gremio, Hortensio and 'Lucentio' (Tranio). She cannot marry until Katherina does. In her lessons she shows a sharp, playful wit, prefers 'Cambio' to 'Licio', and secretly marries the real Lucentio. At the final feast she refuses to come when he sends for her, and calls Katherina's obedience foolish."},{"name":"Baptista Minola","group":"Baptista's household","desc":"A rich gentleman of Padua, father of Katherina and Bianca. He refuses to let Bianca marry before her sister, agrees to Petruchio's suit once the dowry is settled, and offers Bianca to whichever suitor guarantees her the biggest widow's settlement. Tricked by the false Vincentio, he is finally reconciled to Lucentio's secret marriage, and adds a second dowry when Katherina wins Petruchio's wager."},{"name":"Petruchio","group":"Petruchio's household","desc":"A gentleman of Verona whose father, Antonio, has just died. He comes to Padua 'to wive it wealthily' and takes on Katherina for her dowry and the challenge. Quick-witted and loud, he praises everything she is not, tells her he was 'born to tame' her, and sets the wedding for Sunday. He treats the taming like the training of a hawk: no food, no sleep, constant contradiction, all wrapped in claims of loving care. He wins the wager at the end with Katherina's obedience."},{"name":"Grumio","group":"Petruchio's household","desc":"Petruchio's old servant and comic sidekick. He opens his master's first scene by misunderstanding 'knock me here', describes the journey to the country house in a tale Curtis can feel, teases the starving Katherina with names of food, and argues with the tailor about the gown."},{"name":"Curtis","group":"Petruchio's household","desc":"A servant at Petruchio's country house who hears Grumio's tale of the journey and reports Petruchio's 'sermon of continency' to Katherina in her bedroom."},{"name":"Nathaniel, Philip, Joseph, Nicholas and Peter","group":"Petruchio's household","desc":"Petruchio's other servants, who greet Grumio, are cursed by their master and see him throw the supper at them. Peter sums up the method: 'He kills her in her own humour.'"},{"name":"Lucentio","group":"Lucentio's household","desc":"A young gentleman of Pisa, son of Vincentio, who comes to Padua to study philosophy and falls in love with Bianca at first sight. He swaps clothes with his servant Tranio and, disguised as the schoolmaster Cambio, woos Bianca through a mock Latin lesson. He marries her in secret, then confesses all when his real father arrives. He loses the wager when Bianca will not come."},{"name":"Tranio","group":"Lucentio's household","desc":"Lucentio's quick-witted servant, who plays his master for most of the play. Dressed as Lucentio he joins the suitors, outbids Gremio for Bianca, persuades a travelling schoolmaster to pose as Vincentio, and tries to have the real Vincentio sent to prison. At the feast he trades jokes with Petruchio as a guest."},{"name":"Biondello","group":"Lucentio's household","desc":"Lucentio's younger servant, told that Tranio is now his master. He describes Petruchio's wedding outfit and broken-down horse, spots the Pedant, arranges the priest for Lucentio's secret wedding, and pretends not to know the real Vincentio."},{"name":"Vincentio","group":"Lucentio's household","desc":"Lucentio's father, a rich merchant of Pisa. On the road to Padua Petruchio makes Katherina greet him as a young girl. At his son's lodging he is told he is an impostor, sees his servant Tranio dressed in silks, and is nearly taken to prison before Lucentio returns married."},{"name":"The Pedant","group":"Lucentio's household","desc":"A travelling schoolmaster from Mantua. Tranio frightens him with an invented death sentence on all Mantuans in Padua and persuades him to pose as Vincentio and guarantee the dowry. When the real Vincentio arrives, he insists from the window that he is Lucentio's father."},{"name":"Hortensio","group":"Bianca's suitors","desc":"A gentleman of Padua, Petruchio's old friend and one of Bianca's suitors. He points Petruchio at Katherina, then disguises himself as Licio, a music teacher, and has the lute broken over his head. When he sees Bianca kissing 'Cambio' he gives her up, marries a wealthy widow and visits Petruchio's 'taming-school'. At the end his widow refuses to come when called."},{"name":"Gremio","group":"Bianca's suitors","desc":"An old, rich suitor to Bianca, called 'greybeard' and 'pantaloon'. He hires 'Cambio' to read love poetry to Bianca on his behalf, bids his house, farm and a ship for her and is outbid by 'Lucentio'. He reports the wild wedding, and is left with only 'my share of the feast'."},{"name":"The Widow","group":"Bianca's suitors","desc":"A wealthy widow who marries Hortensio. At the final feast she trades insults with Katherina, refuses to come when her husband sends for her, and is lectured by Katherina on a wife's duty."},{"name":"The Tailor and the Haberdasher","group":"Others","desc":"Tradesmen who bring Katherina a fashionable cap and gown at Petruchio's house. Petruchio insults them and refuses both, though he quietly arranges for the tailor to be paid."}],"scenes":{"a0s1":{"sum":"Outside an alehouse, the Hostess demands that Christopher Sly pay for the glasses he has broken. He refuses and falls asleep on the ground. A Lord returning from hunting finds him and decides to play a trick: Sly will be carried to the Lord's best room, dressed in fine clothes and told when he wakes that he is a great lord who has been mad. A company of travelling players arrives, and the Lord asks them to perform for a 'lord' who has never seen a play, warning them not to laugh at him. He sends for his page, Bartholomew, to dress as a lady and play Sly's loving wife, using an onion if he cannot cry on command.","key":["The whole play begins as a joke played on a poor man: the Lord changes Sly's clothes and treatment and watches to see if he 'forgets himself'.","Disguise and role-play are introduced before the main story starts: a beggar plays a lord, a boy plays a wife, actors play a play.","The Lord's instructions to his page describe the perfect obedient wife, a picture the play will return to at the end."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the Hostess angry with Sly?","o":["He has insulted her family","He will not pay for glasses he broke","He has stolen her ale","He has started a fight"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the Lord plan to make Sly believe?","o":["That he is dead","That he is a lord who has been mad","That he has won a fortune","That he is a famous actor"],"a":1},{"q":"Who will play Sly's wife?","o":["The Hostess","One of the players","The Lord's page, Bartholomew","The Lord himself"],"a":2}]},"a0s2":{"sum":"Sly wakes in a rich bedroom, surrounded by servants offering sack, sweetmeats and fine clothes. He insists he is Christopher Sly, a tinker who owes money for ale. The Lord and servants tell him he has been mad for fifteen years and describe his music, horses, hounds and paintings. When they mention his beautiful wife, he decides he really is a lord. The page arrives dressed as his lady. Sly wants to take her to bed at once, but the page says the doctors have forbidden it for a night or two. A messenger announces that the players have come to perform a comedy to cheer him up, and Sly settles down with his 'wife' to watch.","key":["Sly lists the jobs he has had (pedlar, card-maker, bear-keeper, tinker) and the alewife he owes: he is firmly rooted in the real English countryside.","He gives in when he is offered a wife, and from that moment he speaks in verse, like a lord.","The page's refusal of the bed uses the same excuse Petruchio will use with Katherina: it is done for the sake of health."],"quiz":[{"q":"How long do the servants say Sly has been mad?","o":["Seven months","Fifteen years","Three days","Forty years"],"a":1},{"q":"What finally convinces Sly that he is a lord?","o":["The music","His fine clothes","Being told he has a beautiful wife","Seeing his reflection"],"a":2},{"q":"Why does the 'wife' refuse to go to bed with Sly?","o":["She is afraid of him","The doctors have said it could bring his madness back","She wants to see the play first","The bed is not ready"],"a":1}]},"a1s1":{"sum":"Lucentio arrives in Padua from Pisa with his servant Tranio, planning to study philosophy. They watch as Baptista tells Bianca's suitors, Gremio and Hortensio, that he will not let her marry until her elder sister Katherina has a husband. The suitors insult Katherina, and she answers them sharply. Baptista sends Bianca indoors and promises to hire schoolmasters for her. Gremio and Hortensio agree to work together to find Katherina a husband. Lucentio, struck by Bianca at first sight, plans to become her schoolmaster, while Tranio takes his place as Lucentio in Padua. They swap clothes, and Biondello is told a cover story. Above the stage, Sly says it is excellent but wishes it were over.","key":["Katherina is called a devil and fit to be 'carted' before she speaks: her reputation arrives before she does.","Baptista's rule (no Bianca until Katherina is married) drives both plots.","Lucentio's love is instant and bookish, full of classical comparisons, and he starts at once with a disguise."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why has Lucentio come to Padua?","o":["To marry Bianca","To study","To collect a debt","To visit Petruchio"],"a":1},{"q":"What is Baptista's rule about his daughters?","o":["Neither may marry a stranger","Bianca may not marry until Katherina has a husband","Katherina must marry Gremio","Both must marry on the same day"],"a":1},{"q":"What disguise does Lucentio plan?","o":["A music teacher","A schoolmaster for Bianca","A merchant from Pisa","A priest"],"a":1}]},"a1s2":{"sum":"Petruchio arrives from Verona with his servant Grumio and gets into a comic quarrel over the words 'knock me here'. He tells his friend Hortensio that his father has died and he has come 'to wive it wealthily in Padua'. Hortensio half-jokingly mentions rich, shrewish Katherina, and Petruchio decides at once to woo her. Hortensio asks to be presented to Baptista disguised as a music teacher so he can court Bianca. Gremio arrives with Lucentio, disguised as a scholar he means to send to Bianca. Then Tranio, dressed as Lucentio, announces himself as another suitor for Bianca. The suitors agree to share the cost of Petruchio's wooing, and all go off to drink together.","key":["Petruchio is open about wanting money: 'If wealthily, then happily'.","His speech about lions, the sea and cannon shows he sees Katherina as a challenge to be beaten, not a person to be known.","Three men are now in disguise or about to be (Lucentio, Tranio, Hortensio), and Gremio is unknowingly hiring his own rival."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why has Petruchio come to Padua?","o":["To study at the university","To find a rich wife","To fight a duel","To sell horses"],"a":1},{"q":"What disguise does Hortensio plan?","o":["A scholar of Latin","A music teacher","A tailor","A servant"],"a":1},{"q":"Who arrives claiming to be Lucentio?","o":["Biondello","Grumio","Tranio","The Pedant"],"a":2}]},"a2s1":{"sum":"Katherina has tied Bianca's hands and demands to know which suitor she loves. Baptista rescues Bianca, and Katherina accuses him of favouring her sister. The suitors arrive: Petruchio presents Hortensio as Licio, a music teacher, Gremio presents Lucentio as Cambio, a scholar, and Tranio, as Lucentio, offers gifts and asks to court Bianca. Petruchio settles the dowry with Baptista. Hortensio returns with a broken lute round his head. Alone with Katherina, Petruchio woos her in a fast battle of puns; she strikes him, and he tells her he will marry her 'will you, nill you'. He tells Baptista they have agreed to marry on Sunday. Baptista then auctions Bianca: Tranio outbids Gremio, but must have his father guarantee the offer.","key":["Katherina's jealousy of her sister shows a fear of being left unmarried: 'I must dance barefoot on her wedding day'.","The wooing is a battle of wit: Petruchio matches her pun for pun and then simply denies what she says.","Both daughters are bargained for in money: a dowry for Katherina, the highest bid for Bianca."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Katherina do to Hortensio during the music lesson?","o":["Locks him out","Breaks the lute over his head","Throws his music in the fire","Bites his hand"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Petruchio tell Baptista after meeting Katherina alone?","o":["She has refused him","They will marry on Sunday","He has changed his mind","She wants a long engagement"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Tranio beat Gremio in the bidding for Bianca?","o":["He offers his father's ships and twice whatever Gremio offers","He offers to live in Padua","He promises to pay Gremio's debts","He challenges Gremio to a duel"],"a":0}]},"a3s1":{"sum":"Lucentio (as Cambio) and Hortensio (as Licio) argue over who should teach Bianca first. She decides for herself, sends Hortensio to tune his lute, and lets Lucentio give his Latin lesson. In a mock translation of a line of Ovid he tells her who he really is. She answers in the same way that she does not trust him yet, but should not despair. Hortensio offers her a written 'gamut' that pleads his love; she rejects it. A servant calls her to help prepare for Katherina's wedding the next day. Alone, Hortensio suspects 'Cambio' and vows to change his love if Bianca strays.","key":["Bianca is not as meek as her reputation: she takes charge of her own lessons and refuses to be 'a breeching scholar'.","Love is conducted through disguise and code, a contrast to Petruchio's open bargaining.","Hortensio's jealousy prepares for his decision to give her up in Act 4."],"quiz":[{"q":"How does Lucentio reveal himself to Bianca?","o":["In a letter","Through a false translation of a Latin passage","By taking off his disguise","In a song"],"a":1},{"q":"What is written in Hortensio's 'gamut'?","o":["A love message hidden in a musical scale","His real name and address","A poem about Katherina","The price of the lute"],"a":0},{"q":"What interrupts the lessons?","o":["Gremio arrives","A servant calls Bianca to prepare for the wedding","Katherina breaks in","Baptista dismisses the tutors"],"a":1}]},"a3s2":{"sum":"On the wedding day Petruchio is late, and Katherina weeps with shame. Biondello reports him coming in a ragged, mismatched outfit on a diseased old horse. Petruchio arrives and refuses to change: 'To me she's married, not unto my clothes.' Tranio tells Lucentio he will find a man to pose as his father. Gremio describes the wedding: Petruchio swore, cuffed the priest, threw the wine-soaked bread in the sexton's face and kissed the bride with a loud smack. Back from church, Petruchio announces they must leave before the feast. Katherina refuses to go, but he declares her his property and carries her off, pretending to defend her from thieves. Baptista sets Lucentio and Bianca in the couple's places at the feast.","key":["Petruchio humiliates Katherina in public, starting with the way he dresses, and turns her own temper against her.","His speech 'She is my goods, my chattels' states the legal view of a wife as a husband's property.","Katherina tries to rule the moment ('I will be angry') but is physically removed from her own wedding feast."],"quiz":[{"q":"Why is Katherina in tears at the start of the scene?","o":["Bianca has insulted her","Petruchio is late for the wedding","Her father has disowned her","She has lost her dowry"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Petruchio do to the priest during the service?","o":["Pays him double","Strikes him","Locks him out","Asks him to hurry"],"a":1},{"q":"What happens when Katherina refuses to leave before the feast?","o":["Petruchio agrees to stay","Petruchio leaves alone","Petruchio claims her as his property and carries her off","Baptista orders her to go"],"a":2}]},"a4s1":{"sum":"Grumio arrives first at Petruchio's cold country house and tells Curtis about the journey: Katherina's horse fell on her in the mud, Petruchio beat Grumio for it, and Katherina waded through the mud to pull him off. Petruchio and Katherina arrive; he curses the servants, strikes one, calls the mutton burnt and throws the supper at them. He takes Katherina to bed without food. Curtis reports that he is preaching her a sermon on self-control. Alone, Petruchio explains his method: like a falconer with a wild hawk, he will keep her hungry and awake, pretending everything is done out of love, to 'kill a wife with kindness'.","key":["Petruchio shows Katherina her own violent temper in himself: Peter says 'He kills her in her own humour'.","For the first time Katherina pleads for others ('Patience, I pray you') rather than attacking.","The falconry soliloquy states the method openly, in the language of training an animal."],"quiz":[{"q":"What happened on the journey, according to Grumio?","o":["They were robbed","Katherina's horse fell on her in the mud","Petruchio fell into a river","They lost their way"],"a":1},{"q":"Why does Petruchio refuse the supper?","o":["He says it is burnt and will make them angry","It is poisoned","It is not his favourite","Katherina refuses to eat first"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Petruchio compare Katherina to in his soliloquy?","o":["A wild horse","A falcon to be trained","A caged bird","A stubborn ox"],"a":1}]},"a4s2":{"sum":"In Padua, Hortensio shows Tranio (as Lucentio) that Bianca is kissing her tutor 'Cambio'. Hortensio reveals his identity and both swear to give her up; Hortensio will marry a rich widow within three days. Tranio tells Bianca and Lucentio, and jokes that Hortensio has gone to Petruchio's 'taming-school'. Biondello spots an old man coming down the hill. Tranio tells the traveller, a schoolmaster from Mantua, that Mantuans in Padua face death, and offers to protect him if he poses as Vincentio, Lucentio's father.","key":["Tranio cleverly removes a rival by pretending to be outraged himself.","The 'taming-school' joke shows that Petruchio's methods have become famous.","A second false father joins the crowd of impostors."],"quiz":[{"q":"What makes Hortensio give up Bianca?","o":["Baptista refuses him","He sees her kissing 'Cambio'","Gremio wins her","He falls for Katherina"],"a":1},{"q":"Whom does Hortensio decide to marry?","o":["A rich widow","Katherina","A merchant's daughter","Nobody"],"a":0},{"q":"How does Tranio persuade the Pedant to help?","o":["He pays him","He says Mantuans in Padua face death and offers protection","He threatens him","He promises him Bianca"],"a":1}]},"a4s3":{"sum":"Katherina, starving and sleepless, begs Grumio for food; he teases her with names of dishes and then refuses each. Petruchio arrives with meat and makes her thank him. He invites a tailor and haberdasher to dress her for her father's house, then rejects the cap ('When you are gentle, you shall have one too') and the gown, while Katherina insists on her right to speak her mind. Grumio and the tailor quarrel over the order. Petruchio quietly has Hortensio promise the tailor his pay. He says they will go to Baptista's in their plain clothes, since 'tis the mind that makes the body rich'. When Katherina corrects him about the time of day, he refuses to leave until she agrees it is whatever time he says.","key":["Katherina's speech 'My tongue will tell the anger of my heart' is one of her strongest claims to freedom of speech.","Petruchio again denies her something she wants, this time fashion and status, while claiming to protect her.","The quarrel over the time sets up the test of the sun and moon in 4.5."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Grumio do when Katherina asks for food?","o":["Brings her bread","Offers dishes and then refuses each one","Tells Petruchio","Gives her his own dinner"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Petruchio say about the cap?","o":["It is too small and silly","It is too expensive","It is the wrong colour","It is perfect"],"a":0},{"q":"Why does Petruchio refuse to leave for Padua?","o":["The horses are lame","Katherina has contradicted him about the time","The tailor has not been paid","It is raining"],"a":1}]},"a4s4":{"sum":"Tranio brings the Pedant, dressed as Vincentio, to Baptista. The Pedant agrees to the marriage of 'Lucentio' and Bianca and promises a proper settlement. Baptista agrees, and they decide to sign the contract privately at Tranio's lodging, sending 'Cambio' to fetch Bianca. Biondello explains to Lucentio that while the fathers are busy with the false contract, a priest at Saint Luke's church is ready to marry him and Bianca in secret.","key":["Baptista is fooled because the false father says exactly what he wants to hear about money.","The real lovers use the fathers' business as cover to marry for love.","Lucentio, who has spent the play in disguise, now acts in his own name."],"quiz":[{"q":"Where will the marriage contract be signed?","o":["In Baptista's house","At the church","At Tranio's lodging","At the Pegasus inn"],"a":2},{"q":"What does Biondello tell Lucentio to do?","o":["Run back to Pisa","Marry Bianca secretly at Saint Luke's church","Confess to Baptista","Challenge Tranio"],"a":1},{"q":"Why is Baptista's house unsuitable for the contract?","o":["It is being repaired","Servants might overhear and Gremio might interrupt","Katherina is there","It is too small"],"a":1}]},"a4s5":{"sum":"On the road to Padua, Petruchio says the moon is shining. When Katherina says it is the sun, he threatens to turn back. Advised by Hortensio, she agrees it is the moon, then the sun, or whatever he chooses to call it. They meet an old man, and Petruchio greets him as a beautiful young girl. Katherina joins in, then apologises for her mistake when Petruchio corrects her. The old man is Vincentio, travelling to see his son. Petruchio tells him that Lucentio has married Bianca. Hortensio, encouraged, goes off to his widow.","key":["Katherina gives in, but with energy and humour: she outdoes Petruchio's praise of the 'virgin'.","Some readers see a shared game here; others see her spirit broken.","Petruchio's news about Lucentio's marriage is premature, which adds to the confusion of 5.1."],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Petruchio insist is shining?","o":["The stars","The moon","A candle","The sun"],"a":1},{"q":"How does Katherina greet the old man?","o":["As her father","As a young budding virgin","As a beggar","As a priest"],"a":1},{"q":"Who is the old man?","o":["Gremio","Vincentio, Lucentio's father","The Pedant","Baptista's brother"],"a":1}]},"a5s1":{"sum":"Lucentio and Bianca hurry off to church. Petruchio brings Vincentio to Lucentio's house, where the Pedant, at the window, claims to be Vincentio and calls the real one an impostor. Biondello pretends not to know his old master and is beaten. Tranio, in Lucentio's clothes, has Vincentio arrested as a madman. Gremio half defends him but will not swear to it. Then Lucentio and Bianca return married and kneel for pardon; the impostors run away. Lucentio explains everything. Vincentio and Baptista go in, angry but calming. Katherina asks Petruchio to follow; first he demands a kiss in the street, and when she hesitates, she gives it.","key":["The disguises collapse at once when the real father arrives: no clothes can hide who someone really is.","A servant dressed as a master nearly sends a master to prison, which shows how unstable rank can be.","The kiss in the street is gentler than any earlier exchange between Katherina and Petruchio: 'Better once than never'."],"quiz":[{"q":"Who claims to be Vincentio from the window?","o":["Tranio","The Pedant","Biondello","Gremio"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Tranio try to do to the real Vincentio?","o":["Have him sent to prison","Pay him off","Hide him","Send him back to Pisa"],"a":0},{"q":"What does Petruchio ask Katherina for before they follow the others?","o":["Her ring","A kiss in the street","An apology","Her purse"],"a":1}]},"a5s2":{"sum":"At Lucentio's banquet the three couples sit together. Petruchio and the Widow trade jokes, and Katherina quarrels with the Widow, until Bianca leads the women out. Tranio teases Petruchio about his shrew, so Petruchio proposes a wager: each husband will send for his wife, and the one whose wife comes first wins. They bet a hundred crowns. Bianca says she is busy; the Widow says Hortensio should come to her; Katherina comes at once. Sent back, she brings the other wives in, and on Petruchio's order throws her cap under her foot. Then, at his command, she lectures them at length on a wife's duty, ending with an offer to place her hand under her husband's foot. Petruchio claims his kiss and takes her to bed. Hortensio and Lucentio wonder at it.","key":["The ending reverses the beginning: Katherina obeys and Bianca, the 'mild' sister, refuses.","Katherina's speech is the longest in the play; whether it is sincere, ironic or a performance is the play's central debate.","The final line ('by your leave, she will be tamed so') leaves a note of doubt, and Sly never returns to close the frame."],"quiz":[{"q":"What is the wager about?","o":["Whose wife is the best cook","Whose wife comes first when sent for","Who can drink most","Who is richest"],"a":1},{"q":"What answer does Bianca send?","o":["She will come at once","She is busy and cannot come","She is ill","She has gone home"],"a":1},{"q":"What does Katherina offer at the end of her speech?","o":["To leave Petruchio","To put her hand beneath her husband's foot","To pay the wager","To teach the other wives"],"a":1}]}},"notes":{"4":"Sly mangles his history ('Richard Conqueror' for William) and his Spanish ('paucas pallabris' for pocas palabras, few words). He is a comic, very English figure.","9":"A third-borough was a parish constable. Sly's joke about 'fourth or fifth borough' shows he is unafraid and very drunk.","12":"The Lord cares more for his hounds than for people: notice how he talks about dogs with a breeder's expertise before he notices the man on the ground.","18":"'Would not the beggar then forget himself?' asks the play's question: are we what we are, or what others treat us as?","21":"The Lord directs the joke like a play, with props, costumes, music and lines for his actors. The Induction is about theatre as much as about Sly.","43":"Boys played all women's parts on Shakespeare's stage, so the audience watches a boy actor playing a page playing a lady. The Lord's description of a dutiful wife foreshadows Katherina's last speech.","53":"Sly speaks in prose, the language of ordinary people. The servants answer in verse.","55":"Burton-heath and Wincot are real places near Stratford-upon-Avon, Shakespeare's home. Marian Hacket and her alehouse may have been real too.","67":"Sly switches to verse, the language of lords, the moment he hears he has a beautiful wife. He becomes what he is told he is.","91":"The page's excuse (doctors' orders to keep away from his bed) mirrors Petruchio's later excuses about food and sleep: denial dressed up as care.","102":"Lucentio speaks of study and virtue in elaborate verse; he will abandon philosophy within a hundred lines when he sees Bianca.","103":"Tranio's advice ('No profit grows where is no pleasure ta'en') is the comedy's own motto. Notice the servant is wittier than the master.","109":"To 'cart' a woman was to drive her through the streets in a cart as a public punishment for being a scold or a prostitute. Gremio's pun is a cruel insult.","110":"Katherina's first words are a protest about being made a 'stale' (a laughing stock, also a decoy) to win Bianca suitors. She sees exactly how she is being used.","112":"Katherina answers the insult in rhyming couplets, with a threat of violence. Her wit is as quick as her temper.","119":"Katherina mocks Bianca as a 'pretty peat' (a spoiled pet) who could cry on purpose if she wanted. She thinks her sister's meekness is an act.","120":"Bianca's humble words are spoken in front of her suitors. Later scenes suggest she knows how to perform obedience.","128":"Katherina refuses to be given a timetable like a servant or a child. Her complaint is about being controlled.","143":"Lucentio compares Tranio to Anna, sister and confidante of Dido, Queen of Carthage, in Virgil's Aeneid. His love comes straight from books.","147":"The 'daughter of Agenor' is Europa, whom Jove carried off to Crete disguised as a bull. A god who disguised himself for love is a fitting model for Lucentio.","159":"Master and servant swap places. This reversal of rank is one of the play's running jokes, from Sly upwards.","174":"This is the last time Sly speaks. In the anonymous play The Taming of a Shrew he comments throughout and closes the play, but here the frame is never completed.","185":"The 'knock me here' muddle depends on a double meaning: knock at the gate for me, or hit me. Grumio may be deliberately misunderstanding.","200":"Petruchio gives his reasons plainly: his father is dead, he has money, and he wants more. 'To wive and thrive' links marriage and profit in one breath.","202":"Florentius married an ugly old hag to learn a riddle's answer (the story is told by the medieval poet John Gower). Xanthippe, wife of Socrates, was famous as a scold. Petruchio will take any woman if she is rich.","205":"'Board her' means both woo her and attack her like a ship. Petruchio's images of love are images of conquest.","209":"Katherine 'the curst' means bad-tempered or shrewish. The title is given by the men in Padua, and it sticks.","235":"Petruchio's catalogue of noise (lions, storms, cannon, battle) turns wooing into war. The final 'Tush, tush! fear boys with bugs' (scare children with bogeymen) dismisses a woman's tongue as nothing.","258":"Leda's daughter is Helen of Troy, who had countless suitors. Paris won her by abduction: a hint of how rivalry over a woman can end.","263":"Tranio, the disguised servant, sums up the two sisters in two neat lines: one known for a scolding tongue, the other for beautiful modesty.","275":"The sisters' quarrel is the only scene they share alone. Bianca offers to obey her elder sister in everything, which may be sincere or provoking.","290":"To 'lead apes in hell' was the proverbial fate of women who died unmarried. Katherina fears being left behind while her favoured sister marries.","325":"Petruchio's image of two raging fires suggests he sees himself as Katherina's equal, and will win by being even more extreme.","334":"Frets are the bars on a lute's neck; to fret also means to be annoyed. Katherina's pun becomes a physical joke.","339":"Petruchio's plan is to contradict whatever Katherina does by describing the opposite. He tells the audience before he does it, so we watch the method at work.","343":"Petruchio says 'Kate' eleven times in ten lines, renaming her as he likes. 'Cates' were delicacies, so he makes her name a dish.","348":"The wooing is a fast exchange of stichomythia (alternating single lines), full of puns on bearing, burdens, buzzing and stings. Katherina is his equal in wit.","365":"Petruchio's 'tongue in your tail' is a coarse sexual joke, and Katherina strikes him for it.","369":"Katherina turns the idea of a gentleman against him: a gentleman who hits a woman loses his coat of arms.","388":"Petruchio's praise is ironic: he describes Katherina as everything she has just shown she is not, and then invents a limp to deny it.","396":"'Will you, nill you, I will marry you' (whether you are willing or not). He moves from 'thou' to the more distant 'you' as he states the business terms.","403":"Griselda was a legend of wifely patience; Lucrece of chastity. Petruchio's ideal of a wife is all patience and silence.","407":"Petruchio invents a private agreement and a love scene that never happened. Katherina does not contradict him: her silence here is one of the play's puzzles.","410":"'Kiss me, Kate' is the play's most famous line, later used as the title of Cole Porter's 1948 musical based on the play.","423":"Gremio's list of goods (tapestries, plate, cushions, cows) shows Bianca's hand being sold at auction. Notice the sly condition: 'If whilst I live she will be only mine'.","437":"'Faced it with a card of ten' means bluffed with a low card. Tranio has offered riches he does not have.","450":"The Latin lines come from Ovid's Heroides, letters of abandoned women. Lucentio uses them as a code to tell Bianca who he is.","458":"Bianca 'translates' the same Latin into a reply. She is in control: she warns, holds back and encourages at once.","470":"The gamut is the musical scale (G, A, B, C, D, E), with the old sol-fa names. Hortensio hides a love letter in it, but Bianca dismisses it as an odd new invention.","482":"Katherina's fear is public shame: that Petruchio wooed her as a joke and will leave her a laughing stock. It shows how much a woman's reputation depended on marriage.","498":"Biondello's list of the horse's diseases (glanders, lampass, spavins, staggers and more) is a comic catalogue of ruin. The outfit is a deliberate insult to the wedding.","523":"'To me she's married, not unto my clothes' raises a serious point under the joke: what matters is the person, not appearance.","539":"Gremio's report means we never see the wedding itself. Petruchio's behaviour in church parodies Katherina's own violence.","558":"Katherina makes her last stand of open defiance. Notice how she appeals to the guests and her father to support her.","563":"'She is my goods, my chattels' uses the language of the Tenth Commandment, which lists a man's wife among his property. Petruchio treats her leaving as a rescue from thieves, a performance staged for the crowd.","570":"'Petruchio is Kated' means he has caught Katherina's disease. Bianca and Gremio think both are mad.","584":"Grumio's 'winter tames man, woman, and beast' is a comic version of the main plot.","606":"We hear about the journey only in Grumio's story, which he refuses to finish. The image of Katherina wading through mud to save a servant shows a new side to her.","644":"Katherina's first gentle words in the play are spoken in defence of a servant.","653":"Choler (anger) was thought to be produced by hot, dry food such as overcooked meat. Petruchio uses medical theory as an excuse to starve her.","656":"Peter's 'He kills her in her own humour' explains the method: Petruchio out-shrews the shrew.","662":"Falconry terms: a haggard is a wild adult hawk; to 'stoop' is to dive to the lure; to 'watch' is to keep awake; 'bate and beat' is to flap the wings in resistance. Katherina is described as a hunting bird to be trained.","678":"Hortensio drops his disguise in disgust. Notice that he calls the man Bianca loves a 'cullion' (base fellow), not knowing he is a gentleman.","694":"Tranio's 'taming-school' makes Petruchio's methods into a lesson that other men can learn, which is how Hortensio will use them.","710":"Tranio invents the death sentence on the spot. His quick lies show how easily authority can be faked.","725":"Katherina compares herself to the beggars at her father's door. Her speech is the first time we hear her alone with her thoughts, and she is starving.","761":"Katherina insists on her right to speak: 'My tongue will tell the anger of my heart, / Or else my heart, concealing it, will break.' It is one of her most sympathetic speeches.","772":"Petruchio's insults turn the tailor into his tools and measures: thread, thimble, yard, nail. The scene is played for farce.","800":"Grumio's 'take up my mistress' gown' is a bawdy pun: lift up, rather than take back.","806":"Petruchio's speech on honour shining through mean clothes echoes his wedding outfit. Is he teaching a real lesson about appearance, or just denying her a gown?","808":"'It shall be what o'clock I say it is.' Petruchio claims power even over time; Hortensio's reply says he would like to command the sun.","866":"Katherina's surrender is full of wit: she turns the joke back on him by saying the moon 'changes even as your mind'.","868":"In the game of bowls, the bowl is weighted (the bias) so that it curves. Petruchio means Katherina is now running the way he wants.","872":"Katherina throws herself into the game, with more flowery praise than Petruchio used. Is this obedience, or a shared joke?","928":"Vincentio is horrified to see his servant in a silk doublet and copatain (tall, pointed) hat. Clothes show rank, and Tranio has been dressing above his.","957":"'Counterfeit supposes' is a nod to Supposes, George Gascoigne's 1566 comedy, which supplied the Bianca plot.","981":"'Better once than never, for never too late' is a proverb turned round. The kiss is the gentlest moment between them.","985":"Lucentio's welcome opens with an image of music: 'our jarring notes agree'. The last scene tests whether they really do.","1020":"Bianca, the quiet sister, is quick with sexual jokes about birds and bows at the feast, and leads the women out.","1029":"The wager sets the three marriages side by side, as a contest of obedience.","1071":"Baptista pays a second dowry 'for she is changed, as she had never been'. Even the change in Katherina is measured in money.","1085":"Katherina's speech is 44 lines long. It compares a wife's duty to a subject's duty to a prince, a common idea in sermons such as the Elizabethan homily on marriage. Productions play it sincerely, ironically, wearily or as a private joke between husband and wife.","1090":"'You hit the white' puns on Bianca's name, which means white in Italian, and on the white centre of an archery target.","1093":"The play ends on a doubt: Lucentio calls it 'a wonder' that she will be tamed so."},"gloss":{"pheeze":"fix, settle your hash","stocks":"a wooden frame for locking up offenders' feet; also wooden blocks, dull people","paucas pallabris":"few words (garbled Spanish)","Sessa":"be quiet, shut up","denier":"a tiny French coin","third-borough":"a constable","brach":"a female hound","emboss'd":"foaming from exhaustion","fault":"a break in the scent","practise on":"play a trick on","dulcet":"sweet","ewer":"a jug of water for washing","diaper":"a towel","husbanded":"managed","modesty":"restraint","Belike":"probably","buttery":"pantry, store room for food and drink","antick":"clown","obeisance":"a bow or curtsy","sack":"sherry, a Spanish wine","conserves":"preserved fruit","raiment":"clothes","doublets":"close-fitting jackets","bestraught":"distracted, mad","bear-herd":"a man who keeps a performing bear","on the score":"owing, on credit","Semiramis":"a legendary queen of Assyria, famed for lust","welkin":"the sky","Cytherea":"Venus, goddess of love","sedges":"reeds","leet":"a local manor court","goodman":"husband","commonty":"comedy (Sly's mistake)","gambold":"a frolic, a romp","nursery of arts":"Padua, famous for its university","Mi perdonato":"pardon me (Italian)","stoics":"followers of a philosophy that rejects pleasure","Balk logic":"argue logic","importune":"pester","cart":"punish by driving through the streets in a cart","stale":"a laughing stock; a decoy","mates":"low fellows","noddle":"head","froward":"stubborn, contrary","peat":"a pet, a spoiled child","mew her up":"shut her up, like a hawk in a cage","dam":"mother","dole":"lot, fortune","Redime te captum quam queas minimo":"buy yourself out of captivity as cheaply as you can (Latin)","curst":"bad-tempered, shrewish","shrewd":"sharp-tongued, shrewish","Basta":"enough (Italian)","Uncase thee":"undress","descried":"seen, spotted","rebused":"(Grumio's mistake for) abused","pate":"head","Con tutto il cuore ben trovato":"with all my heart, well met (Italian)","Alla nostra casa ben venuto; molto honorato signor mio Petruchio":"welcome to our house, my much honoured Signior Petruchio (Italian)","'leges":"alleges","two-and-thirty, a pip out":"past it, a little drunk (from a card game)","aglet-baby":"a tiny figure on the metal tag of a lace","trot":"hag","rope-tricks":"tricks deserving the hangman's rope (rhetoric)","woodcock":"a fool (the bird was thought stupid)","bags":"moneybags","ordnance":"cannon","'larums":"calls to arms","bugs":"bogeymen","brave":"finely dressed","jade":"a worn-out horse","Alcides":"Hercules","contrive":"pass the time","quaff carouses":"drink toasts","ben venuto":"welcome; host (Italian)","gawds":"trinkets","Minion":"hussy","hilding":"a good-for-nothing","lead apes in hell":"the fate of women who die unmarried","Backare":"stand back","Cunning":"skilful","dower":"the money given to a bride","specialities":"special contracts","fiddler":"a contemptuous word for a musician","twangling Jack":"a twanging fool","Kate Hall":"a joke grand house for Kate","moveable":"a piece of furniture","joint-stool":"a wooden stool","buzzard":"a useless hawk; a fool","turtle":"turtle-dove","craven":"a cock that will not fight","crab":"crab apple, a sour wild apple","coxcomb":"a fool's cap, shaped like a cock's crest","halt":"limp","Dian":"Diana, goddess of chastity and hunting","extempore":"improvised","will you, nill you":"whether you like it or not","Grissel":"Griselda, a legendary patient wife","Lucrece":"a Roman wife famous for her chastity","meacock":"cowardly, feeble","mart":"bargain, market","Tyrian":"purple or crimson","counterpoints":"quilts","Valance":"bed fringe","milch-kine":"milking cows","argosy":"a large merchant ship","galliasses":"large galleys","jointure":"the property settled on a wife if her husband dies","cavil":"a quibble","card of ten":"a low card used to bluff","breeching scholar":"a schoolboy who can be whipped","Pedascule":"little pedant (mock Latin)","gamut":"the musical scale","nice":"fussy","rudesby":"a rude fellow","spleen":"bad temper","jerkin":"a short jacket","chapeless":"without the metal tip of its sheath","glanders":"a horse disease causing swollen glands","spavins":"a swelling of a horse's leg joints","staggers":"a disease that makes a horse stagger","bots":"worms","crupper":"a strap under a horse's tail","lackey":"a footman","caparisoned":"decked out","kersey":"coarse woollen cloth","accoutrements":"outfit, clothes","digress":"turn aside, break my word","gogs-wouns":"God's wounds, an oath","muscadel":"a sweet wine","sops":"pieces of cake or bread soaked in wine","sexton":"church caretaker","domineer":"feast riotously","chattels":"movable property","buckler":"shield","junkets":"sweets, delicacies","jades":"worn-out horses","ray'd":"dirtied","fustian":"coarse cloth","Imprimis":"firstly","bemoiled":"covered in mud","logger-headed":"blockheaded","malt-horse":"a heavy brewer's horse, a dull drudge","choler":"anger, caused by too much bile","continency":"self-control","stoop":"dive down to the lure","lure":"a bundle of feathers used to call back a hawk","man":"tame (a hawk)","kites":"birds of prey","bate":"flap the wings and try to fly off the fist","cullion":"a base fellow","haggard":"a wild hawk; a wild woman","mercatante":"merchant (Italian)","pedant":"schoolmaster","neat's foot":"a calf's or ox's foot","amort":"downcast","farthingales":"hooped petticoats","porringer":"a soup bowl","custard-coffin":"a pie crust","demi-cannon":"a large cannon","censer":"an incense burner with a perforated lid","kennel":"gutter","nail":"a measure of cloth, one sixteenth of a yard","be-mete":"measure; beat","bottom":"a reel of thread","mete-yard":"a measuring stick","habiliments":"clothes","rush-candle":"a cheap candle made of a rush dipped in fat","bias":"the weight in a bowl that makes it curve","bedazzled":"dazzled","cony-catched":"cheated","copatain hat":"a tall, pointed hat","crack-hemp":"gallows-bird, one who will be hanged","supposes":"pretences, impostures","blear'd":"dimmed, deceived","eyne":"eyes","gird":"a jibe","gall'd":"rubbed sore, hurt","holidame":"holiness; by Our Lady","vail your stomachs":"lower your pride","sped":"done for","the white":"the centre of an archery target"}};
const PARTS={"21":[[0,7,"Just like a flattering dream or an empty fantasy. Then pick him up and carry the joke off well. Carry him gently to my finest bedroom and hang it all round with my sexiest pictures. Bathe his filthy head in warm perfumed water and burn sweet-smelling wood to scent the room. Have music ready for when he wakes, to make a sweet and heavenly sound."],[8,14,"And if he happens to speak, be ready at once to bow low and humbly and say, \"What does your honour command?\" Let one of you wait on him with a silver basin full of rose-water scattered with flowers, another carry the jug, a third a towel, and say, \"Would your lordship like to cool your hands?\""],[15,21,"Have someone ready with an expensive suit of clothes to ask him what he wants to wear. Have another tell him about his hounds and horse, and that his lady is grieving over his illness. Convince him he's been mad, and when he says he is himself, say he's dreaming, because he is nothing other than a mighty lord."],[22,24,"Do this, and do it naturally, good sirs. It'll be wonderful entertainment if it's handled with restraint."]],"39":[[0,3,"That's right; you were excellent in it. Well, you've come at just the right moment, especially since I have some fun planned that your skill can help with a great deal."],[4,10,"There is a lord who will hear you perform tonight. But I'm worried about your self-control. If you catch sight of his odd behaviour (because his honour has never seen a play), you might burst out laughing and offend him. I warn you, sirs, if you smile he'll lose his temper."]],"43":[[0,9,"You there, go to my page Bartholomew and have him dressed in every detail like a lady. When that's done, take him to the drunkard's room, call him \"madam\" and bow to him. Tell him from me that, if he wants my favour, he must behave with dignity, the way he has seen noble ladies behave towards their husbands. Let him show the drunkard that kind of respect, with a soft, low voice and humble curtsies,"],[10,18,"and say, \"What does your honour command, so that your lady and humble wife can show her duty and her love?\" And then, with warm hugs and tempting kisses, laying his head on his chest, he must shed tears, as if overjoyed to see his noble husband restored to health, after seven years of thinking himself nothing but a poor, disgusting beggar."],[19,25,"And if the boy can't cry on command the way women can, an onion will do the trick: wrapped up secretly in a handkerchief, it will force his eyes to water whether he likes it or not. See this done as fast as you can. I'll give you more instructions soon."]],"60":[[0,3,"and twenty caged nightingales are singing. Or would you like to sleep? We'll take you to a couch softer and sweeter than the bed specially made up for the lustful queen Semiramis."],[4,10,"Say you'd like to walk, and we'll strew the ground with flowers. Or would you like to ride? Your horses will be decked out with harness studded with gold and pearl. Do you love hawking? You have hawks that will fly higher than the morning lark. Or would you like to hunt? Your hounds will make the sky ring and draw shrill echoes from the hollow earth."]],"102":[[0,8,"Tranio, because I longed so much to see beautiful Padua, the nursery of the arts, I have arrived in fertile Lombardy, the pleasant garden of great Italy. My father's love and permission have equipped me with his goodwill and your good company, my trusty servant, proven in everything. So let's settle here and perhaps begin a course of learning and clever studies."],[9,15,"Pisa, famous for its serious citizens, gave me my life, and gave life first to my father, Vincentio, a merchant who trades all over the world and comes of the Bentivolii family. It's right that Vincentio's son, brought up in Florence, should fulfil all the hopes people have of him and add good deeds to his good fortune."],[16,23,"So, Tranio, while I'm studying, I'll apply myself to virtue and to the part of philosophy that deals with happiness achieved through virtue. Tell me what you think, because I have left Pisa and come to Padua like a man who leaves a shallow puddle to plunge into the deep sea, and seeks to drink his fill."]],"103":[[0,8,"Pardon me, my good master. I feel exactly the same as you, and I'm glad you're keeping to your resolve to suck the sweets of sweet philosophy. Only, good master, while we admire this virtue and moral discipline, let's not be stoics, or wooden blocks, I beg you, or be so devoted to Aristotle's rules that we give up Ovid's poems of love entirely."],[9,15,"Argue logic with the friends you have, and practise rhetoric in everyday conversation. Use music and poetry to liven you up. As for mathematics and metaphysics, tackle them when you have the appetite for them. You get nothing out of what you don't enjoy. In short, sir, study what you like best."]],"126":[[0,4,"And because I know she takes most delight in music, instruments and poetry, I'll keep schoolmasters in my house fit to teach her while she's young. If you, Hortensio, or you, Signior Gremio, know any such men, recommend them to me."],[5,9,"I'll be very generous to skilled men, and generous in giving my own children a good upbringing. And so, goodbye. Katherina, you can stay, because I have more to talk about with Bianca."]],"143":[[0,6,"Oh, Tranio! Until I found it was true, I never thought it possible or likely. But look: while I stood idly watching, I felt love-in-idleness work on me. And now I'll confess plainly to you, who are as trusted and dear to me as Anna was to her sister Dido, Queen of Carthage:"],[7,10,"Tranio, I burn, I pine, I'll die, Tranio, if I don't win this modest young girl. Advise me, Tranio, because I know you can. Help me, Tranio, because I know you will."]],"159":[[0,7,"Enough, don't worry, because I've got it all worked out. We haven't been seen in any house yet, and nobody can tell from our faces which is servant and which is master. So it follows: you'll be the master, Tranio, in my place, keep house, style and servants as I should. I'll be someone else: a Florentine, a Neapolitan, or some poorer man from Pisa."],[8,11,"It's hatched, and that's how it'll be. Tranio, undress at once and take my coloured hat and cloak. When Biondello comes, he'll wait on you. But first I'll make him promise to hold his tongue."]],"166":[[0,6,"Come here, you. This is no time for jokes, so behave in a way that suits the time. Your fellow Tranio here, to save my life, is putting on my clothes and my appearance, and I've put on his so I can escape. Because in a quarrel since I came ashore, I killed a man, and I'm afraid I was seen."],[7,9,"I order you to wait on him properly while I get away from here to save my life. Do you understand me?"]],"202":[[0,3,"Signior Hortensio, between friends like us, a few words are enough. So, if you know a woman rich enough to be Petruchio's wife (since wealth is the tune I dance my wooing to),"],[4,11,"she could be as ugly as the hag Florentius married, as old as the Sibyl, and as bad-tempered and shrewish as Socrates' wife Xanthippe, or worse: she won't move me, or at least she won't blunt the edge of my desire, even if she were as rough as the swelling Adriatic Sea. I've come to marry wealthily in Padua. If wealthily, then happily in Padua."]],"204":[[0,4,"Petruchio, since we've gone this far, I'll follow up what I started as a joke. I can help you to a wife, Petruchio, with plenty of money, young and beautiful, brought up the way a gentlewoman should be."],[5,9,"Her only fault, and it's fault enough, is that she's unbearably bad-tempered, shrewish and difficult, so far beyond all measure that, even if I were much poorer than I am, I wouldn't marry her for a gold mine."]],"209":[[0,5,"Wait, Petruchio, I must go with you, because my treasure is in Baptista's keeping. He holds the jewel of my life, his youngest daughter, beautiful Bianca, and keeps her from me and others too, who court her and compete with me for her love."],[6,11,"He thinks it impossible, because of the faults I've just described, that Katherina will ever be wooed. So Baptista has given this order: that nobody can visit Bianca until Katherine the shrew has got a husband."]],"217":[[0,4,"Oh, very good. I've read through the list. Listen, sir, I'll have the books very handsomely bound: all books about love, make sure of that, whatever happens, and make sure you don't give her any other lessons. You understand me."],[5,9,"On top of Signior Baptista's generosity, I'll add a tip of my own. Take your papers too, and have them well perfumed, because the lady they're going to is sweeter than perfume itself. What will you read to her?"]],"235":[[0,8,"Why else did I come here but for that? Do you think a little noise can scare my ears? Haven't I in my time heard lions roar? Haven't I heard the sea, whipped up by the winds, rage like an angry boar foaming with sweat? Haven't I heard great cannon on the battlefield and heaven's artillery thundering in the sky? Haven't I heard, in a pitched battle, loud alarms, neighing horses and blaring trumpets?"],[9,12,"And you talk to me about a woman's tongue, which doesn't make half as loud a bang to the ear as a chestnut popping in a farmer's fire? Nonsense! Frighten little boys with bogeymen."]],"258":[[0,5,"Gently, gentlemen! If you're gentlemen, do me the courtesy of hearing me out patiently. Baptista is a noble gentleman who knows something of my father. And even if his daughter were more beautiful than she is, she can have more suitors, with me as one of them."],[6,9,"Beautiful Helen, Leda's daughter, had a thousand suitors, so fair Bianca can easily have one more. And she shall: Lucentio will be one of them, even if Paris himself came hoping to win her alone."]],"299":[[0,7,"You're unfair to me, Signior Gremio. Let me do this. I'm a gentleman of Verona, sir, who has heard of her beauty and her wit, her friendliness and shy modesty, her wonderful qualities and mild behaviour, and so I'm bold enough to come as an eager guest to your house, to see with my own eyes the truth of what I've so often heard."],[8,9,"And as my ticket of entry, I present you with a man of mine,"]],"313":[[0,9,"Forgive me, sir, the boldness is all mine. Though I'm a stranger in this city, I'm offering myself as a suitor to your daughter, to Bianca, beautiful and virtuous. And I know your firm decision that the eldest sister must marry first. All I ask is this permission: that once you know my family, I may be welcome among the other suitors, with the same free access and favour as they have."],[10,13,"And towards your daughters' education, I give you this simple instrument and this small parcel of Greek and Latin books. If you accept them, then they're worth a great deal."]],"334":[[0,6,"Why, no, because she's broken the lute on me. I only told her she'd got her frets wrong and bent her hand to teach her the fingering, when, with a most impatient, devilish temper, she said, \"Frets, you call these? I'll fret and fume with them!\" And with that she hit me on the head, and my head went through the instrument."],[7,11,"And there I stood, dazed for a while, as if in a pillory, looking through the lute, while she called me a rascally fiddler and a twanging fool, with twenty other vile names, as if she'd practised insulting me."]],"339":[[0,8,"I'll wait for her here, and woo her with some spirit when she comes. Suppose she rants: why, then I'll tell her plainly she sings as sweetly as a nightingale. Suppose she frowns: I'll say she looks as fresh as morning roses newly washed with dew. Suppose she's silent and won't say a word: then I'll praise her talkativeness and say she speaks with stirring eloquence."],[9,13,"If she tells me to get out, I'll thank her as if she'd asked me to stay with her a week. If she refuses to marry, I'll ask her which day I should have the banns read, and when we'll be married. But here she comes. And now, Petruchio, speak."]],"343":[[0,5,"You're lying, honestly, because you're called plain Kate, and pretty Kate, and sometimes Kate the shrew. But Kate, the prettiest Kate in Christendom, Kate of Kate Hall, my extra-dainty Kate (because all delicacies are \"cates\", and so, Kate), hear this from me, Kate of my comfort:"],[6,9,"hearing your gentleness praised in every town, your virtues talked about and your beauty proclaimed (though not as highly as you deserve), I've been moved myself to woo you as my wife."]],"388":[[0,9,"No, not a bit. I find you very gentle. I was told you were rough, standoffish and sulky, and now I find the rumours were downright lies. You're pleasant, playful, extremely polite, slow to speak, but sweet as springtime flowers. You can't frown, you can't look sideways at anyone, or bite your lip the way angry girls do, and you don't enjoy arguing. Instead you treat your suitors gently, with mild, soft and friendly talk."],[10,14,"Why do people say Kate limps? Oh, lying world! Kate is as straight and slender as a hazel twig, as brown as hazelnuts and sweeter than their kernels. Oh, let me see you walk. You don't limp."]],"396":[[0,4,"Indeed, that's what I mean to do, sweet Katherine, in your bed. And so, putting all this chatter aside, here it is in plain terms. Your father has agreed that you'll be my wife. Your dowry is agreed. And whether you like it or not, I'll marry you."],[5,11,"Now, Kate, I'm the right husband for you, because, by this daylight in which I see your beauty (the beauty that makes me like you so much), you must marry no man but me. I'm the man born to tame you, Kate, and turn you from a wild Kate into a Kate as obedient as other household cats."]],"407":[[0,8,"Be patient, gentlemen. I'm choosing her for myself. If she and I are happy, what's it to you? We agreed between the two of us, when we were alone, that she would go on being shrewish in company. I tell you, you wouldn't believe how much she loves me. Oh, the kindest Kate! She hung round my neck and piled kiss on kiss so fast, swearing vow after vow, that in a moment she won me to love her."],[9,15,"Oh, you're beginners! It's a wonder to see how tame, when men and women are alone, a feeble coward can make the worst shrew. Give me your hand, Kate. I'll go to Venice to buy clothes for the wedding day. Get the feast ready, father, and invite the guests. I'll make sure my Katherine is finely dressed."]],"423":[[0,10,"First, as you know, my house in the city is richly furnished with silver and gold plate: basins and jugs to wash her dainty hands; my wall-hangings all of crimson Tyrian tapestry. I've stuffed my money into ivory boxes. In cypress-wood chests I keep my woven quilts, expensive clothes, bed-hangings and canopies, fine linen, Turkish cushions studded with pearl, Venetian bed-borders embroidered with gold thread, pewter and brass, and everything that goes with a house and housekeeping."],[11,16,"Then at my farm I have a hundred milking cows, a hundred and twenty fat oxen in my stalls, and everything else in proportion. I'm getting on in years, I must admit, and if I die tomorrow all this is hers, as long as she'll be mine only while I'm alive."]],"482":[[0,5,"The shame is mine alone. I must, indeed, be forced to give my hand against my heart's wishes to a mad, rude, hot-tempered man who wooed in a hurry and means to marry at his leisure. I told you, I did, that he was a raving fool, hiding his bitter jokes behind rough manners."],[6,12,"And to be known as a merry fellow, he'll woo a thousand women, set the wedding day, make friends, invite guests and announce the banns, and yet never mean to marry the woman he's wooed. Now the world will point at poor Katherine and say, \"Look, there's mad Petruchio's wife, if he'd only come and marry her.\""]],"498":[[0,4,"Why, Petruchio is coming in a new hat and an old jacket; a pair of old breeches turned inside out three times; a pair of boots that have been used to keep candle-ends in, one buckled, the other laced; an old rusty sword taken from the town armoury, with a broken handle and no tip to its sheath, and two broken laces. His horse is lame in the hip, with an old moth-eaten saddle and stirrups that don't match."],[5,9,"What's more, it has glanders and is likely to have a rotting spine; it's troubled with swollen gums, infected with farcy, full of swollen leg joints, ruined by spavin, discoloured with jaundice, past any cure for the vives, completely wrecked by staggers, gnawed by worms, sway-backed, with a dislocated shoulder and knock-kneed in front."],[10,14,"It has a half-checked bit and a head-strap of sheep's leather which, having been pulled tight to stop the horse stumbling, has often broken and is now mended with knots; one belly-strap has been patched six times, and it has a woman's velvet crupper, with her two initials neatly set out in studs, and patched here and there with string."]],"528":[[0,4,"But, sir, to her love we need to add her father's approval. To bring that about, as I told your worship before, I'm going to find a man (it doesn't matter much who he is; we'll train him for our purpose),"],[5,9,"and he'll pretend to be Vincentio of Pisa and guarantee here in Padua even bigger sums than I've promised. That way you'll calmly get what you hope for and marry sweet Bianca with her father's consent."]],"541":[[0,9,"She trembled and shook, because he stamped and swore as if the vicar meant to cheat him. But after all the ceremonies were done, he called for wine. \"A toast!\" he said, as if he'd been on board ship drinking with his shipmates after a storm. He gulped down the sweet wine and threw the bread soaked in it right in the sexton's face, for no other reason than that the sexton's beard grew thin and hungry-looking and seemed to be begging him for the bread while he was drinking."],[10,16,"That done, he took the bride round the neck and kissed her lips with such a loud smack that the whole church echoed as they parted. And when I saw this, I left out of sheer embarrassment. And after me, I know, the crowd is coming. There's never been such a mad wedding. Listen, listen! I can hear the musicians playing."]],"563":[[0,7,"They shall go forward, Kate, at your command. Obey the bride, all you who serve her. Go to the feast, celebrate and live it up, drink deep toasts to her virginity, be mad and merry, or go and hang yourselves. But as for my pretty Kate, she must come with me. No, don't look angry, or stamp, or stare, or fret. I'll be master of what's my own."],[8,13,"She's my goods, my possessions. She's my house, my household furniture, my field, my barn, my horse, my ox, my donkey, my anything. And here she stands: let anyone touch her who dares. I'll take the law on the proudest man who blocks my way in Padua."],[14,17,"Grumio, draw your weapon. We're surrounded by thieves. Rescue your mistress, if you're a man. Don't be afraid, sweet girl. They won't touch you, Kate. I'll shield you against a million."]],"662":[[0,8,"So I've begun my reign with cunning, and I hope to end it successfully. My falcon is now keen and very hungry, and until she comes down to the lure she mustn't be fully fed, because then she never looks at the lure. I have another way to tame my wild hawk, to make her come and know her keeper's call: that is, to keep her awake, as we do with those hawks that flap and struggle and won't obey."],[9,17,"She ate no food today, and she won't eat any. Last night she didn't sleep, and tonight she won't either. As with the meat, I'll find some made-up fault in the way the bed is made, and I'll throw the pillow here, the bolster there, the bedspread this way and the sheets that way. Yes, and in all this commotion I'll pretend it's all done out of loving care for her. In the end, she'll stay awake all night."],[18,23,"And if she happens to nod off, I'll rant and shout and keep her awake with the noise. This is the way to kill a wife with kindness, and so I'll curb her mad and headstrong temper. If anyone knows a better way to tame a shrew, let him speak now. It would be a kindness to share it."]],"718":[[0,5,"To save your life in this emergency, I'll do you this favour for his sake, and you'll count it not the worst of your luck that you look like Signior Vincentio. You'll take on his name and reputation, and you'll be put up as a friend in my house."],[6,9,"Make sure you play the part properly! You understand me, sir. You'll stay like that until you've done your business in the city. If this is a kindness, sir, accept it."]],"725":[[0,8,"The more I'm wronged, the more his spite shows. What, did he marry me to starve me? Beggars who come to my father's door get charity straight away when they ask for it; if not, they find charity somewhere else. But I, who never knew how to beg and never needed to, am starved of food, dizzy from lack of sleep, kept awake by swearing and fed on shouting."],[9,14,"And what annoys me more than all I'm going without, he does it in the name of perfect love, as if to say that if I slept or ate it would make me deathly ill, or kill me at once. Please go and get me something to eat. I don't care what, as long as it's wholesome food."]],"751":[[0,4,"Eat it all up, Hortensio, if you love me. Much good may it do your kind heart! Kate, eat quickly. And now, my honey love, we'll go back to your father's house and celebrate as splendidly as anyone,"],[5,10,"with silk coats and caps and golden rings, with ruffs and cuffs and hooped petticoats and things, with scarves and fans and two changes of fine clothes, with amber bracelets, beads and all this frippery. What, have you finished eating? The tailor is waiting for you, to dress your body in his frilly treasures."]],"806":[[0,9,"Well, come on, my Kate. We'll go to your father's in these honest, plain clothes. Our purses will be proud and our clothes poor, because it's the mind that makes the body rich. And as the sun breaks through the darkest clouds, so honour shines through the plainest clothes. What, is the jay more precious than the lark because its feathers are more beautiful? Or is the adder better than the eel because its patterned skin pleases the eye?"],[10,17,"Oh no, good Kate. You aren't any the worse for these poor clothes and plain outfit. If you think it shameful, blame me. So cheer up. We'll leave at once to feast and enjoy ourselves at your father's house. Go and call my men, and let's go to him straight away. Bring our horses to the end of Long Lane. We'll mount there, and walk on foot till then."],[18,19,"Let's see. I think it's about seven o'clock now, and we can easily get there by lunchtime."]],"825":[[0,7,"Gently, son! Sir, with your permission: having come to Padua to collect some debts, my son Lucentio told me about a serious matter of love between your daughter and himself. And because of the good reports I hear of you, and because of the love he has for your daughter and she for him, so as not to keep him waiting too long,"],[8,14,"I'm happy, as a caring father, to see him married. And if you like it no less than I do, then once we reach some agreement you'll find me ready and willing to agree to her being matched with him. I can't be fussy with you, Signior Baptista, when I hear such good things of you."]],"826":[[0,4,"Sir, forgive me for what I have to say. Your frankness and brevity please me. It's quite true that your son Lucentio here loves my daughter, and she loves him, or else they're both very good at hiding their feelings."],[5,9,"And so, if you'll say no more than this, that you'll treat him like a father and guarantee my daughter a sufficient settlement, the match is made and everything is done. Your son will have my daughter with my consent."]],"879":[[0,8,"Happily met, and happier for your son. And now by law, as well as by your respected age, I can call you my loving father. Your son has by now married this lady's sister, my wife's sister. Don't be surprised or upset: she has a good reputation, a rich dowry and good birth, and besides, she has all the qualities fit for the wife of any noble gentleman."],[9,11,"Let me embrace old Vincentio, and let's travel on to see your honest son, who'll be overjoyed at your arrival."]],"985":[[0,4,"At last, after a long time, our clashing notes are in harmony, and now that the raging war is over, it's time to smile at narrow escapes and dangers that have passed. My lovely Bianca, welcome my father, while I welcome yours with the same kindness."],[5,10,"Brother Petruchio, sister Katherina, and you, Hortensio, with your loving widow, enjoy the best food, and welcome to my house. This banquet is a dessert to round off our great feast. Please sit down, because now we're sitting to chat as well as to eat."]],"1085":[[0,9,"Shame, shame! Smooth out that threatening, unkind frown, and don't dart scornful looks from those eyes to wound your lord, your king, your governor. It spoils your beauty as frost nips the meadows, ruins your reputation as whirlwinds shake lovely buds, and is in no way fitting or attractive. An angry woman is like a fountain that has been stirred up: muddy, ugly, thick, robbed of its beauty. And while it's like that, nobody, however dry or thirsty, will stoop to sip or touch a drop of it."],[10,18,"Your husband is your lord, your life, your keeper, your head, your sovereign: someone who cares for you, and to support you puts his body through hard work on sea and land, staying awake at night in storms and by day in the cold, while you lie warm at home, safe and secure. And he asks no other payment from you but love, kind looks and true obedience: too little payment for so great a debt."],[19,28,"The same duty a subject owes his ruler, a woman owes her husband. And when she's stubborn, irritable, sulky, sour, and doesn't obey his honest wishes, what is she but a vile, quarrelling rebel and a wicked traitor to her loving lord? I'm ashamed that women are so foolish as to declare war when they should kneel for peace, or seek to rule, dominate and control, when they're bound to serve, love and obey."],[29,39,"Why are our bodies soft and weak and smooth, unfit for hard work and trouble in the world, unless it's so that our gentle natures and our hearts should match our outward parts? Come, come, you stubborn, feeble worms! My spirit has been as proud as either of yours, my heart as great, my reason perhaps greater, to trade word for word and frown for frown. But now I see our lances are only straws, our strength as weak as straw, our weakness beyond compare, so that we seem to be most what in fact we are least."],[40,43,"So lower your pride, because it's no use, and place your hands beneath your husband's foot. As a sign of that duty, if he wishes, my hand is ready. May it give him pleasure."]]};
const QUOTES={"themes":{"taming":["Taming and power","Hawks, horses and wildcats: Petruchio's campaign to master Katherina, and who really holds power in a marriage."],"marriage":["Marriage and money","Dowries, bids, wagers and widows' settlements: love and marriage measured in crowns."],"voice":["Women's voices and silence","Scolding, silence, obedience and speech: who gets to talk, and what happens when a woman speaks her mind."],"disguise":["Disguise and identity","Masters as servants, servants as masters, a beggar as a lord: can clothes change who you are?"],"wit":["Wit and wordplay","Puns, insults and verbal duels: language as a weapon and a game."],"fathers":["Fathers and children","Baptista and his daughters, Vincentio and Lucentio, and the false fathers in between."],"theatre":["Play-acting and illusion","The Induction, the players and the performances characters put on for each other."],"class":["Masters and servants","Tranio, Grumio, Biondello and Sly: rank turned upside down, and back again."]},"quotes":[{"id":"18","who":"LORD","text":"Would not the beggar then forget himself?","themes":["theatre","disguise","class"],"note":"The Lord's question introduces the idea that people become what they are treated as."},{"id":"19","who":"FIRST HUNTSMAN","text":"Believe me, lord, I think he cannot choose.","themes":["theatre","taming"],"note":"Sly will have no choice about who he is: a hint of Katherina's position later."},{"id":"55","who":"SLY","text":"What! I am not bestraught.","themes":["disguise","class"],"note":"Sly holds on to his real identity, briefly."},{"id":"67","who":"SLY","text":"Am I a lord? and have I such a lady?","themes":["disguise","theatre","marriage"],"note":"The offer of a wife is what makes Sly accept his new identity, and he switches to verse."},{"id":"67","who":"SLY","text":"Upon my life, I am a lord indeed;","themes":["disguise","class"],"note":"Sly is transformed by being told he is a lord, just as Katherina will be transformed by being told she is gentle."},{"id":"83","who":"PAGE","text":"My husband and my lord, my lord and husband; I am your wife in all obedience.","themes":["theatre","voice","marriage"],"note":"A boy plays the perfect obedient wife: obedience is presented as a performance from the start."},{"id":"103","who":"TRANIO","text":"No profit grows where is no pleasure ta'en;","themes":["class","wit"],"note":"The servant gives the master the best advice in the scene."},{"id":"109","who":"GREMIO","text":"To cart her rather: she's too rough for me.","themes":["voice","taming"],"note":"Katherina is publicly shamed by the men before she speaks."},{"id":"110","who":"KATHERINA","text":"To make a stale of me amongst these mates?","themes":["voice","marriage"],"note":"Katherina's first line protests that she is being used as bait to clear the way for her sister."},{"id":"112","who":"KATHERINA","text":"To comb your noddle with a three-legg'd stool,","themes":["voice","wit"],"note":"Her wit is as violent as it is quick."},{"id":"115","who":"TRANIO","text":"That wench is stark mad or wonderful froward.","themes":["voice"],"note":"An outsider's first impression of Katherina."},{"id":"116","who":"LUCENTIO","text":"But in the other's silence do I see Maid's mild behaviour and sobriety.","themes":["voice"],"note":"Bianca is admired for silence, which is exactly what Katherina refuses to give."},{"id":"128","who":"KATHERINA","text":"What! shall I be appointed hours, as though, belike, I knew not what to take and what to leave? Ha!","themes":["voice","fathers"],"note":"Katherina rejects being controlled like a child or a servant."},{"id":"136","who":"GREMIO","text":"any man is so very a fool to be married to hell?","themes":["marriage","voice"],"note":"The suitors speak of Katherina as a devil and of marriage to her as damnation."},{"id":"143","who":"LUCENTIO","text":"Tranio, I burn, I pine, I perish, Tranio,","themes":["marriage","wit"],"note":"Lucentio's love is instant and full of the clichés of romantic poetry."},{"id":"159","who":"LUCENTIO","text":"Thou shalt be master, Tranio, in my stead,","themes":["disguise","class"],"note":"Master and servant swap places in a moment."},{"id":"177","who":"SLY","text":"'Tis a very excellent piece of work, madam lady: would 'twere done!","themes":["theatre"],"note":"Sly's last words in the play are a comic verdict on the play itself."},{"id":"200","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Haply to wive and thrive as best I may;","themes":["marriage"],"note":"Petruchio rhymes marriage with profit."},{"id":"202","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"I come to wive it wealthily in Padua; If wealthily, then happily in Padua.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"The bluntest statement of marriage as a money matter in the play."},{"id":"203","who":"GRUMIO","text":"why, nothing comes amiss, so money comes withal.","themes":["marriage","class"],"note":"The servant exaggerates his master's greed for comic effect."},{"id":"205","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"For I will board her, though she chide as loud As thunder when the clouds in autumn crack.","themes":["taming","wit"],"note":"Wooing as a naval attack."},{"id":"210","who":"GRUMIO","text":"Katherine the curst! A title for a maid of all titles the worst.","themes":["voice"],"note":"The nickname shows how Katherina's reputation defines her."},{"id":"235","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Have I not in my time heard lions roar?","themes":["taming"],"note":"Petruchio treats Katherina as a danger to be faced down, like a lion or a storm."},{"id":"235","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Tush, tush! fear boys with bugs.","themes":["taming","voice"],"note":"He dismisses a woman's tongue as a bogeyman for children."},{"id":"263","who":"TRANIO","text":"The one as famous for a scolding tongue As is the other for beauteous modesty.","themes":["voice"],"note":"The sisters are defined by their reputations: one scolds, one is modest."},{"id":"275","who":"BIANCA","text":"So well I know my duty to my elders.","themes":["voice","fathers"],"note":"Bianca's talk of duty may be sincere, or a way to provoke her sister."},{"id":"290","who":"KATHERINA","text":"She is your treasure, she must have a husband;","themes":["fathers","marriage"],"note":"Katherina believes her father loves Bianca more."},{"id":"290","who":"KATHERINA","text":"And, for your love to her, lead apes in hell.","themes":["fathers","marriage"],"note":"Her fear of dying unmarried lies under her anger."},{"id":"325","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"I am as peremptory as she proud-minded;","themes":["taming"],"note":"Petruchio means to out-match Katherina rather than calm her."},{"id":"325","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"For I am rough and woo not like a babe.","themes":["taming"],"note":"Roughness is his method from the start."},{"id":"339","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Say that she rail; why, then I'll tell her plain She sings as sweetly as a nightingale:","themes":["taming","wit"],"note":"He plans to answer everything she does with its opposite."},{"id":"343","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"You lie, in faith, for you are call'd plain Kate,","themes":["taming","voice"],"note":"Petruchio renames her at once, taking control of her name."},{"id":"349","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Women are made to bear, and so are you.","themes":["wit","marriage"],"note":"A pun on bearing weight and bearing children."},{"id":"358","who":"KATHERINA","text":"If I be waspish, best beware my sting.","themes":["wit","voice"],"note":"Katherina warns him and enjoys the game."},{"id":"382","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Now, by Saint George, I am too young for you.","themes":["wit"],"note":"The duel is fast and evenly matched."},{"id":"396","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"And will you, nill you, I will marry you.","themes":["taming","marriage"],"note":"Her consent is not part of the deal."},{"id":"396","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"For I am he am born to tame you, Kate,","themes":["taming"],"note":"He names his project outright."},{"id":"396","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Conformable as other household Kates.","themes":["taming","voice"],"note":"The aim is a 'conformable' wife, like a household cat."},{"id":"404","who":"KATHERINA","text":"I'll see thee hang'd on Sunday first.","themes":["voice"],"note":"Her last word in the scene before she falls silent."},{"id":"407","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"'Tis bargain'd 'twixt us twain, being alone, That she shall still be curst in company.","themes":["theatre","taming"],"note":"Petruchio invents a private agreement and makes her defiance part of his story."},{"id":"410","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"And kiss me, Kate; we will be married o' Sunday.","themes":["marriage","taming"],"note":"The play's best-known line, spoken as he leaves her with no chance to answer."},{"id":"413","who":"BAPTISTA","text":"And venture madly on a desperate mart.","themes":["marriage","fathers"],"note":"Baptista sees his daughter's marriage as a risky business deal."},{"id":"422","who":"BAPTISTA","text":"'Tis deeds must win the prize, and he of both That can assure my daughter greatest dower Shall have my Bianca's love.","themes":["marriage","fathers"],"note":"Bianca is offered to the highest bidder."},{"id":"437","who":"TRANIO","text":"Yet I have fac'd it with a card of ten.","themes":["disguise","class"],"note":"The servant has bluffed the old suitor with riches he does not have."},{"id":"445","who":"BIANCA","text":"I am no breeching scholar in the schools,","themes":["voice"],"note":"The 'mild' sister insists on her own choices."},{"id":"458","who":"BIANCA","text":"I know you not; hic est Sigeia tellus, I trust you not;","themes":["wit","disguise"],"note":"Bianca matches Lucentio's code and keeps control."},{"id":"482","who":"KATHERINA","text":"Who woo'd in haste and means to wed at leisure.","themes":["voice","marriage"],"note":"Katherina fears public shame if Petruchio does not come."},{"id":"482","who":"KATHERINA","text":"Now must the world point at poor Katherine,","themes":["voice","marriage"],"note":"She depends on the marriage she resisted."},{"id":"523","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"To me she's married, not unto my clothes.","themes":["disguise","marriage"],"note":"A joke with a serious point: a person is more than their clothes."},{"id":"558","who":"KATHERINA","text":"The door is open, sir; there lies your way;","themes":["voice","taming"],"note":"Katherina's last open defiance of Petruchio in public."},{"id":"562","who":"KATHERINA","text":"I see a woman may be made a fool, If she had not a spirit to resist.","themes":["voice","taming"],"note":"She defends a woman's right to resist."},{"id":"563","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"I will be master of what is mine own.","themes":["taming","marriage"],"note":"Marriage makes her his possession in law."},{"id":"563","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"She is my goods, my chattels; she is my house,","themes":["marriage","taming"],"note":"The wife as property, in a list that ends with 'my ass, my anything'."},{"id":"570","who":"GREMIO","text":"I warrant him, Petruchio is Kated.","themes":["wit","taming"],"note":"The onlookers think Petruchio has caught her madness."},{"id":"584","who":"GRUMIO","text":"thou knowest winter tames man, woman, and beast;","themes":["taming","class"],"note":"The servant's comic echo of the main plot."},{"id":"644","who":"KATHERINA","text":"Patience, I pray you; 'twas a fault unwilling.","themes":["voice","taming"],"note":"Katherina's first gentle words, in defence of a servant."},{"id":"653","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"For it engenders choler, planteth anger;","themes":["taming"],"note":"He uses medical theory as an excuse to starve her."},{"id":"656","who":"PETER","text":"He kills her in her own humour.","themes":["taming","class"],"note":"A servant explains the method: Petruchio out-shrews the shrew."},{"id":"662","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Thus have I politicly begun my reign,","themes":["taming"],"note":"He speaks of marriage as a kingdom to be ruled with cunning."},{"id":"662","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"My falcon now is sharp and passing empty.","themes":["taming"],"note":"Katherina as a hawk to be trained by hunger."},{"id":"662","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"This is a way to kill a wife with kindness;","themes":["taming"],"note":"Cruelty is disguised as care."},{"id":"662","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"He that knows better how to tame a shrew, Now let him speak; 'tis charity to show.","themes":["taming","theatre"],"note":"He turns to the audience, inviting us to share or judge his method."},{"id":"694","who":"TRANIO","text":"Ay, mistress; and Petruchio is the master,","themes":["taming"],"note":"The 'taming-school' turns his method into a lesson for other men."},{"id":"716","who":"TRANIO","text":"He is my father, sir; and, sooth to say,","themes":["disguise","fathers"],"note":"The servant invents a father for himself."},{"id":"725","who":"KATHERINA","text":"What, did he marry me to famish me?","themes":["taming","voice"],"note":"Katherina's suffering is real, whatever the comedy around it."},{"id":"725","who":"KATHERINA","text":"He does it under name of perfect love;","themes":["taming"],"note":"She sees through his excuse."},{"id":"759","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"When you are gentle, you shall have one too,","themes":["taming","class"],"note":"'Gentle' means both mild and well-born: he controls even her status."},{"id":"761","who":"KATHERINA","text":"My tongue will tell the anger of my heart, Or else my heart, concealing it, will break;","themes":["voice"],"note":"Her strongest defence of free speech."},{"id":"806","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"For 'tis the mind that makes the body rich;","themes":["disguise","class"],"note":"He argues that worth lies inside, not in fine clothes."},{"id":"808","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"It shall be what o'clock I say it is.","themes":["taming"],"note":"Petruchio claims power even over time."},{"id":"809","who":"HORTENSIO","text":"Why, so this gallant will command the sun.","themes":["taming"],"note":"Hortensio's joke sets up the sun and moon test."},{"id":"866","who":"KATHERINA","text":"And the moon changes even as your mind.","themes":["voice","wit","taming"],"note":"Even in surrender, Katherina has the wittier line."},{"id":"866","who":"KATHERINA","text":"What you will have it nam'd, even that it is,","themes":["taming","voice"],"note":"She gives him the power to name the world."},{"id":"867","who":"HORTENSIO","text":"Petruchio, go thy ways; the field is won.","themes":["taming"],"note":"The taming is described as a battle."},{"id":"872","who":"KATHERINA","text":"Young budding virgin, fair and fresh and sweet,","themes":["theatre","wit","taming"],"note":"Katherina joins the game, perhaps with relish."},{"id":"957","who":"LUCENTIO","text":"While counterfeit supposes blear'd thine eyne.","themes":["disguise","fathers"],"note":"The disguise plot is unravelled."},{"id":"962","who":"LUCENTIO","text":"Love wrought these miracles.","themes":["marriage","disguise"],"note":"Lucentio explains the deception as the work of love."},{"id":"981","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Better once than never, for never too late.","themes":["marriage"],"note":"The kiss in the street suggests a new tenderness."},{"id":"1032","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"I'll venture so much of my hawk or hound, But twenty times so much upon my wife.","themes":["marriage","taming"],"note":"Wives are bet on like animals."},{"id":"1044","who":"BIONDELLO","text":"That she is busy and she cannot come.","themes":["voice","marriage"],"note":"The 'mild' Bianca refuses her husband."},{"id":"1070","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Marry, peace it bodes, and love, and quiet life,","themes":["marriage","taming"],"note":"Petruchio's vision of marriage is order with himself in charge."},{"id":"1071","who":"BAPTISTA","text":"For she is chang'd, as she had never been.","themes":["fathers","taming"],"note":"Her father rewards the change with money."},{"id":"1079","who":"BIANCA","text":"The more fool you for laying on my duty.","themes":["voice","marriage"],"note":"Bianca shows the sharp tongue her sister once had."},{"id":"1085","who":"KATHERINA","text":"Thy husband is thy lord, thy life, thy keeper,","themes":["voice","marriage","taming"],"note":"The heart of Katherina's final speech."},{"id":"1085","who":"KATHERINA","text":"Such duty as the subject owes the prince, Even such a woman oweth to her husband;","themes":["marriage","voice"],"note":"Marriage compared to the state: a common idea in Elizabethan sermons."},{"id":"1085","who":"KATHERINA","text":"My mind hath been as big as one of yours,","themes":["voice"],"note":"She admits her former pride, and perhaps her intelligence."},{"id":"1085","who":"KATHERINA","text":"And place your hands below your husband's foot:","themes":["taming","marriage"],"note":"The final gesture of submission, which productions stage in very different ways."},{"id":"1086","who":"PETRUCHIO","text":"Why, there's a wench! Come on, and kiss me, Kate.","themes":["marriage","taming"],"note":"He repeats 'kiss me, Kate', now as a reward."},{"id":"1093","who":"LUCENTIO","text":"'Tis a wonder, by your leave, she will be tam'd so.","themes":["taming","theatre"],"note":"The last line leaves room for doubt."}]};
const EX={"intro":["A lord plays a trick on a drunken tinker, Christopher Sly, persuading him he is a nobleman, and has a company of players perform a comedy for him. The comedy is set in Padua, where rich Baptista will not let his gentle younger daughter Bianca marry until his sharp-tongued elder daughter Katherina has a husband.","Petruchio, from Verona, comes 'to wive it wealthily' and takes on Katherina, while Bianca's suitors, most of them in disguise, compete for her. After a chaotic wedding Petruchio sets out to 'tame' his wife by starving her, keeping her awake and contradicting her, all in the name of love.","Watch how marriage is bargained for in money, how disguise lets people change their rank, and above all how Katherina's voice changes: whether her final speech is surrender, strategy or performance is the question the play leaves open."],"timeline":[{"s":"a0s1","t":"Sly is thrown out of the alehouse and falls asleep","id":9},{"s":"a0s1","t":"The Lord plans to make Sly believe he is a lord","id":21},{"s":"a0s2","t":"Sly accepts that he is a lord with a wife","id":67},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Baptista's rule: no Bianca until Katherina is married","id":108},{"s":"a1s1","t":"Lucentio falls for Bianca and swaps places with Tranio","id":159},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Petruchio comes to Padua to marry money","id":202},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Hortensio offers him Katherina, and Petruchio accepts","id":205},{"s":"a1s2","t":"Tranio, as Lucentio, joins Bianca's suitors","id":258},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Katherina ties up and strikes Bianca","id":282},{"s":"a2s1","t":"The false tutors are presented to Baptista","id":299},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Katherina breaks the lute over Hortensio's head","id":334},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Petruchio and Katherina's battle of wits","id":343},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Petruchio announces the Sunday wedding","id":407},{"s":"a2s1","t":"Tranio outbids Gremio for Bianca","id":426},{"s":"a3s1","t":"Lucentio woos Bianca in a Latin lesson","id":452},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Petruchio arrives late and absurdly dressed","id":511},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Gremio describes the wild wedding","id":539},{"s":"a3s2","t":"Petruchio carries Katherina off before the feast","id":563},{"s":"a4s1","t":"Petruchio throws the supper at the servants","id":649},{"s":"a4s1","t":"His falconry soliloquy: 'kill a wife with kindness'","id":662},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Tranio and Hortensio give up Bianca","id":681},{"s":"a4s2","t":"Tranio persuades the Pedant to pose as Vincentio","id":718},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Grumio torments the starving Katherina","id":730},{"s":"a4s3","t":"Petruchio rejects the cap and gown","id":765},{"s":"a4s4","t":"The false Vincentio agrees the marriage","id":825},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Katherina agrees the sun is the moon","id":866},{"s":"a4s5","t":"Katherina greets Vincentio as a young girl","id":872},{"s":"a5s1","t":"The real Vincentio is nearly sent to prison","id":939},{"s":"a5s1","t":"Lucentio and Bianca return married","id":957},{"s":"a5s2","t":"The wager, and Katherina's speech on a wife's duty","id":1085}],"irony":{"18":"The Lord's trick on Sly is a small version of the whole play: a person is changed by how others treat them.","116":"Lucentio admires Bianca's silence; by the end she is the wife who answers back.","161":"Tranio says Vincentio told him to be 'serviceable' to his son: he will serve him by impersonating him.","217":"Gremio hires Lucentio to woo Bianca on his behalf: he is paying his own rival.","223":"Gremio praises his schoolmaster's 'learning and behaviour', not knowing he is a disguised suitor.","306":"Baptista welcomes Petruchio for his father's sake, unaware of how he will treat his daughter.","388":"Petruchio praises Katherina for gentleness while she is insulting him.","403":"Petruchio says she will be 'a second Grissel' for patience: the audience has just seen her strike him.","424":"Tranio offers houses and ships that belong to a father who does not know he exists.","529":"Lucentio fears his 'fellow schoolmaster' is watching Bianca, not knowing he is Hortensio.","546":"Petruchio thanks the guests for watching him marry 'this most patient, sweet, and virtuous wife'.","681":"Tranio swears never to marry Bianca, which is easy, since he is a servant pretending to woo her for his master.","716":"Tranio tells the Pedant that he looks like Vincentio: the audience will soon see the real one.","879":"Petruchio announces Lucentio's marriage to his father before it has been revealed to anyone, including Baptista.","905":"The Pedant calls the real Vincentio a liar for claiming to be himself.","1029":"Everyone expects Petruchio to lose the wager; the audience has seen the sun and moon scene."},"devices":{"animals":"Hawks, hounds and taming","money":"Money and trade","clothes":"Clothes and disguise","war":"War, storms and fire","music":"Music and harmony","food":"Food and hunger"},"dev":{"12":[["animals","The Lord talks of his hounds with more care than he shows people.","tender well my hounds"]],"18":[["animals","Sly is a 'monstrous beast' and a 'swine' to the Lord.","how like a swine he lies"]],"109":[["animals","Katherina is 'too rough', like an untrained horse.","she's too rough for me"]],"123":[["animals","To 'mew up' is to shut a hawk in a cage.","Why will you mew her up"]],"130":[["food","Gremio's cake is still dough: both suitors have failed.","our cake's dough on both sides"]],"202":[["money","Petruchio links marriage and money in one rhyme.","I come to wive it wealthily in Padua"]],"205":[["war","Wooing as a naval attack: he will 'board' her.","For I will board her"]],"232":[["animals","Gremio calls Katherina a 'wild-cat'.","But will you woo this wild-cat?"]],"235":[["war","Lions, storms, cannon and battle: wooing as war.","Have I not heard great ordnance in the field"]],"325":[["war","Two raging fires: Petruchio will burn out her temper with a greater one.","where two raging fires meet together"]],"334":[["music","Frets and fuming: music turns to violence.","'Frets, call you these?' quoth she 'I'll fume with them'"]],"343":[["food","Kates are 'cates', delicacies: Katherina becomes a dish.","For dainties are all Kates"]],"355":[["animals","Turtle-doves and buzzards: the wooing as hawk and prey.","O, slow-wing'd turtle! shall a buzzard take thee?"]],"357":[["animals","Wasps and stings: Katherina's tongue as a weapon.","Come, come, you wasp"]],"396":[["animals","A 'wild Kate' to be turned into a household cat.","bring you from a wild Kate to a Kate"]],"413":[["money","Baptista as a merchant gambling on a desperate deal.","I play a merchant's part"]],"423":[["money","Gremio's inventory turns Bianca's hand into an auction.","Basins and ewers to lave her dainty hands"]],"445":[["music","Bianca refuses to keep to 'hours' and 'times'.","I'll not be tied to hours nor 'pointed times"]],"523":[["clothes","The wedding clothes test what marriage is really about.","To me she's married, not unto my clothes"]],"563":[["money","Katherina listed with his house, barn, ox and ass.","She is my goods, my chattels"]],"653":[["food","Burnt meat 'engenders choler': food as an excuse for starvation.","For it engenders choler, planteth anger"]],"662":[["animals","The falcon kept hungry until it comes to the lure.","My falcon now is sharp and passing empty"],["animals","Watching (keeping awake) a wild hawk until it obeys.","Another way I have to man my haggard"]],"730":[["food","Grumio's menu of refusals: a cruel game with food.","What say you to a piece of beef and mustard?"]],"765":[["clothes","The gown is mocked as a cannon and an apple tart.","'Tis like a demi-cannon"]],"806":[["clothes","Honour shines through plain clothes like the sun through clouds.","So honour peereth in the meanest habit"]],"868":[["animals","In bowls, the weight (bias) makes the bowl curve: Katherina now runs his way.","thus the bowl should run"]],"985":[["music","Jarring notes now agree: the comedy's harmony.","our jarring notes agree"]],"1032":[["animals","Wives are bet on like hawks and hounds.","I'll venture so much of my hawk or hound"]],"1085":[["war","Women's lances are only straws.","But now I see our lances are but straws"]]},"stage":{"heath":"Outside an alehouse on a heath","chamber":"A bedchamber in the Lord's house","street":"A public place in Padua","house":"A street outside a house in Padua","room":"A room in Baptista's house","country":"Petruchio's country house","road":"A public road to Padua","banquet":"The banqueting room in Lucentio's house"},"why":{"0":"Sets up the play's question of whether identity comes from inside or from how others treat you.","1":"Links Sly's lack of choice to Katherina's later lack of choice.","2":"Shows Sly holding on to who he is, briefly.","3":"Shows that a wife is what makes Sly accept his new role.","4":"Sly's transformation is a model for the 'taming' of Katherina.","5":"Presents wifely obedience as something performed, by a boy, on command.","6":"A comic servant's wisdom that also describes what the audience wants from a comedy.","7":"Katherina is shamed in public before she has said a word.","8":"Her first line shows she understands exactly how she is being used.","9":"Her wit and violence arrive together.","10":"Shows how Katherina looks to a stranger: mad or stubborn.","11":"Bianca is valued for silence, the quality Katherina lacks.","12":"Katherina resists being controlled by timetables and rules.","13":"Shows how the suitors talk of her as a devil and of marriage to her as hell.","14":"Lucentio's love is instant, bookish and exaggerated.","15":"The reversal of master and servant starts the subplot of disguise.","16":"Sly's comic boredom is his last word: the frame is left open.","17":"Petruchio's motives are money first.","18":"The clearest statement that marriage is a financial deal.","19":"Grumio's exaggeration makes Petruchio's greed comic.","20":"Wooing becomes conquest.","21":"Katherina's nickname shows how her reputation traps her.","22":"Petruchio treats Katherina as a danger to be faced, not a person.","23":"Dismisses a woman's speech as harmless noise.","24":"Sets up the contrast between the sisters that the ending reverses.","25":"Bianca's 'duty' may be a pose, which matters at the end.","26":"Reveals the jealousy and hurt behind Katherina's anger.","27":"Shows her fear of being left unmarried.","28":"Petruchio's plan is to be more extreme than Katherina.","29":"Roughness is his method from the start.","30":"His method of contradiction, set out before he uses it.","31":"Renaming her is his first act of control.","32":"Shows the bawdy, competitive energy of the wooing.","33":"Katherina enjoys the game and warns him of her sting.","34":"The duel is fast and evenly matched.","35":"Katherina's consent does not matter to the deal.","36":"Petruchio names his project: to tame her.","37":"The goal is a conforming, domestic wife.","38":"Katherina's last line of open refusal before her silence.","39":"Petruchio invents a story and makes her defiance part of it.","40":"Marriage is announced, not agreed.","41":"Baptista treats the marriage as a business gamble.","42":"Bianca's marriage is decided by an auction.","43":"Tranio's bluff shows how money can be faked.","44":"Bianca has a will of her own.","45":"Bianca is witty and in control in her courtship.","46":"Katherina fears public shame.","47":"She now depends on the marriage she resisted.","48":"A lesson about appearances, delivered as a joke.","49":"Katherina's last open defiance in public.","50":"She defends a woman's right to resist.","51":"Marriage gives Petruchio legal mastery.","52":"The wife as property, in a list of possessions.","53":"Onlookers think Petruchio has caught her madness.","54":"A comic echo of the taming plot.","55":"Katherina's first gentle words, defending a servant.","56":"Petruchio's excuse turns starvation into care.","57":"A servant sums up the method.","58":"Marriage as a kingdom ruled with cunning.","59":"The taming as falconry.","60":"Cruelty disguised as kindness.","61":"Petruchio involves the audience in his method.","62":"His methods are now famous enough to be taught.","63":"A servant invents a father, showing how roles can be faked.","64":"Katherina's suffering is real.","65":"She sees through his excuses.","66":"He controls even her status and appearance.","67":"Her strongest claim to speak her mind.","68":"An argument that worth lies within.","69":"Power over time itself.","70":"Sets up the sun and moon test.","71":"Even when she gives way, her wit survives.","72":"She gives him the power to name the world.","73":"The taming as a military victory.","74":"She joins the game with relish.","75":"The disguises are unravelled.","76":"Love excuses the deception.","77":"A moment of tenderness between them.","78":"Wives are bet on like animals.","79":"Bianca refuses her husband, reversing expectations.","80":"Petruchio's ideal of marriage: order under him.","81":"Even Katherina's change is rewarded in money.","82":"Bianca now has the sharp tongue.","83":"The heart of Katherina's final speech.","84":"Marriage compared to the state.","85":"She admits her former pride and intelligence.","86":"The final gesture of submission.","87":"'Kiss me, Kate' returns as a reward.","88":"The last line leaves room for doubt."},"arc":{"Katherina's defiance":[0,0,7,6,10,5,9,4,3,7,2,2,1,1],"Katherina's compliance":[0,0,0,0,2,1,3,5,4,4,4,8,8,10]},"arcnote":"Katherina does not appear in the Induction, so the lines start at nothing. Her defiance is high from her first entrance and peaks in the wooing scene, where she matches Petruchio and strikes him. It flares again when she refuses to leave her wedding feast and when she defends her right to speak in the cap and gown scene. Compliance starts to rise at the country house, where she pleads for a servant and thanks Petruchio for food, jumps on the road to Padua when she agrees the sun is the moon, and peaks in the final speech. Scenes without her (3.1, 4.2, 4.4) are rated from what others say and expect of her.","context":[["Date","Probably written in the early 1590s, among Shakespeare's first comedies. It was first printed in the First Folio of 1623."],["The Taming of a Shrew","An anonymous play called The Taming of a Shrew was printed in 1594. It tells the same story with different names and keeps Sly on stage throughout, ending with him waking and going home to tame his own wife. Scholars still argue whether it was a source, a later copy or a version of Shakespeare's play."],["Sources","The Bianca plot comes from George Gascoigne's comedy Supposes (1566), a translation of Ariosto's I Suppositi. Lucentio nods to it in 5.1 with 'counterfeit supposes'. The trick played on Sly is an old story, told in The Arabian Nights among other places."],["Scolds and shrews","In Elizabethan England a 'scold' was a woman who quarrelled in public, and could be punished in the ducking stool or by being paraded in a cart. Ballads and pamphlets about shrewish wives being tamed were popular, some of them very violent."],["Marriage and property","Under English law a married woman's property belonged to her husband. Petruchio's 'She is my goods, my chattels' echoes the Tenth Commandment, which lists a wife among a man's possessions. Church sermons such as the Homily on Marriage told wives to obey their husbands, while asking husbands to be gentle."],["Dowries and jointures","Upper-class marriages were arranged around money. A father paid a dowry to the husband; the husband promised a jointure, land or income for his wife if he died first. Baptista's auction of Bianca turns this system into comedy."],["Padua and Italy","Padua had a famous university, which is why Lucentio comes to study there. Italian settings and phrases ('ben venuto', 'basta') gave Elizabethan comedies an exotic, fashionable flavour."],["Falconry","Hawking was a sport of the rich. A wild hawk (a 'haggard') was tamed by keeping it hungry and awake until it would come to the lure. Petruchio's soliloquy uses these exact methods."],["Boy actors","All women's roles were played by boys or young men. The page who plays Sly's wife reminds the audience that Katherina and Bianca are also performances by boy actors."],["The missing ending","Sly vanishes after 1.1. Some productions borrow the ending from The Taming of a Shrew so that Sly wakes up at the end; others drop the Induction entirely."],["Later versions","The play has been rewritten many times: as David Garrick's short Catharine and Petruchio (1754), as the musical Kiss Me, Kate (1948), and as the teen film 10 Things I Hate About You (1999)."],["A response","John Fletcher wrote a sequel, The Woman's Prize, or The Tamer Tamed (around 1611), in which Petruchio, now a widower, marries again and is tamed by his new wife."]],"critics":{"taming":[["George Bernard Shaw, letter to the Pall Mall Gazette (1888)","Paraphrased: Shaw called the last scene disgusting to modern ears and said no decent man could sit through it in the company of a woman without feeling ashamed."]],"voice":[["Coppélia Kahn, Man's Estate (1981)","Paraphrased: Kahn argued that Katherina's final speech is so exaggerated that it satirises the male fantasy of total female obedience, and that she wins her own kind of freedom by performing it."],["Germaine Greer, The Female Eunuch (1970)","Paraphrased: Greer saw Katherina and Petruchio as a couple well matched in energy, and preferred Katherina's honest relationship to Bianca's, which she found manipulative."]],"theatre":[["Harold Bloom, Shakespeare: The Invention of the Human (1998)","Paraphrased: Bloom read Katherina's last speech as ironic, a shared joke between two people who have found each other, more like a game than a surrender."]],"marriage":[["Lynda Boose, 'Scolding Brides and Bridling Scolds' (1991)","Paraphrased: Boose set the play beside real Elizabethan punishments for scolds, such as the scold's bridle, and argued that its comedy rests on a culture of violence towards outspoken women."]],"class":[["Leah Marcus, 'The Shakespearean Editor as Shrew-Tamer' (1992)","Paraphrased: Marcus argued that editors chose the Folio text over A Shrew partly because its ending seemed more settled, and that the alternative play's frame makes the taming more clearly a fantasy."]]},"insults":[{"id":"4","f":"Y'are a baggage","who":"Sly","n":"Sly insults the Hostess as a slut: his first words show his rank and his temper."},{"id":"18","f":"O monstrous beast! how like a swine he lies!","who":"Lord","n":"The Lord sees the poor drunk as an animal, then decides to play with him."},{"id":"109","f":"To cart her rather","who":"Gremio","n":"A public punishment for scolds: Katherina is insulted before she speaks."},{"id":"123","f":"this fiend of hell","who":"Gremio","n":"The suitors call Katherina a devil to her father's face."},{"id":"130","f":"You may go to the devil's dam","who":"Gremio","n":"The devil's mother: the worst they can think of."},{"id":"278","f":"Minion, thou liest","who":"Katherina","n":"Katherina uses 'thou' and 'minion' (hussy) to her sister."},{"id":"285","f":"thou hilding of a devilish spirit","who":"Baptista","n":"Her own father calls her a worthless creature."},{"id":"334","f":"rascal fiddler","who":"Katherina (reported)","n":"Katherina's insults to Hortensio, reported as he stands with a broken lute round his neck."},{"id":"344","f":"You were a moveable","who":"Katherina","n":"She calls Petruchio a piece of furniture."},{"id":"348","f":"Asses are made to bear, and so are you","who":"Katherina","n":"She calls him a donkey."},{"id":"373","f":"you crow too like a craven","who":"Katherina","n":"A coward, a cock that won't fight."},{"id":"389","f":"Go, fool, and whom thou keep'st command","who":"Katherina","n":"Order your servants, not me."},{"id":"402","f":"A mad-cap ruffian and a swearing Jack","who":"Katherina","n":"Katherina's view of Petruchio to her father."},{"id":"419","f":"Greybeard, thy love doth freeze","who":"Tranio","n":"Age against youth in the bidding war."},{"id":"629","f":"You logger-headed and unpolish'd grooms!","who":"Petruchio","n":"Petruchio abuses his servants in front of Katherina, showing her what rage looks like."},{"id":"631","f":"you whoreson malt-horse drudge","who":"Petruchio","n":"He calls Grumio a brewer's cart-horse."},{"id":"737","f":"thou false deluding slave","who":"Katherina","n":"Katherina beats Grumio for teasing her with food."},{"id":"772","f":"Thou flea, thou nit, thou winter-cricket thou!","who":"Petruchio","n":"He shrinks the tailor to an insect."},{"id":"948","f":"Away with the dotard!","who":"Baptista","n":"The real Vincentio is called an old fool."},{"id":"1085","f":"you froward and unable worms","who":"Katherina","n":"Katherina turns insult on the other wives."}],"pronouns":[{"id":"276","f":"Of all thy suitors here I charge thee tell","n":"Katherina uses 'thee' to her younger sister: the elder's right, used as a command."},{"id":"341","f":"Good morrow, Kate; for that's your name, I hear.","n":"Petruchio uses 'you' at first, then shifts to intimate 'thou' within lines."},{"id":"342","f":"Well have you heard, but something hard of hearing","n":"Katherina answers with cold, formal 'you'."},{"id":"388","f":"For thou art pleasant, gamesome, passing courteous","n":"Petruchio's 'thou' is intimate, mocking and familiar at once."},{"id":"396","f":"your father hath consented","n":"He switches to 'you' to state the business terms."},{"id":"558","f":"Do what thou canst, I will not go today","n":"Katherina uses 'thou' to her new husband in defiance."},{"id":"980","f":"Nay, I will give thee a kiss: now pray thee, love, stay.","n":"Now 'thee' and 'love' are affectionate."}],"predict":{"a0s1":["Sly is arrested for not paying","Sly wakes believing he is a lord"],"a0s2":["Sly sees through the trick","Sly settles down to watch a play"],"a1s1":["Petruchio hears of Katherina","Katherina falls for Lucentio"],"a1s2":["Petruchio meets Katherina and loves her at once","Petruchio and Katherina fight with words"],"a2s1":["Bianca starts to like her real tutor","Bianca chooses Hortensio"],"a3s1":["Petruchio is late for the wedding","Katherina runs away before the wedding"],"a3s2":["Petruchio takes Katherina to his house and starves her","Petruchio and Katherina live happily in Padua"],"a4s1":["Hortensio gives up Bianca","Gremio wins Bianca"],"a4s2":["Katherina is given a fine new gown","Katherina is refused food and clothes"],"a4s3":["The false Vincentio meets Baptista","Baptista discovers the trick"],"a4s4":["Katherina and Petruchio meet Lucentio's real father","Katherina refuses to travel with Petruchio"],"a4s5":["The real Vincentio meets his false self","Vincentio goes home without seeing his son"],"a5s1":["Katherina is the only wife who comes when called","Bianca is the most obedient wife"]},"contrast":[{"t":"Katherina and Bianca","pts":["Katherina speaks her mind and is called a shrew; Bianca is admired for silence.","Bianca shows a sharp will in her lessons, and refuses her husband at the end.","The ending reverses their reputations: the 'shrew' obeys and the 'mild' sister answers back."],"ids":[290,1079]},{"t":"Petruchio and Lucentio","pts":["Petruchio woos for money and openly; Lucentio woos for love and in disguise.","Petruchio speaks plainly and roughly; Lucentio in classical allusions and poetry.","Petruchio wins the wager; Lucentio loses it."],"ids":[202,143]},{"t":"Sly and Katherina","pts":["Both are changed by the way they are treated: Sly into a lord, Katherina into a 'tame' wife.","Both are offered food, clothes and comfort, or denied them, by a powerful man.","Both accept a new identity in the end, but we may wonder how real it is."],"ids":[67,866]},{"t":"Tranio and Grumio","pts":["Both are witty servants who help their masters.","Tranio plays his master and outwits the old men of Padua; Grumio clowns and suffers his master's blows.","Tranio's success shows rank can be faked; Grumio's beatings show it cannot be escaped."],"ids":[437,186]},{"t":"Baptista and Vincentio","pts":["Both are rich fathers anxious about their children's marriages and money.","Baptista sells his daughters to the highest bidder; Vincentio crosses Italy to visit his son.","Both are fooled by the false father and forgive the deception."],"ids":[422,936]}],"keyfacts":[["Full title","The Taming of the Shrew"],["Written","Early 1590s; first printed in the First Folio of 1623"],["Genre","Comedy, framed by an Induction"],["Setting","An alehouse and a lord's house in the English countryside (the Induction); Padua and Petruchio's country house near Verona (the play)"],["Protagonists","Katherina and Petruchio"],["Antagonists","Katherina's temper and reputation; Petruchio's will; in the subplot, Baptista's rule and the rival suitors"],["Inciting incident","Baptista's rule that Bianca cannot marry until Katherina does"],["Turning point","The sun and moon scene on the road to Padua (4.5), where Katherina agrees with whatever Petruchio says"],["Climax","The wager at Lucentio's feast and Katherina's speech on a wife's duty (5.2)"],["Point of view","A play within a play, performed for Christopher Sly, who disappears after 1.1"],["Major conflicts","Katherina against Petruchio; Bianca's suitors against each other; masters against servants in disguise"],["Themes","Taming and power, marriage and money, women's voices, disguise and identity"]],"analysis":{"KATHERINA":["Katherina is defined by others before she speaks: 'curst', 'a devil', 'too rough'. Her first words protest that her father is using her as bait for Bianca's suitors, and her jealousy of Bianca suggests a woman who feels unloved and fears being left unmarried.","She is the wittiest character in Padua, matching Petruchio pun for pun in 2.1. But her wit is tied to violence: she ties up her sister, breaks a lute on Hortensio's head and strikes Petruchio.","At the country house she is starved, kept awake and denied the cap and gown, and still insists on her right to speak. On the road she gives way, but with wit, outdoing Petruchio in praise of the 'young budding virgin'.","Her final speech is the play's great problem. It can be played as sincere conversion, as a weary surrender, as irony, or as a performance she and Petruchio share. Notice that it is the longest speech in the play: she has found a way to be heard."],"PETRUCHIO":["Petruchio comes to Padua openly to marry money, and takes on Katherina as a challenge as much as a wife. He is loud, quick-witted and fearless, and he matches her energy.","His method is contradiction: he praises her for the opposite of whatever she does, renames her 'Kate', and announces their wedding against her protests.","After the wedding he turns her own behaviour back on her: he rages at servants, throws food and denies her sleep, all 'in reverend care of her'. His falconry soliloquy shows that this is a deliberate plan.","Some see him as a bully; others as the only man who sees Katherina's worth and plays a game she comes to share. He never strikes her, but he controls her food, sleep, clothes and words."],"BIANCA":["Bianca is first seen as everything Katherina is not: silent, obedient and admired. Lucentio falls for her 'mild behaviour and sobriety'.","In private she is quick and witty: she sends her tutors about their business, decodes Lucentio's Latin and rejects Hortensio's gamut. She knows how to perform modesty.","She marries Lucentio in secret, without her father's consent, and kneels for pardon only when caught.","At the feast she trades bawdy jokes, refuses to come when called and calls her husband a fool. The play ends with the 'mild' sister revealed as the less obedient wife."],"LUCENTIO":["Lucentio arrives to study philosophy and abandons it at first sight of Bianca. His love speaks the language of poetry and myth.","He spends most of the play disguised as Cambio, a schoolmaster, while his servant plays him. His wooing through a mock Latin lesson is clever and romantic.","He marries Bianca secretly, with Biondello's help, and is saved from his father's anger by confessing that 'Love wrought these miracles'.","He is Petruchio's opposite: romantic where Petruchio is practical. At the end he loses the wager, and his romance looks less secure than Petruchio's bargain."],"TRANIO":["Tranio is Lucentio's servant and the cleverest schemer in Padua. His first advice, to enjoy study and pleasure together, sets the tone of comedy.","Dressed as Lucentio he bluffs Gremio with invented ships and lands, scares the Pedant into playing his father, and removes Hortensio as a rival.","His disguise lets him live as a gentleman, and he enjoys it, defending his 'pearl and gold' against the real Vincentio.","He is unmasked and runs away, but returns as a guest at the feast, joking with Petruchio. The servant who played a master is a mirror of Sly the beggar who played a lord."],"GRUMIO":["Grumio is Petruchio's old servant and the play's clown, whose jokes depend on twisting words: 'knock me here'.","He describes his master's greed, warns that Petruchio will out-scold Katherina, and tells the story of the muddy journey.","At the country house he joins in the taming by tormenting Katherina with food and refusing it.","His quarrel with the tailor turns the scene of the gown into farce. Like the other servants, he shows the household Petruchio rules."],"SLY":["Christopher Sly is a drunken tinker from near Stratford, firmly English among the Italians of the play he watches.","Persuaded by the Lord's servants that he is a lord, he accepts his new identity only when offered a beautiful wife, and switches from prose to verse.","He is an impatient audience: 'would 'twere done!' After 1.1 he is never heard again, and the frame is left open.","He mirrors Katherina: someone whose identity is changed by the way powerful people treat them. The comparison invites us to ask whether her 'taming' is as much of a trick as his lordship."]},"motifs":[["Taming and animals","Katherina is a wildcat, a wasp, a falcon, a haggard to be manned. Petruchio treats marriage like training an animal, and the wives are bet on like hawks and hounds.",[22,36,37,59,60,78]],["Money and bargaining","Dowries, jointures, auctions and wagers: almost every marriage in the play is a financial deal.",[17,18,41,42,43,81]],["Clothes and disguise","Sly in a lord's clothes, Tranio in Lucentio's, Petruchio's absurd wedding outfit and the cap and gown: clothes change how people are treated, but not who they are.",[4,15,48,66,68,75]],["Speech and silence","Who talks, who is talked about, and who is made silent. Katherina is defined by her tongue, Bianca by her silence, until the end reverses them.",[8,11,12,38,67,85]],["Food and sleep","The Lord offers Sly a banquet; Petruchio denies Katherina food and sleep. Comfort is a tool of power.",[56,57,59,64,65,60]],["The sun and the moon","Petruchio's claim to command time and nature becomes the test of Katherina's obedience on the road.",[69,70,71,72,73,74]]],"symbols2":[["The falcon","Petruchio's image of Katherina as a hawk to be trained by hunger and lack of sleep."],["The cap and gown","Fashionable clothes Petruchio denies Katherina, and the cap she later throws under her foot on his order."],["Petruchio's wedding clothes","A mocking costume that humiliates Katherina and tests whether marriage is about appearances."],["The lute","Broken over Hortensio's head: music, the art of harmony, turned into a weapon."],["The sun and the moon","The test of whether Katherina will accept her husband's word over her own eyes."],["The wager","The final bet, a hundred crowns on whose wife obeys: love measured in money."]],"bigq":[["Is Katherina really tamed at the end?",["Her speech is long, fluent and sincere in tone","It can be played as irony or a shared joke","She has found a way to be heard and to win","The final line, and Sly's absence, leave the ending uncertain"],[83,85,86,88]],["Is Petruchio a bully or a match for Katherina?",["He starves her and controls her clothes and sleep","He matches her wit and energy, as no one else does","He never strikes her, and shows her her own temper","His motives begin with money"],[18,36,57,60]],["Why does Shakespeare frame the play with Christopher Sly?",["It makes the taming a play within a play, a performance","Sly is changed by others, like Katherina","His disappearance leaves the frame open","It reminds us that a boy plays the wife"],[0,1,4,5]],["Who is the real 'shrew' at the end?",["Bianca refuses to come and calls her husband a fool","The Widow mocks Katherina and Hortensio","Katherina scolds the other wives at length","Petruchio out-shrews everyone"],[79,82,84,57]],["Is marriage in the play about love or money?",["Petruchio comes to wive wealthily","Bianca is sold to the highest bidder","Lucentio loves at first sight","The final wager makes obedience a bet"],[18,42,14,78]],["What does disguise show about identity?",["Tranio is accepted as a gentleman because of his clothes","Sly becomes a lord when treated as one","Petruchio says it is the mind, not clothes, that makes us rich","The real father exposes every disguise"],[15,4,68,75]]],"symbols":{"Tame":"\\btam(e|ed|ing|er)\\b","Shrew":"\\bshrews?\\b|\\bcurst\\b","Kate":"\\bKates?\\b","Wife":"\\bwife\\b|\\bwives\\b","Money":"\\bcrowns?\\b|\\bducats?\\b|\\bgold\\b|\\bdowry\\b|\\bwealth","Clothes":"\\bcap\\b|\\bgown\\b|\\bapparel\\b|\\bclothes\\b|\\bhabit\\b"},"stagerules":[["alehouse|heath","heath"],["lord's house|bedchamber","chamber"],["petruchio","country"],["road","road"],["lucentio's house","banquet"],["room","room"],["before","house"],["public|street|place","street"]],"solo":{"45":1,"339":1,"437":1,"477":1,"577":1,"662":1,"852":1,"884":1},"iago":[["a1s2","Petruchio hears of rich, shrewish Katherina and decides to woo her for her dowry."],["a2s1","He plans to contradict everything she says by praising its opposite."],["a2s1","He renames her 'Kate' and matches her in a battle of wit."],["a2s1","He tells her he will marry her 'will you, nill you'."],["a2s1","He announces a Sunday wedding and invents a loving agreement."],["a3s2","He arrives late, in absurd clothes, to shame her publicly."],["a3s2","He swears and strikes the priest during the ceremony."],["a3s2","He carries her off before the feast, calling her his property."],["a4s1","He beats servants and rages, showing her her own temper."],["a4s1","He throws away the supper and keeps her from sleep."],["a4s1","He explains his falconry method: hunger and watching."],["a4s3","Grumio torments her with food; Petruchio makes her thank him."],["a4s3","He refuses the cap and gown she wants."],["a4s3","He insists it is whatever time he says it is."],["a4s5","She agrees that the sun is the moon."],["a5s1","She kisses him in the street when he asks."],["a5s2","She comes when called, throws down her cap and lectures the wives."]],"rel":{"0":[["LORD","SLY","deceive"],["PAGE","SLY","deceive"],["SLY","PAGE","love"],["HOSTESS","SLY","hate"]],"1":[["LUCENTIO","BIANCA","love"],["GREMIO","BIANCA","love"],["HORTENSIO","BIANCA","love"],["KATHERINA","BIANCA","hate"],["TRANIO","LUCENTIO","trust"],["GREMIO","KATHERINA","hate"],["PETRUCHIO","HORTENSIO","trust"]],"2":[["PETRUCHIO","KATHERINA","love"],["KATHERINA","PETRUCHIO","hate"],["KATHERINA","BIANCA","hate"],["TRANIO","BAPTISTA","deceive"],["HORTENSIO","BAPTISTA","deceive"],["LUCENTIO","GREMIO","deceive"],["BAPTISTA","BIANCA","love"]],"3":[["LUCENTIO","BIANCA","love"],["BIANCA","LUCENTIO","love"],["HORTENSIO","LUCENTIO","hate"],["PETRUCHIO","KATHERINA","love"],["KATHERINA","PETRUCHIO","hate"],["TRANIO","GREMIO","deceive"]],"4":[["PETRUCHIO","KATHERINA","deceive"],["KATHERINA","PETRUCHIO","trust"],["GRUMIO","KATHERINA","deceive"],["TRANIO","HORTENSIO","deceive"],["TRANIO","PEDANT","deceive"],["PEDANT","BAPTISTA","deceive"],["LUCENTIO","BIANCA","love"]],"5":[["PETRUCHIO","KATHERINA","love"],["KATHERINA","PETRUCHIO","love"],["LUCENTIO","VINCENTIO","trust"],["TRANIO","VINCENTIO","hate"],["BIANCA","LUCENTIO","hate"],["WIDOW","KATHERINA","hate"]]},"examq":[["How does Shakespeare present Katherina?",[8,12,26,67,71,83]],["How does Shakespeare present Petruchio's methods of taming?",[30,36,56,59,60,69]],["How does Shakespeare present marriage and money?",[17,18,41,42,78,81]],["How does Shakespeare present disguise and identity?",[0,4,15,48,68,75]],["How does Shakespeare present the ending of the play?",[79,82,83,86,87,88]],["How does Shakespeare present the relationship between Katherina and Bianca?",[11,24,25,26,44,82]]],"check":["Answered the exact question in the first sentence","Made a clear argument (a thesis) and kept to it","Used short quotations, built into my own sentences","Explained what the quotation suggests, not just what it says","Zoomed in on a single word or image","Named a method (stichomythia, pun, imagery, soliloquy, irony, structure) and its effect","Linked to context where it helps the argument","Considered more than one interpretation, especially of the ending","Referred to the whole play, including the Induction","Ended with a conclusion that answers the question"],"place":{"a0s1":"England","a0s2":"England","a1s1":"Padua","a1s2":"Padua","a2s1":"Padua","a3s1":"Padua","a3s2":"Padua","a4s1":"Petruchio's house","a4s2":"Padua","a4s3":"Petruchio's house","a4s4":"Padua","a4s5":"On the road","a5s1":"Padua","a5s2":"Padua"},"models":[[36,"Shakespeare presents Petruchio's taming as a deliberate project, announced to Katherina before it begins.","'Born to tame you' makes his role sound like destiny, and 'tame' places her with animals to be trained.","The audience may laugh at his confidence, but the speech also removes her choice: she is told what she will become.","Elizabethan readers knew ballads in which shrews were tamed by force; Petruchio's method is psychological, not physical.","A more positive reading sees him recognising a match in her energy, and offering her a contest."],[59,"Shakespeare presents the taming through the language of falconry, which makes Katherina an animal and Petruchio a trainer.","'Sharp and passing empty' describes a hawk kept hungry so it will obey: her starvation is a technique.","The soliloquy invites the audience to share his plan, so we become complicit, or critical.","Falconry was an aristocratic sport, and the image links marriage to ownership and control.","Some productions play this speech for laughs; others make the audience uneasy about his cruelty."],[67,"Shakespeare gives Katherina a powerful defence of free speech even in the middle of the taming.","The image of a heart breaking from concealed anger shows that silence is not peace but pain.","The audience is invited to sympathise with her, since we have seen her starved and mocked.","In a culture that punished scolds, a woman's right to speak was a live question.","Her speech is overruled at once, which shows how little her words count in Petruchio's house."],[71,"Shakespeare presents Katherina's surrender as witty rather than broken.","'The moon changes even as your mind' gives way to him while calling him changeable, like the moon.","The audience can enjoy her intelligence, which suggests she is choosing to play his game.","Hortensio's 'the field is won' frames it as a battle, but her humour complicates that victory.","A darker reading sees exhausted compliance disguised as humour."],[83,"Shakespeare presents Katherina's final speech as an extreme statement of wifely duty, open to different readings.","'Thy husband is thy lord, thy life, thy keeper' piles up titles until the husband becomes a king and a god.","The speech is her longest and most fluent: the woman once silenced now commands the stage.","It echoes Elizabethan sermons on marriage, which audiences would recognise.","Directors stage it as sincere, ironic or as a private joke between husband and wife, and each changes the play's meaning."],[79,"Shakespeare reverses the sisters' reputations at the end to question what a good wife is.","Bianca's 'busy' reply is the same refusal Katherina once gave, now coming from the 'mild' sister.","The audience, expecting Katherina to fail, is surprised: Petruchio's confidence is rewarded.","In a culture of arranged marriages, Lucentio's love match ends with the least obedience.","This could mean Petruchio's method works, or that all the marriages are performances."]],"lang":{"un":[["202","I come to wive it wealthily in Padua;","In Padua I come to marry wealthily."],["396","For I am he am born to tame you, Kate,","For I am the one born to tame you, Kate."],["761","My tongue will tell the anger of my heart,","My tongue will speak my heart's anger."],["1085","Such duty as the subject owes the prince, Even such a woman oweth to her husband;","A woman owes her husband the same duty that a subject owes the prince."]],"pun":[["343","For dainties are all Kates, and therefore, Kate,","Kates","pun","Kates sounds like cates, delicacies: Katherina becomes something to eat."],["349","Women are made to bear, and so are you.","bear","pun","To bear a load, to bear a man in bed, and to bear children."],["334","'Frets, call you these?' quoth she 'I'll fume with them';","Frets","pun","Frets are a lute's finger-bars; to fret is to be annoyed."],["1090","though you hit the white;","white","pun","The white centre of a target, and Bianca, whose name means white."],["461","The base is right; 'tis the base knave that jars.","base","pun","The bass string, and a base (low) villain."]],"st":[["410","And kiss me, Kate; we will be married o' Sunday.",[["kiss","A command, not a request: he takes what he wants."],["Kate","His name for her, not her own."],["Sunday","The date is fixed: there is no room to refuse."]]],["808","It shall be what o'clock I say it is.",[["shall","Future certainty: his will becomes law."],["I","He makes himself the measure of everything."],["say","Words, not facts, decide what is true."]]],["866","And the moon changes even as your mind.",[["moon","The thing he insists on, now turned into a joke."],["changes","She hints that he is the changeable one."],["your","She places the inconsistency in him, not in herself."]]]]},"dir":[["343","You lie, in faith, for you are call'd plain Kate,","How should Petruchio greet Katherina?",[["Loudly and cheerfully, as if he has known her for years","He disarms her with confidence: she has never met anyone like him."],["Quietly, almost tenderly","He sees something in her that others miss."],["Mockingly, playing to an imagined audience","He is performing the taming already, and she is the butt of the joke."]]],["404","I'll see thee hang'd on Sunday first.","How should Katherina say this, her last line before she falls silent?",[["Furiously, then storm out","She is still fighting, and her silence is contempt."],["Firmly, then pause, uncertain","She is tempted by the escape from her father's house."],["Laughing","She has begun to enjoy the game."]]],["563","She is my goods, my chattels; she is my house,","How should Petruchio play this to the wedding guests?",[["As a comic show, with a wink to the audience","It is a performance to shock Padua, and Katherina half sees it."],["Seriously, gripping her arm","He means every word: she is his property."],["Mock-heroic, sword drawn against imaginary thieves","He turns the whole thing into absurd theatre."]]],["662","My falcon now is sharp and passing empty.","How should Petruchio deliver the falconry soliloquy?",[["Brisk and practical, like a trainer at work","His method is cool and deliberate, which can make it chilling."],["Confiding in the audience, inviting them to laugh","The audience becomes his accomplice."],["Tired and uncertain","He is suffering too, and unsure it will work."]]],["866","Then, God be bless'd, it is the blessed sun;","How should Katherina give way on the road?",[["Wearily, worn down","She has been broken by hunger and lack of sleep."],["With a sly smile","She has understood the game and will play it better than he does."],["Sarcastically, for Hortensio's benefit","She concedes, but makes it clear what she thinks."]]],["1085","Thy husband is thy lord, thy life, thy keeper,","How should Katherina deliver her final speech?",[["Sincerely, as a convert","The taming has worked, and she believes what she says."],["Ironically, with a look at Petruchio","It is a shared joke against the other couples."],["Mechanically, like a prisoner","She has been broken, and the comedy turns dark."]]]],"ev":{"ctx":[["27","396"],["19","396","1092"],["437","957","962"],["109","112","206"],["563","202","1085"],["290","396","413"],["102","194","206"],["662","656","570"],["83","19"],["67","177"],["1086","410","1085"],["562","584","570"]],"cr":{"taming":[["1085","662","653"]],"voice":[["1085","1086"],["358","382","1085"]],"theatre":[["866","872","1085"]],"marriage":[["109","563","1085"]],"class":[["1085","1092","656"]]}}};
